// The mock's question bank, copied from the HS&E Test Practice app's own questions
// (generated by make-bank.py; do not edit by hand). Every source is on hse.gov.uk or legislation.gov.uk.
window.BANK=[{"id":"high-risk-b1-01","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Your job would keep you up a stepladder for about 2 hours without coming down. What does HSE suggest?","options":["Use other equipment, such as a tower scaffold or a mobile platform","Use the stepladder, but come down for a break every hour","Use the stepladder, as long as someone holds the bottom","Use a taller stepladder so you do not have to stretch"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE's guide is that if you would stay up a ladder or stepladder for more than 30 minutes at a time, you should use other equipment. Coming down once an hour still means an hour at a time up the steps. Someone holding the bottom or a taller stepladder does not change how long you are up there.","why_not":{"1":"Coming down each hour still means an hour up the steps; HSE's guide is 30 minutes.","2":"Someone holding the bottom does not shorten your time up there; use other equipment.","3":"A taller stepladder still keeps you up for 2 hours; HSE suggests other equipment after 30 minutes."},"source_title":"Safe use of ladders and stepladders: How and when to use a ladder at work (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/work-at-height/ladders/when-how-to-use-ladders-safely.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-02","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are working from a leaning ladder and need to reach a bit further to one side. What does HSE say?","options":["Keep your belt buckle between the two sides of the ladder. If you cannot reach, climb down and move the ladder","Lean out, as long as you keep one hand on the ladder","Shuffle the ladder sideways while you stay on it","Lean out, as long as someone is footing the ladder"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Leaning out pulls the ladder sideways and it can slide or tip. HSE's simple test: your belt buckle (or belly button) should stay between the two long sides of the ladder. Never try to move a ladder while you are standing on it. Climb down, move it, then go back up.","why_not":{"1":"Leaning out can make the ladder slide or tip, even holding on; keep your belt buckle inside the sides.","2":"HSE says never move a ladder while you are standing on it; climb down first.","3":"Footing does not make overreaching safe; keep your belt buckle between the sides of the ladder."},"source_title":"Safe use of ladders and stepladders: Types of ladder and using them safely (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/work-at-height/ladders/types-of-ladder.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-03","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You need to make a leaning ladder secure. Which way should you use only if none of the others is possible?","options":["Tying both sides of the ladder to a strong point","Fitting a ladder stability device","Wedging the ladder firmly, for example against a wall","Having a workmate foot the ladder (hold it at the bottom)"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE gives an order to follow. First, tie the ladder, with both sides tied. If you cannot, use a stability device. If you cannot do that, wedge it firmly. Having someone foot the ladder comes last, only when none of the others can be done.","why_not":{"0":"Tying both sides is HSE's first choice, not the last one.","1":"A stability device is the second choice, used when you cannot tie the ladder.","2":"Wedging comes before footing; footing is the last resort."},"source_title":"Safe use of ladders and stepladders: Securing ladders and ladders used for access (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/work-at-height/ladders/securing-ladders-used-for-access.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-04","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You need to push a mobile tower scaffold along to the next part of the building. What must happen first?","options":["Everyone gets off it, and all tools and materials are taken off","The person on top holds on tight while it is moved","Nothing, if it is only moving a short way","Someone on the top platform pulls it along from above"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says never move a tower while people or materials are on it. A person or load up top can fall, or make the tower tip over. Move it by pushing or pulling at the base only, and only on firm, level ground with nothing overhead.","why_not":{"1":"Never move a tower with anyone on it; they can fall, or the tower can tip.","2":"Even a short move is unsafe with people or materials on the tower.","3":"Move a tower from the base only, and never with people on it."},"source_title":"Construction: Tower scaffolds (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/scaffold.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-09","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Why should someone on the ground know how to use a MEWP's ground controls?","options":["So they can lower the platform and rescue the operator in an emergency","So they can raise the platform faster than the operator can","So the operator in the basket does not need any training","So they can drive the MEWP around site while the operator works"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An operator can get trapped in the basket, for example against steelwork above, and may not be able to stop the machine. HSE says to have a rescue plan and practise it, and someone on the ground should know what to do and how to use the ground controls. Ground controls are for emergencies, not a reason to skip operator training.","why_not":{"1":"Ground controls are for emergency rescue, not for raising the platform faster.","2":"The operator still needs training; ground controls are for rescue in an emergency.","3":"Ground controls are not for driving around; they let someone lower the platform in an emergency."},"source_title":"Construction: Mobile elevating work platforms (MEWPs) (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/mewp.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-10","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Your energy-absorbing lanyard stopped you falling yesterday. Today it looks fine. What should happen to it?","options":["Take it out of use for good: it must never be used again","Keep using it, as long as it passes your pre-use check","Use it for one more week, then get a new one","Wash it, let it dry, then use it again"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Even if it looks fine, HSE says a lanyard that has stopped a fall should never be used again. Take it out of use straight away, so it can be destroyed or sent back to the maker. A pre-use check cannot make it safe to use again.","why_not":{"1":"A pre-use check cannot make it safe; a lanyard that stopped a fall must never be used again.","2":"Not even for a week; it must be taken out of use straight away.","3":"Washing will not make it safe; a lanyard that stopped a fall must never be reused."},"source_title":"Inspecting fall arrest equipment made from webbing or rope (HSE, INDG367)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg367.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-11","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are sent up onto an old factory roof made of sheets. Nobody has told you whether it is fragile. How should you treat it?","options":["As fragile, until a competent person has confirmed that it is not","As safe, if the job only takes a few minutes","As safe, if it held someone's weight last time","As safe, if you only step where you can see the bolts"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says every roof should be treated as fragile until a competent person has confirmed it is not. Falls through fragile roofs and rooflights kill many workers, and short jobs are no safer: precautions are needed however short the work is. A sheet that held once can still break the next time.","why_not":{"1":"Short jobs are no safer; treat every roof as fragile until a competent person says otherwise.","2":"A sheet that held once can still break; treat the roof as fragile until it is checked.","3":"Seeing bolts does not prove a roof is strong; treat it as fragile until a competent person checks."},"source_title":"Construction: Fragile surfaces (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/fragile.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-12","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A trench with supports was inspected on Friday afternoon. It is now Monday morning and you are about to go in. What must happen first?","options":["A competent person must inspect it at the start of this shift","Nothing: it was inspected on Friday, so it is fine","The first person in checks it while they work","Nothing: it only needs inspecting once a week"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law says nobody may work in a supported excavation unless a competent person has inspected it at the start of that shift and is happy it is safe. Things can change between shifts. Friday's inspection does not cover Monday's shift.","why_not":{"1":"Friday's inspection does not cover Monday; it must be inspected at the start of each shift.","2":"A competent person must check it before anyone goes in, not someone while they work.","3":"Once a week is not enough; a supported trench is inspected at the start of every shift."},"source_title":"The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 22 (legislation.gov.uk)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/22"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-15","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are digging a trench close to an old garden wall. What danger does HSE warn about?","options":["The trench can dig away under the wall's shallow footings, and the wall can fall on you","There is no danger, as long as the digger does not touch the wall","None: the wall helps hold up the side of the trench, so it is safer","The only risk is a fine for damaging the wall"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Many garden and boundary walls have very shallow footings. Even a small trench nearby can undermine them, and the wall can collapse onto the people in the trench. Before digging, decide whether the wall needs extra support.","why_not":{"1":"The digger need not touch it; the trench can undermine the shallow footings and the wall can fall.","2":"The wall does not help; its shallow footings can be undermined and it can fall on you.","3":"The real risk is the wall falling onto people in the trench, not a fine."},"source_title":"Construction: Excavations (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/excavations.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-16","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A drain needs checking for damage. Before anyone plans to go inside, what should be asked first?","options":["Can the job be done from outside, for example with a remote camera?","Who is the smallest person who can fit through the opening?","Has everyone got a dust mask?","Can it be done quickly enough to skip the safety checks?"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The first duty for confined spaces is to avoid going in at all if the work can be done another way. Checks inside can often be done from outside, for example with a remote camera. Only if entry cannot be avoided do you move on to a safe system of work and rescue plans.","why_not":{"1":"First ask if anyone needs to go in at all; the work may be done from outside.","2":"Masks come later, if at all; the first question is whether going in can be avoided.","3":"Never skip safety checks; first ask if the job can be done from outside."},"source_title":"Confined spaces: A brief guide to working safely (HSE, INDG258)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg258.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-17","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Your team wants to use a petrol-driven pump inside a chamber below ground to clear water. What does HSE say?","options":["Never allow it: the exhaust gives off deadly carbon monoxide","It is fine as long as the cover is left open","It is fine for short jobs of under 15 minutes","It is fine as long as one person stays at the top"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Petrol engine exhaust contains carbon monoxide, a poisonous gas, and in a confined space the fumes build up. HSE says it is so dangerous that petrol-engined equipment should never be allowed in confined spaces. An open cover, a short job or a person at the top does not make it safe.","why_not":{"1":"An open cover does not make it safe; carbon monoxide from petrol engines builds up below ground.","2":"Carbon monoxide is too dangerous; HSE says never allow petrol engines in confined spaces, even briefly.","3":"A person at the top cannot stop the fumes building up; it should never be allowed."},"source_title":"Confined spaces: A brief guide to working safely (HSE, INDG258)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg258.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-18","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"When must the plan for rescuing someone from a confined space be ready?","options":["Before anyone goes in to work","As soon as someone inside gets into trouble","Only if the job will take more than an hour","By the end of the first day of the job"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law says nobody may go into a confined space to work unless proper rescue arrangements are already in place. In an emergency there is no time to make a plan.","why_not":{"1":"There is no time to make a plan in an emergency; it must be ready before anyone goes in.","2":"A rescue plan is needed for every confined space entry, however long the job.","3":"The plan must be ready before anyone goes in, not at the end of the day."},"source_title":"The Confined Spaces Regulations 1997, regulation 5 (legislation.gov.uk)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1997/1713/regulation/5"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-20","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A hoist on site has a sign saying it is for goods only. You need to get to the third floor. Can you ride on it?","options":["No: never ride on a goods-only hoist","Yes, if you stand with the materials to stop them moving","Yes, if the hoist operator says it is OK","Yes, if you hold on tight to the side"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A goods-only hoist is not built or marked for carrying people. HSE says passengers must never ride on one. Use the stairs, or a hoist that is marked for passengers.","why_not":{"1":"Standing with the load does not make it safe; passengers must never ride a goods-only hoist.","2":"Nobody can make it OK; HSE says never let passengers ride on a goods-only hoist.","3":"Holding on does not help; a goods-only hoist is not built to carry people."},"source_title":"Construction: Lifting operations (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/lifting-operations.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-21","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You pick up a chain sling to use for a lift. It has no mark showing its safe working load. What should you do?","options":["Do not use it: put it to one side and tell your supervisor","Use it, if it looks strong enough","Use it, but only for light loads","Guess the safe working load from its size and write it on"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says to use only certified lifting equipment that is marked with its safe working load and is not overdue for examination. Without the mark, nobody knows what it can safely lift. Guessing from its size is not safe. Keep it out of use and report it.","why_not":{"1":"Looks are not enough; without a safe working load mark nobody knows what it can lift.","2":"Even light loads are a risk; use only equipment marked with its safe working load.","3":"Guessing from size is not safe; only certified, marked equipment should be used."},"source_title":"Lifting equipment at work: A brief guide (HSE, INDG290)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg290.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b1-25","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A wall that may be holding up part of a building is going to be taken out. Who should decide whether temporary props are needed?","options":["A suitably qualified and experienced engineer","The worker doing the job, by looking at it","Nobody: props are only needed once cracks appear","The client, because they own the building"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Taking out a wall can make part of a building collapse without warning. HSE says a suitably qualified and experienced engineer should decide if temporary support is needed. Waiting for cracks is too late, and guessing by eye is not good enough.","why_not":{"1":"Looking at it is not enough; a qualified, experienced engineer should decide.","2":"Waiting for cracks is too late; the building could collapse without warning.","3":"Owning the building does not make the client an engineer; a qualified engineer should decide."},"source_title":"Construction: Structural stability during alteration, demolition and dismantling (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/buildings.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-01","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are working from a stepladder that has nothing at the top to hold on to. Which steps should you not stand and work on?","options":["Only the very top step","The top three steps, counting the very top one","Only the bottom two steps","None of them, as long as a workmate holds the stepladder"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says do not stand and work on the top three steps of a stepladder, and that includes a step that forms the very top, unless there is a suitable handhold. It is not just the very top step. A workmate holding the stepladder does not give you a handhold.","why_not":{"0":"It is not just the very top step; do not work on the top three steps without a handhold.","2":"The bottom steps are not the problem; it is the top three steps without a handhold.","3":"A workmate holding it is not a handhold; keep off the top three steps."},"source_title":"Safe use of ladders and stepladders: Types of ladder and using them safely (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/work-at-height/ladders/types-of-ladder.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-02","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A leaning ladder is the way up onto a scaffold platform. How far should the ladder stick up above the platform you step off onto?","options":["It should stop level with the platform, so you can step straight off","About 30 centimetres","At least 1 metre","It does not matter, as long as the ladder is tied"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says a ladder used to get to another level should be tied and should stick up at least 1 metre above the place where you step off. This gives you something safe to hold on to as you get on and off. Tying it is needed as well, but it does not replace the extra height.","why_not":{"0":"Stopping level gives you nothing to hold; it should stick up at least 1 metre.","1":"30 centimetres is too short; HSE says at least 1 metre above the landing point.","3":"Tying is needed too, but the ladder must still stick up at least 1 metre."},"source_title":"Safe use of ladders and stepladders: Securing ladders and ladders used for access (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/work-at-height/ladders/securing-ladders-used-for-access.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-03","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are putting up a mobile tower scaffold. One corner is on lower ground, so a workmate wants to put some building blocks under that wheel. What does HSE say?","options":["Never use bricks or blocks to take the weight of any part of a tower","That is fine, as long as the blocks are solid concrete","That is fine, as long as the tower is not very tall","That is fine, as long as nobody stands on that corner"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says a tower must stand on firm, level ground with its locked wheels or base plates properly supported. Bricks or building blocks must never take the weight of any part of a tower, whatever they are made of and however tall the tower is.","why_not":{"1":"Even solid concrete blocks must never take the weight of any part of a tower.","2":"Height makes no difference; bricks or blocks must never hold up a tower.","3":"Blocks must never take the weight of a tower, whether or not anyone stands there."},"source_title":"Construction: Tower scaffolds (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/scaffold.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-04","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Your supervisor asks you to tie plastic sheeting to the outside of a scaffold to keep the dust in. Nobody has told the scaffold company. What should happen first?","options":["Nothing: sheeting is light, so it makes no difference to the scaffold","The people who designed and put up the scaffold must be told, so they can make sure it is designed to take sheeting","Tie it to the top lift only, then tell the scaffold company afterwards","Ask the client, because it is their building"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says never put sheeting or debris netting on a scaffold without first telling the people who designed and put it up. They need to make sure the scaffold is designed for it. Telling them afterwards is too late, and the client is not the person who can decide this.","why_not":{"0":"Sheeting catches the wind and adds load, so the scaffold must be designed for it.","2":"You must tell them before sheeting any part; afterwards is too late.","3":"The client cannot decide this; the scaffold designers and erectors must be told."},"source_title":"Construction: Scaffolds (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/scaffoldinginfo.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-07","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are working on a scaffold at roof level. The wind has become strong and gusty, and loose sheets are starting to lift. What does the law say?","options":["Carry on, but work faster to finish before it gets worse","Carry on, as long as you clip on a harness","Carry on, because the scaffold is tied to the building","Work at height must only go on when the weather does not put people's safety at risk, so stop and get down safely"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The Work at Height Regulations say work at height must only be carried out when the weather does not put the health or safety of the people doing it at risk. Working faster, wearing a harness or the scaffold being tied in does not make strong gusts safe.","why_not":{"0":"Working faster does not make strong gusts safe; stop and get down.","1":"A harness does not make bad weather safe; work at height must stop.","2":"Ties hold the scaffold, not you; work at height must stop in unsafe weather."},"source_title":"The Work at Height Regulations 2005, regulation 4 (legislation.gov.uk)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2005/735/regulation/4"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-11","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Depending on the ground, how much can one cubic metre of soil weigh? (That is a block of soil 1 metre long, 1 metre wide and 1 metre deep.)","options":["About 50 kilograms","About 200 kilograms","More than 1.5 tonnes (1,500 kilograms)","About 500 kilograms"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says that, depending on the conditions, one cubic metre of soil can weigh more than 1.5 tonnes. That is why even a small collapse of a trench side can crush or bury someone working in it.","why_not":{"0":"Far too light; a cubic metre of soil can weigh more than 1.5 tonnes.","1":"Much too light; HSE says it can weigh more than 1.5 tonnes.","3":"Still too light; HSE says it can weigh more than 1.5 tonnes."},"source_title":"Construction: Excavations (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/excavations.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-12","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are in a trench where a pipe and some cables have been uncovered. The ladder is at the far end. Can you use the uncovered pipe as a step to climb out?","options":["No: uncovered services must never be used to hold on to or stand on when climbing out","Yes, as long as it is a metal pipe","Yes, as long as you step on it gently","Yes, as long as the pipe is supported underneath"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says services that have been uncovered may need to be supported, and must never be used as handholds or footholds for climbing out of an excavation. Use the ladder or another proper way out.","why_not":{"1":"Metal or not, uncovered services must never be used as footholds.","2":"Stepping gently is still standing on it; never use services as handholds or footholds.","3":"Even supported, services must never be used for climbing out; use the ladder."},"source_title":"Avoiding danger from underground services (HSE, HSG47)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg47.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-13","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"While hand digging, you uncover a pipe or cable covered in black plastic. Nobody knows yet what it is. How should you treat it?","options":["As an old water pipe that is safe to cut","As a dead cable, because it is not on the plans","As a phone cable, which cannot hurt you","As a live electricity cable"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says water pipes, electricity cables and phone cables can all be covered in black plastic. Until you know for sure what it is, treat any black plastic service as a live electricity cable. HSE also says to treat all services as live until they have been disconnected and proven safe where you are working.","why_not":{"0":"Black plastic can cover electricity cables too; treat it as a live cable.","1":"Not being on the plans does not make it dead; assume all services are live.","2":"It could be an electricity cable; treat any black plastic service as live."},"source_title":"Avoiding danger from underground services (HSE, HSG47)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg47.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-15","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A workmate wants to lean over and put just his head into the opening of a chamber for a quick look. The air inside has not been tested. Why is this dangerous?","options":["Low oxygen can affect you very fast, often with no warning, even if only your head is inside","It is not dangerous, as long as your feet stay outside","It is only dangerous if you can smell something bad","It is only dangerous if you stay there for more than 10 minutes"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says even a small drop in oxygen can affect how well you think. The effects are very fast and there is usually no warning to your senses. This can happen even when only a person's head is inside a confined space, and very low oxygen can cause unconsciousness and death.","why_not":{"1":"Low oxygen can affect you even when only your head is inside.","2":"There is usually no warning to your senses, so you may smell nothing.","3":"The effects are very fast, so even a quick look can harm you."},"source_title":"Safe work in confined spaces: Confined Spaces Regulations 1997, Approved Code of Practice, Regulations and guidance (HSE, L101)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/l101.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-16","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You need to use power tools and lights inside a metal tank. To help prevent electric shock, what kind of equipment does HSE say to use?","options":["Normal mains (230 volt) tools, as long as they look in good condition","Any tools, as long as you wear rubber gloves","Extra low voltage equipment (usually under 25 volts), with a residual current device (RCD) where needed","Any tools, as long as you stand on a dry piece of wood"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says that in some confined spaces, such as inside metal tanks, precautions against electric shock include using extra low voltage equipment, typically under 25 volts, and residual current devices where needed. Gloves, a piece of wood or tools that look fine are not the precautions HSE gives.","why_not":{"0":"Looking fine is not enough; HSE says use extra low voltage equipment inside metal tanks.","1":"Gloves are not what HSE gives; use extra low voltage tools, with an RCD where needed.","3":"Standing on wood is not HSE's precaution; use extra low voltage equipment."},"source_title":"Confined spaces: A brief guide to working safely (HSE, INDG258)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg258.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-17","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Before your team goes into a confined space, the supervisor issues a 'permit to work'. What is it for?","options":["It lets you skip the air test if the job is short","It is a formal check that every part of the safe way of working is in place before anyone goes in","It is just a record of who was on site that day","It means one person can go in alone without anyone outside"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says a permit to work makes sure a formal check is done, so that every part of the safe system of work is in place before people go in or work in the confined space. It is also a way for managers, supervisors and the people doing the work to keep each other informed. It does not replace any of the safety measures.","why_not":{"0":"A permit never lets you skip the air test or any other safety step.","2":"It is more than a list of names; it checks the whole safe system is in place.","3":"A permit does not replace safety measures, such as someone keeping watch outside."},"source_title":"Confined spaces: A brief guide to working safely (HSE, INDG258)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg258.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-18","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A large steel tank has been shut for a long time and rust has formed inside. Why could the air inside be dangerous?","options":["When rust forms it can use up the oxygen, so there may not be enough to breathe","Rust gives off a strong smell that will warn you, so it is not dangerous","Rust only harms the metal, so the air is fine","Rust makes the air too damp to see through"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says a lack of oxygen can happen inside steel tanks and vessels when rust forms. Rust does not warn you with a smell. This is one reason the air in a confined space may need to be tested by a competent person with a gas detector before anyone goes in.","why_not":{"1":"You cannot rely on smell; rust forming can use up the oxygen in a tank.","2":"Rust forming can use up the oxygen in the air, so the air may not be fine.","3":"Damp is not the danger; rust forming can leave too little oxygen to breathe."},"source_title":"Confined spaces: A brief guide to working safely (HSE, INDG258)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg258.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-19","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are using a gin wheel (a simple pulley wheel) to pull buckets up a scaffold. The proper hook is missing, so a workmate bends a piece of steel reinforcing bar into a hook. Can you use it?","options":["No: use a proper hook made to stop the load coming off, or a hook with a safety catch","Yes, as long as the bar is thick enough","Yes, but only for light buckets","Yes, as long as you wrap tape around the end of the hook"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says a gin wheel needs a proper hook designed to stop the load coming off, or a hook with a safety catch, which helps hold the load if it snags. Do not use bent reinforcing bars or other home-made hooks, whatever the load.","why_not":{"1":"Thickness does not matter; never use bent bars or other home-made hooks.","2":"Even light loads can come off a home-made hook; use a proper hook.","3":"Tape does not make it safe; use a proper hook or one with a safety catch."},"source_title":"Construction: Lifting operations (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/lifting-operations.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-20","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A load is about to be lifted, but its weight is not spread evenly. Its centre of gravity has been found. What does HSE say to do next?","options":["Lift it fast to full height, to get it over with","Have someone stand on the light end to balance it","Lift it, and steady it from underneath with your hands","Raise it a few inches off the ground and pause"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says before lifting an unbalanced load, find out its centre of gravity, then raise it a few inches off the ground and pause. If it slips or tips, little harm is done when it drops from that height.","why_not":{"0":"Lifting fast to full height means a slip could drop it from high up; pause first.","1":"That puts someone on a moving load; HSE says raise it a few inches and pause.","2":"Hands under a load could be crushed if it drops; raise it a little and pause."},"source_title":"Lifting equipment at work: A brief guide (HSE, INDG290)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg290.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-22","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A goods hoist on site has a gate at each floor. When should these landing gates be open?","options":["All day, so loading is quicker","Only while the hoist is being loaded or unloaded at that floor","Whenever the hoist platform is at ground level","Whenever someone on that floor is waiting for the hoist"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says that to stop people falling down the hoistway, the landing gates must be kept closed except while loading and unloading. The gates should also be secure and not able to swing into the hoistway.","why_not":{"0":"Gates left open all day let people fall down the hoistway.","2":"Gates stay shut except while loading or unloading at that floor.","3":"Waiting is not loading; keep gates shut so nobody falls down the hoistway."},"source_title":"Construction: Lifting operations (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/lifting-operations.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-23","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"During a strip-out, rubble is being piled up on an upper floor before it is taken away. Why does this need to be controlled?","options":["It does not: floors are built to hold any weight","Only because it looks untidy","The build-up of debris adds load, and a structure must never be overloaded, or the floor could collapse","Only because the rubble might get wet"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says structures should never be overloaded. The build-up of debris during demolition adds load to the floors, and a structure carrying extra load like this may need temporary bracing or props. Floors are not built to hold any amount of weight.","why_not":{"0":"Floors are not built to hold any amount of weight; piled debris adds load and can overload them.","1":"Tidiness is not the main reason; the extra weight can overload the floor.","3":"Wet or dry, the build-up of debris adds load to the floor."},"source_title":"Construction: Structural stability during alteration, demolition and dismantling (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/buildings.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b2-24","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A row of temporary props is holding up a floor while a wall below it is taken out. A workmate wants to tie a pulley to one of the props to lift materials. What does the law say?","options":["A temporary support must only be used for the job it was designed and put in for","That is fine, as long as the materials are light","That is fine, as long as two props are used instead of one","That is fine, as long as the props were checked this morning"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The CDM Regulations 2015 say any temporary support must be designed and kept able to take the loads it could face, and must only be used for the purpose it was designed and put in for. Hanging a pulley on a prop adds loads it was not designed for, even with light materials.","why_not":{"1":"Even light loads are wrong; a temporary support must only do the job it was designed for.","2":"Two props still take loads they were not designed for.","3":"A morning check does not change what the prop was designed for."},"source_title":"The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 19 (legislation.gov.uk)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/19"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-01","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Your ladder is a little too short to reach a gutter. A workmate says to stand the ladder's feet on the roof of the van parked below. What should you do?","options":["Do it, as long as the van's handbrake is on","Stand the ladder on a pallet instead, because it is lower","Do it, as long as someone sits in the van to keep it steady","Get a longer ladder or other equipment: never stand a ladder on something that can move, like a van"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says do not stand a ladder on things that can move, and it names vans and pallets. A handbrake or someone sitting in the van does not make the van roof a safe, firm base. Get a ladder that is long enough, or use other equipment.","why_not":{"0":"A handbrake does not make a van a safe base; never stand a ladder on a van.","1":"HSE names pallets too; never stand a ladder on something that can move.","2":"Someone in the van does not make it firm; never stand a ladder on a van."},"source_title":"Safe use of ladders and stepladders: Types of ladder and using them safely (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/work-at-height/ladders/types-of-ladder.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-02","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"An electrician needs a ladder to reach a light fitting he is working on. Which kind of ladder should he use?","options":["A metal ladder, as long as its feet stand on a dry floor","A fibreglass or wooden ladder, because it does not carry electricity","An aluminium ladder, because it is the lightest to carry","Any ladder, as long as he wears rubber-soled boots"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says to use a ladder that does not conduct electricity, such as fibreglass or timber, for any electrical work. Metal ladders such as aluminium ones conduct electricity. Boots or a dry floor do not change what the ladder is made of.","why_not":{"0":"Metal ladders carry electricity; a dry floor does not change that.","2":"Aluminium carries electricity; use fibreglass or wood for electrical work.","3":"Boots do not change what the ladder is made of; use a non-conductive ladder."},"source_title":"Safe use of ladders and stepladders: Types of ladder and using them safely (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/work-at-height/ladders/types-of-ladder.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-05","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are on a stepladder fitting a smoke alarm to a ceiling. For a moment you need both hands. How can you still keep 'three points of contact'?","options":["One foot on the steps and both hands on the alarm","You cannot: you must keep one hand on the stepladder, so the job is not allowed","Two feet on the steps, and your body (knees or chest) resting against the stepladder","Stand on one foot and hook the other leg round the stepladder"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says three points of contact does not always mean two feet and one hand. When you need both hands free for a short time, it can be two feet plus your body, using your knees or chest, supported by the stepladder. Make sure there is something to hold before and after.","why_not":{"0":"One foot is not enough support; use two feet and your body against the steps.","1":"You can do it: two feet and your body against the steps count as three points.","3":"Standing on one foot is unsafe; keep both feet on the steps and lean your body on them."},"source_title":"Common work at height myths (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/work-at-height/myths.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-07","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Your team is planning some work at height. Someone says the rescue plan is simply: 'If anyone gets stuck or falls, we ring 999.' What does HSE say?","options":["The plan should not rely only on the emergency services: the team needs its own set way to rescue people","Make the plan after work starts, once you see what could go wrong","That is enough, because only the fire service is allowed to rescue people","No rescue plan is needed if the job is short"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says to plan for emergencies and rescue, agree a set procedure, and make sure workers know it. It also says do not rely entirely on the emergency services for rescue in your plan.","why_not":{"1":"The rescue plan must be made before work starts, so everyone knows it.","2":"Not true; HSE says do not rely only on the emergency services to rescue people.","3":"Even short jobs need a plan for emergencies and rescue."},"source_title":"Working at height: A brief guide, INDG401(rev2) (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg401.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-09","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"To get a long beam onto a scaffold platform, the scaffolder has to take off a section of guard-rail. What does the law say?","options":["Take it off only for as long as needed, have other safety measures while it is off, and put it back as soon as you can","Work with the gap open: nothing else is needed if everyone is careful","Take off the whole length of guard-rail so there is plenty of room","Leave it off for the rest of the week, as more beams are coming"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law says guard-rails and similar protection may be taken off only for the time and to the extent needed, and must be put back as soon as practicable. Work must not go on while they are off unless other effective safety measures are in place.","why_not":{"1":"Being careful is not enough; other safety measures are needed while the guard-rail is off.","2":"Remove only as much as you need, for as short a time as possible.","3":"It must go back as soon as it can, not stay off for days."},"source_title":"The Work at Height Regulations 2005, Schedule 2 (legislation.gov.uk)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2005/735/schedule/2"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-10","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A scaffold tie (a fixing that holds the scaffold to the building) is in the way of a window you are fitting. What should happen?","options":["Take it out yourself, because one tie does not matter","Loosen it a little so you can work round it","Take it out, and put it back at the end of the day","Ask for a competent scaffolder: if the tie is moved, an equal tie must be fitted nearby"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Ties keep the scaffold stable. Only competent people may alter a scaffold. HSE says if a tie is removed so work can go on, an equivalent tie must be fitted nearby to keep the scaffold stable. Taking it out yourself, even for a day, can make the scaffold unsafe.","why_not":{"0":"Every tie matters; only a competent person may alter a scaffold.","1":"Loosening a tie can make the scaffold unstable; only competent people may alter it.","2":"Even for a day the scaffold may be unsafe; an equal tie must be fitted nearby."},"source_title":"Construction: Scaffolds (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/scaffoldinginfo.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-11","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You arrive at a scaffold the scaffolders have not finished. It has warning signs, and a barrier blocks the ladder. You only need to go up for 5 minutes. What do you do?","options":["Go up carefully, keeping away from the unfinished end","Go up, as long as a scaffolder is still working on it","Stay off: it must not be used until it is finished and ready for use","Move the barrier, go up, and put the barrier back afterwards"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says a scaffold that is not ready for use, including while it is being put up, taken down or altered, should have warning signs and a physical barrier to stop people getting to the danger area. The signs and barrier mean keep off, however short the job.","why_not":{"0":"Nobody should use an unfinished scaffold, even keeping away from the unfinished end.","1":"Scaffolders working on it does not make it ready; stay off until it is finished.","3":"The barrier is there to keep you off; never move it, however short the job."},"source_title":"Construction: Scaffolds (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/scaffoldinginfo.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-12","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You will work from a MEWP (mobile elevating work platform) basket next to a deep river. The risk assessment says the biggest danger is drowning if the MEWP ends up in the water. What should you wear?","options":["Nothing extra, because the basket has guard-rails","A harness clipped to the basket, with a life jacket over it","A harness clipped to the basket, as on any MEWP","A life jacket, not a harness"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says work next to or over water must be assessed to see whether the bigger risk is falling from the basket or drowning if the MEWP falls into the water. Where there is a risk of drowning, wear a life jacket, not a harness.","why_not":{"0":"Guard-rails will not help if the MEWP goes into the water; wear a life jacket.","1":"A harness could hold you to a sinking MEWP; where drowning is the risk, wear no harness.","2":"Here drowning is the main risk, so HSE says a life jacket, not a harness."},"source_title":"The selection, management and use of mobile elevating work platforms, GEIS6 (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/geis6.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-13","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are in a MEWP fixing long lengths of pipe. They do not fit in the basket, so a workmate says to rest them across the guard-rails. What does HSE say?","options":["It is fine if you hold them on the rail with one hand","It is fine for short pipes under 3 metres","Never balance materials on the guard-rails: use other equipment to handle them, such as a crane or telehandler","It is fine, as long as the pipes are taped to the rails"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says materials should never be balanced on the handrails of a MEWP. Instead, think about using other plant to handle the materials, such as a second MEWP, a scissor lift, a crane or a telehandler. This cuts the risk of the MEWP overturning and of handling injuries.","why_not":{"0":"Holding them does not make it safe; never balance materials on the guard-rails.","1":"Short pipes are no exception; never balance materials on a MEWP's guard-rails.","3":"Taped on, they are still balanced on the rails; use other equipment to move them."},"source_title":"The selection, management and use of mobile elevating work platforms, GEIS6 (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/geis6.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-14","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"The overload alarm on your MEWP keeps going off after you load tools and blocks into the basket. A workmate says he knows how to switch the alarm off. What should you do?","options":["Never switch it off: take weight out, because people, tools and materials all count towards the load","Keep working: the alarm is only a reminder","Switch it off just for this one job","Switch it off, because it only counts the weight of the people"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Everything in the basket adds to the MEWP's safe working load: people, tools, materials and any handling devices. HSE says never disable the overload system. If it goes off, remove the overload straight away if you can.","why_not":{"1":"The alarm warns of overload; take the extra weight out straight away.","2":"Never switch off the overload system, not even for one job.","3":"Wrong: tools and materials count towards the load as well as people."},"source_title":"The selection, management and use of mobile elevating work platforms, GEIS6 (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/geis6.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-16","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"How should you check your webbing fall-arrest lanyard before you use it?","options":["Look at it through the bag: if it looks clean, it is fine","Pass the whole lanyard slowly through your hands in good light, feeling and looking for damage","Give it a sharp tug to see if it holds","Look at the date on the label: if it is in date, it is fine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says the pre-use check should be done by feel and by eye. Pass the whole lanyard slowly through your hands to find small cuts in the edges, fibres that have gone soft or hard, or dirt and chemicals. Look at it in good light. It normally takes a few minutes.","why_not":{"0":"A look through the bag misses small cuts; pass the whole lanyard through your hands.","2":"A tug will not find small cuts or damaged fibres; check it by feel and by eye.","3":"Being in date does not show damage; check the whole lanyard by hand and eye."},"source_title":"Inspecting fall arrest equipment made from webbing or rope, INDG367 (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg367.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-17","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A trench will need trench sheets and props. The digger is ready, but the sheets will not arrive until tomorrow. What should happen?","options":["Dig today and fit the sheets tomorrow","Dig today, but only let people in for quick jobs","Wait: the support equipment should be on site before the digging starts","Dig today if the ground looks firm"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says decide what support a trench needs before digging, and make sure the equipment, such as trench sheets and props, is on site before work starts.","why_not":{"0":"Supports must be on site before digging starts, not the next day.","1":"Even quick jobs in an unsupported trench are dangerous; wait for the supports.","3":"Firm-looking ground can still collapse; the supports must be there first."},"source_title":"Construction: Excavations (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/excavations.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-18","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Instead of using supports, the sides of an excavation are being sloped back (battered). The ground is wet. What does HSE say about the slope?","options":["It can be steeper, because wet soil sticks together","It needs to be much flatter (less steep) than it would be in dry ground","Water makes no difference: only the depth matters","It can be the same as in dry ground"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Sloping the sides back to a safe angle can make an excavation safer. HSE says that in wet ground a much flatter slope is needed.","why_not":{"0":"Wrong way round; wet ground needs a much flatter slope, not a steeper one.","2":"Water does matter; HSE says wet ground needs a much flatter slope.","3":"Wet ground needs a much flatter slope than dry ground."},"source_title":"Construction: Excavations (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/excavations.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-20","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A trench is being dug beside a large tree, and the digger is cutting through its thick roots. Why is this a safety problem, not just bad for the tree?","options":["Tree roots can carry electricity","It is not a safety problem: it only harms the tree","Cut roots give off a poisonous gas","Cutting or damaging the roots can make the tree fall over"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE warns that damage to trees during excavation, including cutting through or damaging roots, can lead to harm from trees falling over.","why_not":{"0":"Roots do not carry electricity; the danger is the tree falling over.","1":"It is a safety problem; cutting roots can make the tree fall on people.","2":"Roots do not give off poison gas; the danger is the tree falling over."},"source_title":"Construction: Excavations (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/excavations.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-21","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are digging a deep pit on the site of an old gas works. Why does HSE warn about pits and trenches in ground like this?","options":["There is no extra danger once the old buildings have gone","The only danger is getting your clothes dirty","The ground is always too hard to dig by hand","Poisonous gas can leak into them from the contaminated ground"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says poisonous gas, fume or vapour can leak into trenches and pits in contaminated land, such as old refuse tips and old gas works.","why_not":{"0":"The ground itself can still be contaminated after the buildings have gone.","1":"The real danger is poisonous gas leaking into the pit.","2":"Hard ground is not HSE's warning; poisonous gas can leak into pits here."},"source_title":"Confined spaces: A brief guide to working safely, INDG258(rev1) (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg258.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-22","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A workmate says: 'This room is not on any list of confined spaces, so it can never be one.' Is he right?","options":["No, but only if the room has no door at all","No: a place can become a confined space because of the work being done there, or while it is being built or changed","Yes: only places on an official list count","Yes: only spaces underground can be confined spaces"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says it is not possible to give a complete list of confined spaces. Some places become confined spaces when work is done in them, or while they are being built or altered. What matters is whether there is a risk of death or serious injury from things like gas, fumes or lack of oxygen.","why_not":{"0":"A room can be a confined space even with a door; it depends on the risk.","2":"There is no full list; places can become confined spaces because of the work.","3":"Confined spaces can be above ground too, such as tanks and badly aired rooms."},"source_title":"Confined spaces: A brief guide to working safely, INDG258(rev1) (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg258.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-24","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Work is to be done in a chamber where flammable vapour is likely. What kind of tools and lights does HSE say are essential?","options":["Non-sparking tools and specially protected lighting","Ordinary work lights, as long as they are LED and stay cool","Any tools, as long as the lid of the chamber is left open","Ordinary tools, as long as they are battery powered"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Where a flammable or explosive atmosphere is likely, a spark from a tool or light could set it off. HSE says non-sparking tools and specially protected lighting are essential in these places.","why_not":{"1":"Ordinary lights can still spark; specially protected lighting is essential.","2":"An open lid does not stop a spark lighting the vapour; use non-sparking tools.","3":"Battery tools can still spark; use non-sparking tools where vapour is likely."},"source_title":"Confined spaces: A brief guide to working safely, INDG258(rev1) (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg258.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-25","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are working in a sewer where only a little water is flowing along the bottom. Can drowning still be a danger?","options":["No: you cannot drown in water below knee height","Only if you cannot swim","Yes: water can flow in, and people can drown in even a small depth of liquid","No: sewers never fill up with water"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says liquid can flow into a confined space, for example in sewers, and cause drowning. It also warns that drowning can happen in even a small depth of liquid.","why_not":{"0":"You can drown in even a small depth of liquid, and more can flow in.","1":"Being able to swim does not stop it; people drown in even small depths of liquid.","3":"Liquid can flow into sewers and cause drowning."},"source_title":"Safe work in confined spaces, L101 (Third edition) (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/l101.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-26","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are asked to climb into a large hopper, half full of dry sand, to clear a blockage. What hidden danger does HSE warn about?","options":["There is no danger, because sand is not a chemical","Dry sand can catch fire on its own","The sand can flow and bury you, so you cannot breathe","The only danger is getting sand in your boots"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says free-flowing solids such as sand, grain and flour can cover a person and stop them breathing. In a confined space the danger is greater, because the material has nowhere else to flow.","why_not":{"0":"It does not need to be a chemical; flowing sand can bury you.","1":"Fire is not the warning; sand can flow over you and stop you breathing.","3":"The real danger is being buried by flowing sand and not being able to breathe."},"source_title":"Safe work in confined spaces, L101 (Third edition) (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/l101.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-27","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A drainage chamber has wet limestone chippings packed around the pipes. Why might the air inside be unsafe to breathe?","options":["Wet limestone gives off extra oxygen, which makes a fire likely","Stone chippings cannot change the air in a space","Limestone only matters when it is dry and dusty","Wet limestone chippings can raise the level of carbon dioxide, so there may not be enough oxygen"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE lists wet limestone chippings used in drainage work as one cause of a lack of oxygen, because they raise the level of carbon dioxide when they get wet.","why_not":{"0":"Wet limestone does not add oxygen; it raises carbon dioxide and leaves less oxygen.","1":"Wet limestone chippings can change the air by raising carbon dioxide.","2":"It is when limestone gets wet that it raises carbon dioxide levels."},"source_title":"Safe work in confined spaces, L101 (Third edition) (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/l101.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-28","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"Your workmate is down a manhole. A dumper has been parked with its engine running right next to the opening. Why is this dangerous?","options":["It is only a noise problem","Exhaust fumes from the dumper can get into the manhole and poison the air","Exhaust fumes always rise, so they cannot get into a manhole","It is only dangerous if the dumper runs on petrol"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says harmful fumes can get into a confined space from things happening outside it, including exhaust fumes from mobile plant on construction sites.","why_not":{"0":"It is not just noise; exhaust fumes can get into the manhole.","2":"Wrong; exhaust fumes from plant outside can get into confined spaces.","3":"Exhaust from any mobile plant can get in, not just from petrol engines."},"source_title":"Safe work in confined spaces, L101 (Third edition) (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/l101.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-29","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A site crane only ever lifts materials, never people. There is no examination scheme for it. At least how often must it have a thorough examination by a competent person?","options":["Only once, when it is first put up","At least once every 12 months","At least once every 7 days","At least once every 5 years"],"correct":1,"explanation":"LOLER sets in-service thorough examination periods: at least every 6 months for lifting accessories and for equipment that lifts people, and at least every year for all other lifting equipment, unless a competent person has drawn up an examination scheme.","why_not":{"0":"One check when new is not enough; it needs a thorough examination at least yearly.","2":"7 days is not the legal period for thorough examination; it is at least every 12 months.","3":"5 years is far too long; it must be examined at least every 12 months."},"source_title":"Lifting equipment at work: A brief guide, INDG290(rev1) (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg290.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-30","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A site hoist has not been used for many months while the site was shut. Its last thorough examination was not long before the site closed. What should happen before it is used again?","options":["Nothing: its last examination is still in date","The operator just runs it up and down once to see if it works","Grease the moving parts and put it back into use","It should be thoroughly examined again, because being out of use for a long time counts as exceptional circumstances"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says lifting equipment should always be thoroughly examined after 'exceptional circumstances', and gives being out of use for long periods as an example, along with damage or failure. This applies even if the last examination is still in date.","why_not":{"0":"Being out of use for long counts as exceptional, so it needs examining again anyway.","1":"A quick test run is not a thorough examination by a competent person.","2":"Greasing is not an examination; it must be thoroughly examined first."},"source_title":"Thorough examination of lifting equipment: A simple guide for employers, INDG422 (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg422.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-31","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A bundle of steel is about to be lifted. Nobody is sure how heavy it is, and it might be too much for the lifting gear. What should happen?","options":["Do not lift it until you are sure of its weight and that the equipment can take it","Lift it, but keep everyone well back","Lift it slowly and see whether the sling holds","Guess the weight from its size and go ahead"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE's list of things not to do includes lifting a load if you doubt its weight or whether the equipment is good enough. Find out the weight first, and never go over the safe working load of the machine or the accessories, such as slings.","why_not":{"1":"Keeping back does not stop the gear failing; do not lift if you doubt the weight.","2":"Testing it by lifting risks the sling failing; find out the weight first.","3":"Guessing could overload the gear; never lift if you doubt the weight."},"source_title":"Lifting equipment at work: A brief guide, INDG290(rev1) (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg290.pdf"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-32","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"During a crane lift, the wind picks up and the load starts to swing about. Who should have the authority to stop the lift?","options":["Nobody: once a lift has started it must be finished","Only the client who owns the building","Only the crane hire company's office","The crane supervisor, who directs the lift and can stop it if it becomes dangerous"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says the crane supervisor directs and supervises the lift. The supervisor must be competent and must have the authority to stop the lifting operation if it is judged dangerous to carry on.","why_not":{"0":"A dangerous lift should be stopped; the crane supervisor has the authority to stop it.","1":"The client is not running the lift; the crane supervisor can stop it.","2":"The hire office cannot see the lift; the crane supervisor on site can stop it."},"source_title":"Construction: Lifting operations (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/lifting-operations.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-33","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"How should the controls of a goods hoist on a building site be set up?","options":["With call buttons on every floor, so anyone can bring it up","Anywhere, as long as the hoist has a warning horn","So it can be worked from one place only, where the operator can see all the landing levels","With controls on the platform, so a person can ride up and work it"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says goods hoists should be set up so they can be operated from one position only, for example at ground level, where the operator can see all the landing levels.","why_not":{"0":"Goods hoists should be worked from one place only, not from every floor.","1":"A horn is not enough; the operator must work it from one place seeing all landings.","3":"People must never ride a goods hoist; it is worked from one position."},"source_title":"Construction: Lifting operations (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/lifting-operations.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-35","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A building in a busy town centre is being knocked down floor by floor from the top, using small machines. Why is this 'top-down' method often chosen?","options":["It gives more control over how stable the building stays and where the rubble goes","It means no exclusion zone is needed","It is always the fastest method","It means the building does not need a survey first"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says top-down demolition, taking a building down floor by floor with small plant, allows control of the stability of the structure and of the build-up and spread of debris. That is why it is often preferred on town centre sites.","why_not":{"1":"Top-down does not remove the need for an exclusion zone; it gives more control of debris.","2":"Speed is not the reason; it gives control of stability and debris.","3":"A survey is still needed; top-down is chosen for control, not to skip checks."},"source_title":"Construction: Demolition (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/demolition.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-36","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"During demolition, in what order should parts of a structure usually be taken down?","options":["The main stiff core goes first, to get it out of the way","The bottom floor goes first, so the rest drops down","Whatever is easiest to reach, in any order","Parts that are not needed to keep it standing go first, working towards the stiff parts that hold it up"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says the order of work should rule out an uncontrolled collapse. For example, parts that are not critical to overall stability are removed first, and the work then moves towards the stiff parts, such as braced cores, that keep the structure up.","why_not":{"0":"Taking the stiff core first could cause an uncontrolled collapse; it comes last.","1":"Taking the bottom first could bring the building down out of control.","2":"Any order could cause an uncontrolled collapse; follow a planned order."},"source_title":"Construction: Demolition (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/demolition.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-37","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"The exclusion zone round a demolition job was marked out on the first day. The work has now moved on to a different stage. What does HSE say?","options":["Once marked, an exclusion zone must never be changed","Exclusion zones may need to change as the work changes, and if anything collapses the debris must stay inside the zone","Small bits of debris landing outside the zone do not matter","Exclusion zones only matter on the first day"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says exclusion zones may need to change as the work goes on, so the plan should allow for this. If there is a collapse, deliberate or accidental, the debris must stay inside the zone.","why_not":{"0":"Exclusion zones may need to change as the work changes.","2":"All debris must stay inside the zone if anything collapses, even small bits.","3":"Exclusion zones matter for the whole job, not just day one."},"source_title":"Construction: Demolition (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/demolition.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-38","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"A sheeted scaffold is tied to a building that is being taken down from the top. The building is now lower than the top of the scaffold. What is the danger?","options":["There is no danger: sheeting makes a scaffold stronger","The sheeted scaffold sticking up above the building catches the wind like a sail, so it must be lowered as the building comes down","None, as long as the ties are taken off so the scaffold can move with the wind","The only problem is that dust can blow out"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Sheeting makes the wind push harder on a scaffold, and that load goes into the building through the ties. HSE says a sheeted scaffold that relies on ties must be lowered as the building is brought down, and must not act as a large sail above the partly demolished structure.","why_not":{"0":"Sheeting makes the wind push harder on the scaffold; it does not make it stronger.","2":"The scaffold relies on its ties; it must be lowered as the building comes down.","3":"The bigger danger is the wind load on the sheeted scaffold, not just dust."},"source_title":"Construction: Structural stability during alteration, demolition and dismantling (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/buildings.htm"},{"id":"high-risk-b3-40","topic":"High risk activities","sign":null,"question":"You are working on a demolition job. You think you know a quicker way to take down a wall than the way set out in the plan. What should you do?","options":["Follow the plan and instructions you were given, and raise your idea with the person in charge","Do it your way, but tell your workmates to stand back","Follow the plan only while the supervisor is watching","Do it your way, as it is quicker"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says sub-contractors and site workers must follow the instructions and plans they are given, and make sure their workmates do too. If you have a better idea, tell the person in charge rather than changing the method yourself.","why_not":{"1":"Do not change the method yourself; follow the plan you were given.","2":"You must follow the plan all the time, not only when someone is watching.","3":"Quicker is not safer; follow the plan and tell the person in charge your idea."},"source_title":"Construction: Demolition (HSE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/demolition.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-01","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Your jacket got soaked in the rain. There is a heater in the drying room. How should you dry the jacket?","options":["Hang it up in the drying room, not on the heater","Lay it over the heater so it dries faster","Put it on the heater and turn the heater up high","Roll it up wet and leave it in your locker"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A site should have somewhere to dry wet clothes. But HSE says clothes should not be put straight onto heaters, because they can catch fire. Hang the jacket up in the drying room instead. Rolling it up wet does not dry it at all.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says clothes should not go straight onto heaters, because they can catch fire.","2":"Putting clothes on a heater can start a fire, and turning it up makes this worse.","3":"Rolling it up wet does not dry it at all. Hang it in the drying room."},"source_title":"Provision of welfare facilities during construction work (Construction Information Sheet No 59) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/cis59.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-04","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"A workmate says: \"The law says any load under 25 kg is safe to lift.\" Is he right?","options":["No. The law sets no weight limit, and HSE's guideline weights are not safe limits","Yes. Any load under 25 kg is always safe","Yes, but only for men","No. The legal limit is 20 kg"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The Manual Handling Operations Regulations do not set any weight limits. HSE gives guideline weights to help employers find low-risk lifts, but HSE says these are not 'safe limits'. A lighter load can still hurt you, for example if you twist while lifting it or hold it away from your body. Think about the whole task, not just the weight.","why_not":{"1":"The law sets no weight limit, and HSE says its guideline weights are not safe limits.","2":"The law sets no weight limit for anyone, men or women.","3":"There is no legal limit of 20 kg. The law sets no weight limits at all."},"source_title":"Manual handling at work: A brief guide (INDG143) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg143.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-05","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Your employer has given your gang a trolley to move blocks to the bricklayers. Carrying them by hand would be quicker. What should you do?","options":["Use the trolley, as your employer has set up","Carry them by hand to save time","Use the trolley only when the supervisor is watching","Carry two blocks at a time so there are fewer trips"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law says you must make full and proper use of the safe way of working your employer has set up to cut the risk of injury. The trolley is there to protect your back. Carrying blocks by hand to save time throws that protection away.","why_not":{"1":"You must make full and proper use of the safe way of working your employer has set up.","2":"You must use the trolley all the time, not just when someone is watching.","3":"Carrying two blocks puts more strain on you. Use the trolley your employer has provided."},"source_title":"The Manual Handling Operations Regulations 1992, regulation 5: Duty of employees","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1992/2793/regulation/5"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-06","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Which of these is NOT covered by the COSHH Regulations, because it has its own law?","options":["Asbestos","Cement dust","Fumes from solvent paint","Germs in rat urine that cause Weil's disease"],"correct":0,"explanation":"COSHH covers most substances at work that can harm your health, such as dusts, fumes, chemicals and germs. HSE says COSHH does not cover lead, asbestos or radioactive substances, because each of these has its own regulations. Cement dust, paint fumes and the germs that cause Weil's disease (leptospirosis) are all covered by COSHH.","why_not":{"1":"Cement dust is covered by COSHH. Only lead, asbestos and radioactive substances have their own law.","2":"Paint fumes are covered by COSHH. Only lead, asbestos and radioactive substances have their own law.","3":"Germs at work, like those causing Weil's disease, are covered by COSHH."},"source_title":"What is a 'substance hazardous to health'? - COSHH - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/coshh/basics/substance.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-07","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"The dust extraction hose on your saw has split. What does COSHH say you must do?","options":["Report it to your employer straight away","Tape it up and carry on without telling anyone","Keep using it until the end of the job","Take the hose off and cut without extraction"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Under COSHH you must make full and proper use of the controls you are given, such as dust extraction, and report any fault to your employer straight away. A split hose lets dust escape, so it may not be protecting you. Cutting with no extraction at all is even worse.","why_not":{"1":"COSHH says you must report a fault to your employer straight away, not hide it.","2":"A split hose lets dust out. Report the fault straight away, do not keep using it.","3":"Cutting with no extraction is even worse. Report the fault to your employer straight away."},"source_title":"The Control of Substances Hazardous to Health Regulations 2002, regulation 8: Use of control measures etc.","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2002/2677/regulation/8"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-08","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are clearing out an old building where rats have been living. Why must you cover any cuts on your hands with waterproof plasters?","options":["Germs from rat urine can get into your body through cuts and cause Weil's disease","Plasters stop rats from biting you","Cuts heal faster under plasters, so you lose less work time","It is only to keep blood off the materials"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Weil's disease (a type of leptospirosis) comes from the urine of infected rats. The germs get into your body through cuts and scratches, or through your mouth, throat and eyes. It starts like flu and can become very serious. Cover cuts, wear gloves, wash your hands often, and eat and drink away from the work area.","why_not":{"1":"Plasters do not stop bites. They stop germs from rat urine getting in through cuts.","2":"The reason is not healing time. Cuts let in germs from rat urine that cause Weil's disease.","3":"The reason is not keeping blood off things. Germs from rat urine can get in through cuts."},"source_title":"Harmful Micro-Organisms: Leptospirosis / Weil's Disease from rats - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/hazardous-substances/harmful-micro-organisms/leptospirosis-weils-disease.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-09","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are cutting paving slabs with a cut-off saw that has a water attachment. How should you use the water?","options":["Keep enough water on the blade for the whole time you are cutting","Wet the ground first, then cut dry","Only turn the water on for long cuts","Turn the water off once the dust cloud gets smaller"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Water stops silica dust getting into the air, but only when it is used properly: enough water for the whole time the work is being done. Just wetting the ground before you cut does not work. Even with water you still need the right mask, because water does not catch all of the dust.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says just wetting the ground before cutting does not work. Water is needed the whole time.","2":"Water must be used for the whole time you cut, short cuts as well as long ones.","3":"Water must stay on for the whole time you cut, or dust gets into the air again."},"source_title":"Dust (construction FAQ) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-dust.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-11","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You will use a cut-off saw on concrete blocks, with water on the blade. You also need a mask. Which type of disposable mask does HSE advise for this work?","options":["FFP3","FFP1","A plain dust mask with no FFP rating","A cloth face covering"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Cutting concrete makes silica dust, and water does not catch all of it. HSE names FFP2 and FFP3 as the two types to ask for with construction dust, and says FFP3 is the best one for work that makes a lot of dust or involves silica or wood dust. A tight-fitting mask also needs a face-fit test, and you must be clean-shaven for it to seal.","why_not":{"1":"HSE names FFP2 and FFP3 for construction dust, and FFP3 is best for silica. FFP1 is weaker.","2":"HSE says ask for FFP2 or FFP3 masks, and FFP3 for silica. An unrated mask is neither.","3":"A cloth face covering is not a dust mask. HSE advises an FFP3 mask for silica dust."},"source_title":"Dust (construction FAQ) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-dust.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-13","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are in a hearing protection zone near a breaker. You lift one ear defender off for a few minutes to talk to a workmate. What does HSE say about this?","options":["Taking it off even for a short time greatly cuts the protection you get","A few minutes without it does not matter","It is fine as long as your other ear is covered","It only matters if the breaker runs all day"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Wear hearing protection the whole time you are doing noisy work or are in a hearing protection zone. HSE says taking it off even for a short while really reduces the overall protection you get, so your hearing can still be damaged.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says taking it off even for a short while really cuts the protection you get.","2":"One bare ear still gets damaged. Keep hearing protection on the whole time in the zone.","3":"Even a short break from protection cuts the overall protection you get, however long the breaker runs."},"source_title":"Noise: Don't lose your hearing! (INDG363) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg363.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-15","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Under the Control of Vibration at Work Regulations 2005, what is the daily exposure limit value for hand-arm vibration?","options":["5 m/s² A(8)","2.5 m/s² A(8)","1.15 m/s² A(8)","10 m/s² A(8)"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The daily exposure limit value for hand-arm vibration is 5 m/s² A(8), averaged over an 8-hour day. Your employer must make sure you are not exposed above it. 2.5 m/s² A(8) is the exposure action value: if it is likely to be reached, your employer must cut exposure as low as reasonably practicable. 1.15 m/s² A(8) is the limit for whole-body vibration, not hand-arm.","why_not":{"1":"2.5 m/s² A(8) is the exposure action value, not the limit value.","2":"1.15 m/s² A(8) is the limit for whole-body vibration, not hand-arm vibration.","3":"10 m/s² A(8) is not in the Regulations. The hand-arm limit is 5 m/s² A(8)."},"source_title":"The Control of Vibration at Work Regulations 2005, regulation 4: Exposure limit values and action values","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2005/1093/regulation/4"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-17","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"After weeks of using a breaker, your fingers often tingle and go numb. What should you do?","options":["Report it straight away to your employer or the person who does your health checks","Wait and see if it goes away on its own","Wear thicker gloves and say nothing","Keep quiet so you are not moved off the job"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Tingling and numbness can be early signs of hand-arm vibration syndrome (HAVS). HSE says report problems with your hands promptly to your employer or the person who does your health checks. Once HAVS damage is done it is permanent, so finding it early matters. Thick gloves keep hands warm but do not stop the vibration.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says report hand problems promptly. HAVS damage is permanent, so waiting can make it worse.","2":"Thick gloves do not stop vibration. Report the problem to your employer straight away.","3":"Keeping quiet lets the damage get worse. HSE says report hand problems promptly."},"source_title":"Hand-arm vibration: A guide for employees (INDG296) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg296.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-18","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Can water on its own harm the skin on your hands at work?","options":["Yes. Having wet hands for a long time, such as over 2 hours in a shift, can cause dermatitis","No. Only chemicals can harm your skin","No. Water is always good for your skin","Only if the water is very hot"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A lot of contact with water, especially with soap, can cause dermatitis. HSE gives examples: having wet hands for more than 2 hours in a shift, or more than 20 hand washes. Wear waterproof gloves for wet work, dry your hands well and use moisturising cream.","why_not":{"1":"Water can harm skin too. Wet hands for over 2 hours a shift can cause dermatitis.","2":"Lots of contact with water, especially with soap, can cause dermatitis.","3":"It does not need to be hot. Having wet hands for a long time can cause dermatitis."},"source_title":"What are work-related skin diseases? - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/skin/employ/whatare.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-19","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Since you started mixing mortar, the skin on your hands has become dry, itchy and red. What should you do?","options":["Report it to your employer","Ignore it, because it is normal for builders","Scrub your hands with a strong solvent to get them cleaner","Stop wearing gloves so your skin can breathe"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Dry, itchy, red skin can be an early sign of dermatitis. If nothing is done it can turn into flaking, cracks, swelling and blisters. HSE says report it to your employer, who may send you to an occupational health doctor or nurse. Do not clean your skin with solvents, and keep wearing the right gloves.","why_not":{"1":"Dry, itchy, red skin can be early dermatitis. HSE says report it to your employer.","2":"HSE says do not use solvents to clean your skin. Report the rash instead.","3":"Keep wearing the right gloves. Taking them off lets more mortar reach your skin."},"source_title":"Skin checks for dermatitis (free poster) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/skin/assets/docs/skindermatitis.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-20","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You work outside all summer and your skin goes brown. What does HSE say a tan means?","options":["Your skin has been damaged by the sun's UV rays","Your skin is now safe from the sun","You do not need to cover up any more","It only matters if your skin blisters"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says a tan is a sign that your skin has been damaged by ultraviolet (UV) rays in sunlight. Even mild redness is a sign of damage, and too much sun raises the risk of skin cancer. Keep your top on, wear a hat that covers your ears and neck, use high-factor sunscreen, take breaks in the shade and check your skin for moles that change.","why_not":{"1":"A tan does not make you safe. HSE says it is a sign your skin is already damaged.","2":"You still need to cover up. A tan is a sign of sun damage, not protection.","3":"Even a tan or mild redness is a sign of damage, so it matters before any blisters."},"source_title":"Skin at work: Outdoor workers and sun exposure - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/skin/sunprotect.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-21","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"A workmate tells the supervisor he has a drinking problem and asks for help. What does HSE say a good drug and alcohol policy should aim to do?","options":["Help and support him, rather than lead to him being sacked","Sack him straight away","Tell the rest of the gang about his problem","Ignore it, because it is his private life"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says that when a worker tells the employer about a drug or alcohol problem, a good policy should aim to help and support them, not lead to dismissal. If his work is safety-critical, such as driving or using machinery, he may need to move to other work for a while. The policy should also say clearly when disciplinary action will be taken.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says a good policy should aim to help and support him, not lead to him being sacked.","2":"Telling the gang does not help him. HSE says the aim is to help and support him.","3":"It is not just private. HSE says a good policy should aim to help and support him."},"source_title":"Managing drug and alcohol misuse at work: Develop a policy - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/alcoholdrugs/develop-policy.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-22","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Your company tests for drugs and alcohol. Your employer has good reasons to test you, but you refuse. What does HSE say?","options":["You cannot be forced to take the test, but you may face disciplinary action","You can be made to take the test by force","Nothing can happen to you if you refuse","Only the police can ask you to take a test at work"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says workers must agree (consent) to a drug or alcohol test, so you cannot be made to take one. But if you refuse when your employer has good grounds for testing, you may face disciplinary action. Testing is most likely in safety-critical jobs, such as driving or operating machines.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says you must agree to a test. Nobody can make you take one.","2":"If your employer has good reasons to test, refusing may lead to disciplinary action.","3":"Employers can test with your agreement. It is not only the police who can ask."},"source_title":"Managing drug and alcohol misuse at work: Screening and testing - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/alcoholdrugs/screening-testing-drugs-alcohol.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-23","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"For weeks you have been sleeping badly, feeling nervous and finding it hard to concentrate at work. What does HSE advise?","options":["Talk to someone, such as your manager, as soon as you can","Keep it to yourself, because stress is a sign of weakness","Have a few drinks after work to cope","Wait until it gets really bad before saying anything"],"correct":0,"explanation":"These can be signs of stress. HSE says it is important to talk to someone, such as your manager, as soon as possible, so they can help and stop things getting worse. If the problem is your manager, you can talk to a union or employee representative, HR, a counselling service or your GP. Stress is not a weakness and can happen to anyone.","why_not":{"1":"Stress is not a weakness. HSE says talk to someone, such as your manager, as soon as you can.","2":"Drinking does not fix stress. HSE says talk to someone, such as your manager, as soon as possible.","3":"Waiting lets things get worse. HSE says talk to someone as soon as possible."},"source_title":"Help for employees on stress at work - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/stress/help-employee.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-24","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You have worked long shifts all week and you are very tired. How can this tiredness (fatigue) affect you at work?","options":["You react more slowly and may not see how risky something is","It makes you more careful, so you are safer","It only matters for people who work in offices","It only matters if you drive on public roads"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Fatigue means your body and mind work less well because of long hours, lack of sleep or badly planned shifts. HSE says it causes slower reactions, memory lapses, poor attention and underestimating risk, which can lead to mistakes and accidents. It is often a root cause of major accidents.","why_not":{"1":"Tiredness does not make you safer. It slows your reactions and makes you misjudge risk.","2":"Fatigue affects people in any job, including site work, not just office workers.","3":"Fatigue matters in all work, not just road driving. It can cause mistakes and accidents on site."},"source_title":"Fatigue - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/humanfactors/topics/fatigue.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b1-25","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"A worker is happy to do extra night shifts on top of his normal hours. Who has the legal duty to manage the risk of him becoming dangerously tired?","options":["His employer, even though he wants the extra hours","Only the worker, because he chose to do it","Nobody, as long as he is paid overtime","The client who owns the site"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says the legal duty to manage the risks from fatigue is on the employer, whatever the worker's own wish to work extra hours. Keeping within the Working Time Regulations is not enough on its own. The employer should set limits on hours, overtime and shift-swapping. Night workers are at extra risk, because day sleep is often lighter and shorter.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says the legal duty is on the employer, even if the worker wants extra hours.","2":"Overtime pay does not remove the duty. The employer must manage the risk of fatigue.","3":"HSE puts the legal duty on his employer, not the client."},"source_title":"Fatigue - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/humanfactors/topics/fatigue.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-01","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"A woman working on your site is pregnant. Under CDM 2015, what must the rest facilities include, where it is needed?","options":["A suitable place where she can lie down to rest","Nothing extra. She uses the same chairs as everyone else","A space in her own car where she can rest","Nothing. She must go home if she needs to rest"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The welfare rules in CDM 2015 say rest rooms or rest areas must, where necessary, have suitable facilities for a pregnant woman or a nursing mother to rest lying down. Sending her to her car or home does not meet this.","why_not":{"1":"Ordinary chairs are not enough. Where needed, she must have a suitable place to rest lying down.","2":"A car is not a rest facility. CDM 2015 says she needs a suitable place to lie down.","3":"She should not have to go home. The site must give her somewhere to rest lying down."},"source_title":"The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, Schedule 2: Minimum welfare facilities required for construction sites","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/schedule/2"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-03","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You have picked up a box of tiles from the floor. You need to put it on a bench to your side. How should you turn?","options":["Move your feet to turn your whole body","Keep your feet still and twist at the waist","Lean over sideways and drop it on the bench","Twist quickly so you hold the weight for less time"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says you should avoid twisting your back or leaning sideways, especially while your back is bent. Keep your shoulders facing the same way as your hips. Turning by moving your feet is better than twisting while you lift.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says avoid twisting your back. Move your feet to turn instead.","2":"HSE says avoid leaning sideways, especially with your back bent.","3":"Twisting fast still strains your back. Turn by moving your feet."},"source_title":"Manual handling at work: Good handling technique - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/msd/manual-handling/good-handling-technique.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-04","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are carrying a heavy slab that has to sit in an exact spot. What does HSE say is the safe way to get it in place?","options":["Put it down first, then slide it into the right position","Hold it up and shuffle it about until it is in the right spot","Drop it from waist height and kick it into place","Hold it out at arm's length so you can see the spot better"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE's advice is: put down, then adjust. If a load must go in an exact place, put it down first and then slide it into position. Holding a heavy load while you move it about puts extra strain on your back.","why_not":{"1":"Holding a heavy load while you move it about strains your back. Put it down, then adjust.","2":"Dropping and kicking it is not safe. HSE says put it down, then slide it into place.","3":"Holding a load at arm's length adds strain. Put it down first, then slide it into place."},"source_title":"Manual handling at work: Good handling technique - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/msd/manual-handling/good-handling-technique.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-06","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Two cleaning products do the same job. One is much less harmful to health than the other. What does COSHH say the employer should prefer to do?","options":["Use the less harmful product, if reasonably practicable","Use the stronger product and give everyone masks","Let each worker pick whichever they like","Use whichever product is cheaper"],"correct":0,"explanation":"COSHH says the first choice is to stop people being exposed. Swapping a harmful substance for one that is safer, called substitution, is the preferred way to do this where reasonably practicable. Masks and other PPE come last, not first.","why_not":{"1":"COSHH prefers swapping to a safer product. Masks come last, not first.","2":"COSHH says the employer should prefer the less harmful product, not leave it to each worker.","3":"Price is not the rule. COSHH prefers the less harmful product where reasonably practicable."},"source_title":"The Control of Substances Hazardous to Health Regulations 2002, regulation 7: Prevention or control of exposure","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2002/2677/regulation/7"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-07","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Because of the substances you work with, your employer tells you to go for health checks (health surveillance). What does COSHH say about this?","options":["You must go. It is in your working hours and your employer pays","It is up to you. You can say no","You must go, but in your own time","You must go, and you pay for it yourself"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Under COSHH, an employee who needs health surveillance must go when the employer asks. It happens during working hours and the employer pays. These checks can find early signs of harm before it gets worse.","why_not":{"1":"COSHH says you must go when your employer asks. It is not up to you.","2":"The checks happen in your working hours, not your own time.","3":"Your employer pays for the checks, not you."},"source_title":"The Control of Substances Hazardous to Health Regulations 2002, regulation 11: Health surveillance","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2002/2677/regulation/11"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-08","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Your gang wants to run a petrol generator inside a basement to power some lights. Why does HSE say this is so dangerous?","options":["In just a few minutes it can make enough carbon monoxide to kill","It is only a danger if you can smell the fumes","It is safe for the first hour, then the fumes build up","The only risk is that it is too noisy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Petrol engines make carbon monoxide, a poisonous gas you cannot see, smell or taste. HSE says using petrol equipment like generators or cut-off saws indoors, in enclosed spaces or in trenches can make dangerous levels even after a few minutes. Keep generators outside where the fumes can blow away.","why_not":{"1":"Carbon monoxide has no smell, so you cannot rely on smelling it.","2":"It is not safe for an hour. HSE says even a few minutes can make deadly levels.","3":"Noise is not the only risk. The engine makes carbon monoxide, which can kill."},"source_title":"Carbon monoxide - Construction - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/hazardous-substances/carbon-monoxide.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-09","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are drilling into concrete. You cannot see any dust in the air. Does that mean there is no silica dust to breathe in?","options":["No. The finest silica dust is too small to see in normal light","Yes. If you cannot see dust, the air is clean","Yes, as long as the drill is new","No, but fine dust cannot get into your lungs"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says the fine silica dust that gets deep into your lungs, called respirable crystalline silica, is too fine to see with normal lighting. So clear-looking air is not proof that it is safe. Keep using the dust controls.","why_not":{"1":"Clear-looking air is not proof. The finest silica dust is too small to see in normal light.","2":"A new drill still makes silica dust, and the finest dust is too small to see.","3":"This fine silica dust is the kind that gets deep into your lungs."},"source_title":"Cancer and construction: Silica - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/cancer-and-construction/silica-dust.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-10","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are cutting blocks with a cut-off saw outside in the open air. A workmate says you do not need any dust control outdoors. What does HSE say?","options":["Do not assume dust levels are low outside when using powerful tools","He is right. Outdoors the wind always blows the dust away","He is right, as long as you stand upwind","Dust control is only needed in rooms with no windows"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Dust builds up more in enclosed spaces, but HSE warns not to assume dust levels will be low when working outside with high-energy tools such as cut-off saws. These tools make a lot of dust very quickly, so you still need controls like water or extraction.","why_not":{"1":"HSE warns not to assume dust levels are low outside when using powerful tools.","2":"Standing upwind is not enough. Powerful tools make a lot of dust fast, so use controls.","3":"Dust control is needed outdoors too when using high-energy tools like cut-off saws."},"source_title":"Construction dust (Construction Information Sheet No 36) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/cis36.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-11","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"The waste bag in the dust extraction unit on your tool is full. What should you do with it?","options":["Seal it and put it in the right waste container","Empty it into a skip and put the same bag back","Shake it out outside so it can be used again","Leave it in the unit until the end of the job"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says to empty the extraction unit regularly, using the correct disposable waste bags. Seal the bag and put it in the right waste container. Do not empty the bags to use them again, because that puts the dust back into the air.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says do not empty the bags to use them again. Seal it and bin it properly.","2":"Shaking it out puts the dust back in the air. HSE says do not reuse these bags.","3":"HSE says empty the unit regularly. A full bag should be sealed and put in the right container."},"source_title":"Controlling construction dust with on-tool extraction (Construction Information Sheet No 69) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/cis69.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-12","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"In a noisy area, a workmate wears earplugs and very strong ear defenders on top, so he can hear almost nothing. What is the problem with this?","options":["Too much protection can stop him hearing warnings and talking to others","There is no problem. More protection is always better","The only problem is that it looks silly","It is only a problem if he is the supervisor"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says hearing protection should not cut the noise at the ear to below 70 dB. Too much protection can make it hard to talk and to hear warning signals, so people can become cut off from what is happening around them. They may also take it off and risk their hearing.","why_not":{"1":"More is not always better. Too much protection can stop him hearing warnings.","2":"The problem is safety, not looks. He may not hear warnings or talk to others.","3":"Over-protection is a problem for anyone, because they may not hear warning signals."},"source_title":"Hearing protection - Noise - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/noise/hearingprotection.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-13","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Loud noise at work has damaged a worker's hearing. What does HSE say about this damage?","options":["It is permanent. There is no cure, and hearing aids cannot fully make up for it","It will come back after a few weeks away from noise","A hearing aid will make his hearing as good as new","It can be cured with ear drops"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says once your hearing has gone, it will not come back and there is no cure. Hearing aids cannot fully replace natural hearing. This is why you must protect your hearing before damage happens.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says once your hearing has gone, it will not come back.","2":"HSE says hearing aids cannot fully make up for lost natural hearing.","3":"HSE says there is no cure for hearing lost to noise."},"source_title":"Noise - Construction - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/physical-ill-health-risks/noise.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-14","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You often use a hammer-action breaker for about 2 hours a day. Using HSE's simple guide, how much risk from hand-arm vibration are you likely to be at?","options":["High risk, likely above the exposure limit value","Low risk, because 2 hours is less than half a shift","No risk, as long as you wear thick gloves","Medium risk, because hammer tools only become high risk after 4 hours"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE's simple guide says people are at high risk, above the exposure limit value, if they regularly use hammer-action tools for more than about an hour a day. Medium risk starts at about 15 minutes a day. The 4-hour figure is for some rotary and other tools, not hammer tools.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says over about an hour a day with hammer tools is high risk, not low.","2":"Gloves do not protect you from vibration, so they do not lower this risk.","3":"The 4-hour figure is for some rotary tools. Hammer tools are high risk after about an hour."},"source_title":"Vibration - Construction - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/physical-ill-health-risks/vibration.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-15","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"The disc on your cutter is worn and blunt, but it still cuts slowly. Why should you change it?","options":["Blunt tools vibrate more and make the job take longer, so you get more vibration","A blunt disc vibrates less, so it is actually safer","It does not matter as long as the cutter still works","Only because a new disc looks more professional"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says blunt, damaged or inefficient tools give off more vibration. They also make the job take longer, so you are exposed for longer. Keeping cutting tools sharp and in good repair lowers the risk.","why_not":{"1":"It is the other way round. HSE says blunt tools vibrate more, not less.","2":"A blunt disc vibrates more and takes longer, so you get more vibration.","3":"It is not about looks. Blunt tools give more vibration and take longer."},"source_title":"Vibration - Construction - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/physical-ill-health-risks/vibration.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-17","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"A workmate says: \"Any thick rubber gloves will protect you from any chemical for as long as you like.\" What does HSE say?","options":["No glove protects against every substance, and no glove protects against a substance for ever","He is right. Thick gloves stop all chemicals","He is right, as long as the gloves are new each week","Gloves are only needed for chemicals that smell strong"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says gloves differ in material and thickness. No glove material protects against all substances, and no glove protects against a particular substance for ever. The right glove depends on the substance, and gloves must be changed in time.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says no glove material protects against every substance.","2":"New gloves each week is not the rule. No glove protects against a substance for ever.","3":"Smell is not the test. The right glove depends on the substance, and none lasts for ever."},"source_title":"Choosing the right gloves to protect skin: a guide for employers - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/skin/employ/gloves.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-18","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Your hands get dry from your work. When does HSE say it is especially good to use moisturising cream?","options":["At the end of the working day","Only once a month","Only when your skin is already cracked and bleeding","Never, because cream makes skin weaker"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE advises moisturising as often as possible, and especially at the end of the day. It puts back the natural oils that keep your skin's protective barrier working. Do not wait until the skin cracks.","why_not":{"1":"Once a month is far too little. HSE says moisturise as often as possible.","2":"Do not wait for cracks. Moisturising often helps keep your skin's barrier working.","3":"HSE says moisturising helps, because it puts back the natural oils that protect your skin."},"source_title":"How do I prevent skin problems in my business? - Skin at work - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/skin/employ/prevention.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-19","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You work outside a lot. You notice a mole on your arm that is getting bigger and has started to itch. What does HSE advise?","options":["See a doctor promptly","Wait a year and see if it goes away","Cover it with a plaster and forget about it","Get more sun on it so it dries up"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Too much sun can lead to skin cancer. HSE says to check your skin regularly for unusual moles or spots. See a doctor promptly if anything is changing in shape, size or colour, or is itching or bleeding.","why_not":{"1":"Do not wait. HSE says see a doctor promptly if a mole is changing or itching.","2":"Covering it does not help. HSE says see a doctor promptly about a changing mole.","3":"More sun makes it worse. Too much sun can lead to skin cancer."},"source_title":"Skin at work: Outdoor workers and sun exposure - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/skin/sunprotect.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-20","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"What does HSE include when it talks about drug and alcohol misuse?","options":["Illegal drugs, and also misusing alcohol, medicines and substances such as solvents","Only illegal drugs","Only being drunk at work","Only drugs that are bought on the street"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says drug and alcohol misuse means using illegal drugs and also misusing alcohol, medicines and substances such as solvents. So misusing medicines, or sniffing solvents, counts too, not just illegal drugs.","why_not":{"1":"It is not only illegal drugs. It also covers misusing alcohol, medicines and solvents.","2":"It is not only being drunk. It also covers drugs, medicines and solvents.","3":"It is not only street drugs. Misusing medicines or solvents counts too."},"source_title":"Managing drug and alcohol misuse at work: Overview - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/alcoholdrugs/index.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-21","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"A workmate has had several near misses lately and keeps missing work without saying why. HSE says these can be warning signs of drug or alcohol misuse. What else does HSE say?","options":["They can also be signs of other things, like stress or illness","They always prove that he is taking drugs","They only matter if he is a driver","They are normal, so there is nothing to think about"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE lists things like unexplained absences and more accidents or near misses as possible warning signs of drug or alcohol misuse. But it also says these can be signs of other things, like stress or illness. So do not jump to conclusions, but do not ignore them either.","why_not":{"1":"They do not prove anything. HSE says they can also be signs of stress or illness.","2":"These signs matter in any job, not just for drivers.","3":"HSE lists them as warning signs, so they should not be ignored."},"source_title":"Managing drug and alcohol misuse at work: Overview - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/alcoholdrugs/index.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-22","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"What does HSE say about stress?","options":["It is not an illness, but it can make you ill","It is an illness that only affects weak people","It cannot affect your body, only your mood","It is never caused by work"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says stress is not an illness, but it can make you ill. It can cause symptoms that affect how you feel in your body and in your mind. Employers have a legal duty to assess the risk of work-related stress and act on it.","why_not":{"1":"Stress is not an illness, and it is not about being weak.","2":"HSE says stress can affect how you feel in your body as well as your mind.","3":"Work can cause stress. Employers must assess the risk of work-related stress."},"source_title":"Work-related stress and how to manage it: Overview - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/stress/overview.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b2-25","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Your shift finishes at 10pm. Under the Working Time Regulations, an adult worker is entitled to a rest of at least how long before starting work again?","options":["11 hours in a row, so not before 9am","6 hours, so from 4am","8 hours, so from 6am","There is no minimum rest in the law"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The Working Time Regulations give an adult worker the right to at least 11 hours' rest in a row in each 24-hour period. Finishing at 10pm means the rest lasts until at least 9am. Young workers are entitled to 12 hours. The law has some special cases, but this is the normal rule. Proper rest between shifts helps stop dangerous tiredness.","why_not":{"1":"6 hours is too short. The law gives at least 11 hours' rest in a row.","2":"8 hours is too short. The law gives at least 11 hours' rest in a row.","3":"There is a minimum. Adult workers are entitled to at least 11 hours' rest in a row."},"source_title":"The Working Time Regulations 1998, regulation 10: Daily rest","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/1833/regulation/10"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-03","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"The welfare cabin is heated by a gas (LPG) heater. A workmate has stuffed rags into the air vents to stop the draught. What should you do?","options":["Leave the rags in, as a warm cabin is safer in winter","Pull the rags out. Blocked vents can let deadly carbon monoxide build up","Turn the heater up higher to make up for the draught","Open the cabin door once an hour and leave the vents blocked"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says gas heaters and cookers without enough air can make carbon monoxide, which can kill. The fixed vents must be kept clear, even in cold weather when people block them with rags or newspaper. Take the rags out and tell your supervisor. Opening the door now and then is not the same as proper ventilation.","why_not":{"0":"Blocked vents can let carbon monoxide build up, which can kill. Warmth is not worth that.","2":"Turning the heater up with blocked vents can make more carbon monoxide.","3":"Opening the door now and then is not proper ventilation. The vents must stay clear."},"source_title":"Provision of welfare facilities during construction work (Construction Information Sheet No 59) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/cis59.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-05","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You have picked up a box of fixings and have a good hold on it. Where should you look as you carry it?","options":["Down at the box, so you can see it is still level","At your feet the whole time","Over your shoulder, to check behind you","Ahead of you, with your head up"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says keep your head up when you handle a load. Once you have a secure hold, look ahead, not down at the load. Looking down bends your neck and back, and you cannot see where you are going.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says look ahead, not down at the load, once you have a secure hold.","1":"Looking down bends your neck and back. Keep your head up and look ahead.","2":"Looking over your shoulder twists you. Keep your head up and look ahead."},"source_title":"Manual handling at work: Good handling technique - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/msd/manual-handling/good-handling-technique.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-07","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Pallets of blocks and bags of mortar have been left out in the rain without a cover. Why does HSE say materials should be kept dry?","options":["Wet materials can get heavier and slippery to handle","Wet materials are lighter, so people carry too many at once","It only matters for the look of the finished wall","Rain makes no difference to manual handling"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says keep materials dry so they do not gain weight or become slippery when you handle them. Soaked blocks and bags weigh more than you expect, and a wet surface is harder to grip, so you are more likely to strain yourself or drop the load.","why_not":{"1":"It is the other way round. Wet materials get heavier, not lighter.","2":"It matters for your body too. Wet materials get heavier and slippery to handle.","3":"Rain does make a difference. Wet materials gain weight and are harder to grip."},"source_title":"Construction physical ill health risks: Manual handling - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/physical-ill-health-risks/manual-handling.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-09","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Your overalls are covered in a hazardous product you have been using. It is lunch time. What does COSHH say should happen to them?","options":["Keep them on in the canteen, then take them off at home time","Brush them down outside the canteen, then go in","Take them home and wash them with your family's washing","Take them off when you leave the work area and keep them away from clean clothes"],"correct":3,"explanation":"COSHH says protective clothing that may be contaminated must be taken off when you leave the working area and kept apart from clean clothing. Wearing it into the canteen or taking it home spreads the substance to places where you eat and to other people. Your employer must arrange for it to be cleaned, or thrown away if needed.","why_not":{"0":"Dirty overalls must come off when you leave the work area, not be worn in the canteen.","1":"Brushing down is not enough. COSHH says take them off when you leave the work area.","2":"Taking them home spreads the substance to your family. Keep them away from clean clothes."},"source_title":"The Control of Substances Hazardous to Health Regulations 2002, regulation 9","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2002/2677/regulation/9"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-10","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"A new floor sealer has come with a safety data sheet. A workmate says: \"That sheet is the risk assessment, so nothing else needs doing.\" What does HSE say?","options":["He is right. The safety data sheet is the risk assessment","No. The safety data sheet is not a risk assessment, but it helps make one","Safety data sheets are only for the fire service","Safety data sheets are only needed for products sold to the public"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says a safety data sheet is not a risk assessment. It tells you about the product's hazards, how to handle and store it, and what to do in an emergency. That information is used to help make the risk assessment for the actual job, which looks at how, where and for how long the product will be used.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says a safety data sheet is not a risk assessment.","2":"Safety data sheets are for the people using the product, to help make the risk assessment.","3":"Safety data sheets come with products used at work. They help you make the risk assessment."},"source_title":"Chemical safety data sheets - COSHH - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/coshh/basics/datasheets.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-11","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are checking the label on a tub of chemicals for hazard symbols. What do the GB CLP hazard pictograms look like?","options":["A diamond with a red border and a white background","A yellow triangle with a black border","A blue circle with a white picture","A green square with a white picture"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says the GB CLP hazard pictograms are diamond shaped, with a red border and a white background, and a black symbol inside. They warn you that a chemical could harm people or the environment. A label can show more than one.","why_not":{"1":"CLP hazard pictograms are diamonds with a red border and white background, not yellow triangles.","2":"CLP hazard pictograms are diamonds with a red border and white background, not blue circles.","3":"CLP hazard pictograms are diamonds with a red border and white background, not green squares."},"source_title":"Hazard pictograms (symbols) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/chemical-classification/labelling-packaging/hazard-symbols-hazard-pictograms.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-12","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are spraying a solvent-based paint. A workmate gives you a dust mask with a particle filter. What does HSE say about this?","options":["It is fine, because any mask stops paint fumes","It is fine if you only spray for a short time","A particle filter stops spray mist but not solvent vapour. You need the right gas or vapour filter","It is fine if you hold your breath while spraying"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says particle filters protect against spray mist but do not protect you from solvent vapours. For vapour you need the right gas or vapour filter, and the filter must be changed at suitable intervals. A tight-fitting mask also needs to be fit tested.","why_not":{"0":"Not every mask stops fumes. A particle filter does not stop solvent vapour.","1":"A short time does not help. A particle filter still lets solvent vapour through.","3":"Holding your breath is not protection. You need the right gas or vapour filter."},"source_title":"Construction hazardous substances: Solvents - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/hazardous-substances/solvents.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-13","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You have been using a strong solvent-based glue in a small room with the door shut. You start to feel dizzy and have a headache. What is the most likely cause, and why does it matter?","options":["You are hungry, so it is nothing to do with the work","Breathing the solvent vapour. It can harm you and make an accident more likely","The smell is unpleasant but cannot affect your health","Tiredness from the morning, so you should carry on until lunch"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says even lower levels of solvents can cause headaches, sickness, dizziness or light-headedness, and some of these effects can make an accident more likely. Stop, get some fresh air and tell your supervisor. HSE says ventilation is always needed with solvents, such as open doors and windows, and more ventilation for higher-risk work.","why_not":{"0":"Dizziness and headache are known effects of breathing solvent vapour, so it is likely the work.","2":"HSE says solvent vapour can cause headaches and dizziness, so it can affect your health.","3":"Do not carry on. Solvent effects can make an accident more likely, so get fresh air."},"source_title":"Construction hazardous substances: Solvents - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/hazardous-substances/solvents.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-14","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You only cut a few blocks now and then during the day, a minute or so each time. Does HSE say this short dusty work is safe?","options":["Yes. Dust only harms you if you cut for hours without a break","Yes, as long as each cut takes less than five minutes","Yes, if you cough the dust out afterwards","No. Lots of quick dusty jobs still add up and can harm you over time"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says construction work often involves many quick tasks through the day, and these can still make a lot of dust. Over time this dust can harm your lungs. Control the dust every time, so the total you breathe over the years does not build up.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says quick dusty tasks through the day can still harm you over time.","1":"Short cuts still make a lot of dust, and it adds up over time.","2":"Coughing does not clear the fine dust. Control the dust every time."},"source_title":"Dust (construction FAQ) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-dust.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-17","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are about to chase a brick wall with a wall chaser that has on-tool extraction. What type of extraction unit (vacuum) does HSE say you should use?","options":["An L (Low) class unit","A household vacuum cleaner","An H (High) or M (Medium) class unit","Any vacuum, as long as it is switched on"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says choose an H (High) or M (Medium) class extraction unit for construction dust. An L (Low) class unit is only suitable for lower-toxicity dusts like the gypsum in plasterboard. Brick dust contains silica, so an L class or household vacuum is not good enough.","why_not":{"0":"An L class unit is only for lower-toxicity dust like plasterboard. Brick dust needs H or M class.","1":"A household vacuum is not made for construction dust. HSE says use an H or M class unit.","3":"Not any vacuum will do. HSE says choose an H or M class unit for construction dust."},"source_title":"Controlling construction dust with on-tool extraction (Construction Information Sheet No 69) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/cis69.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-18","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You passed a face-fit test for your tight-fitting mask last year. Since then you have lost a lot of weight. What does HSE say?","options":["You should have the fit test done again","Nothing changes. A fit test lasts for life","Just pull the straps tighter","Only a new job needs a new fit test"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says a fit test should be done again whenever something changes that could alter how the mask fits your face. Weight loss or gain is one example. Others are major dental work, facial piercings, and changes to other headwear you wear with the mask. Tightening the straps does not make up for a poor fit.","why_not":{"1":"A fit test does not last for life. Weight loss or gain means it should be done again.","2":"Tighter straps do not fix a poor fit. You need a new fit test.","3":"Changes to you, like weight loss, also mean you need a new fit test."},"source_title":"Guidance on respiratory protective equipment (RPE) fit testing (INDG479) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg479.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-19","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"After a day near a breaker, your hearing sounds muffled. By the next morning it seems normal again. What does HSE say this means?","options":["Nothing. If it comes back by morning, there is no problem","It means your ears have got used to the noise","It only matters if it happens every day for a year","It is a sign that something should be done about the noise"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says muffled hearing at the end of the day is a sign that something should be done about the noise, even if your hearing seems better by the next morning. Noise damage builds up slowly and you may not notice until it is too late. Tell your employer about any ear or hearing trouble.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says muffled hearing is a warning sign, even if it is better by morning.","1":"Your ears do not get used to noise. Muffled hearing is a sign of a noise problem.","2":"Do not wait. Noise damage builds up slowly, and muffled hearing is a warning now."},"source_title":"Noise: Don't lose your hearing! (INDG363) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg363.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-20","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"HSE gives simple signs that your employer should be doing something about noise. Which of these is one of them?","options":["You can hear a radio playing in the site cabin","You use noisy powered tools or machinery for over half an hour a day","You can talk normally to someone standing next to you","You work outdoors rather than inside a building"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says your employer would be expected to be doing something about noise if, for example, you use noisy powered tools or machinery for over half an hour a day. Working outdoors does not make noise safe, and being able to talk normally to someone close by is not a warning sign.","why_not":{"0":"Hearing a radio is not one of HSE's warning signs about noise.","2":"Being able to talk normally nearby is not a warning sign. Raising your voice would be.","3":"Working outdoors does not make noise safe, and it is not one of HSE's warning signs."},"source_title":"Noise: Don't lose your hearing! (INDG363) - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg363.pdf"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-22","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Your daily noise exposure is between the lower and upper exposure action values. What does the law require your employer to do about hearing protection?","options":["Give you hearing protectors if you ask for them","Nothing, until the noise is over the upper action value","Make you wear earplugs and ear defenders together","Move you to a different site"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says that when your noise exposure is between the lower and upper exposure action values, your employer must give you hearing protectors if you ask for them. Above the upper action value, your employer must provide them and make sure they are used properly.","why_not":{"1":"Between the two values, your employer must give you protectors if you ask.","2":"The law does not say to wear both together here. You get protectors if you ask.","3":"The law does not say to move you. Between the two values, you get protectors if you ask."},"source_title":"Hearing protection - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/noise/hearingprotection.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-23","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"After years of noisy work, a worker often hears a ringing in his ears, even when it is quiet. What does HSE say about this?","options":["It is a sign that his ears have toughened up","It always goes away after a week off work","It is called tinnitus and it can become permanent","It only happens to people who listen to loud music"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says loud noise can cause a ringing in the ears called tinnitus, which can become permanent. Hearing damage from noise does not get better, and there is no cure. Protect your hearing and tell your employer if you notice ringing or muffled hearing.","why_not":{"0":"Ears do not toughen up. Ringing in the ears is tinnitus, a sign of noise damage.","1":"It does not always go away. HSE says tinnitus can become permanent.","3":"Noise at work can cause tinnitus too, not just loud music."},"source_title":"Construction physical ill health risks: Noise - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/physical-ill-health-risks/noise.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-25","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"HSE gives a rough guide to how long you can use a hammer-action tool, such as a breaker, before you are likely to be above the exposure action value. About how long a day is it?","options":["About 4 hours a day","About 8 hours a day","There is no time guide, only a weight guide","About 15 minutes a day"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says you are particularly at risk if you regularly use hammer-action tools for more than about 15 minutes a day, because you are likely to be above the exposure action value in the regulations. For some rotary tools the guide is about an hour a day. Above the action value, your employer must act to reduce the risk.","why_not":{"0":"4 hours is far too long. For hammer tools, HSE says about 15 minutes a day.","1":"8 hours is far too long. For hammer tools, HSE says about 15 minutes a day.","2":"HSE does give a time guide: about 15 minutes a day for hammer tools."},"source_title":"Worried about your hands? - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/vibration/hav/yourhands.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-28","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You do not hold the power tool yourself. Instead, you press metal parts against a fixed pedestal grinder. Are you at risk from hand-arm vibration?","options":["Yes. Holding a workpiece that vibrates against a machine also puts you at risk","No. Only people holding the tool are at risk","No, because the grinder is bolted down","Only if the grinder is battery powered"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says you are also at risk if you hold workpieces that vibrate while a powered machine, such as a pedestal grinder, works on them. The vibration still passes into your hands and arms, even though you are not holding the tool.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says holding a vibrating workpiece against a machine also puts you at risk.","2":"Being bolted down does not help. The vibration still passes into your hands through the workpiece.","3":"Battery or not makes no difference. Holding a vibrating workpiece puts you at risk."},"source_title":"Worried about your hands? - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/vibration/hav/yourhands.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-29","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are about to put on gloves to work with a product that can harm your skin. What does HSE say you should do first?","options":["Rub some of the product on your hands to test it","Put the gloves on over dirty hands to save time","Wet your hands so the gloves slide on","Wash your hands"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says wash your hands before eating and drinking, and before you put on gloves. Putting gloves on over dirty hands traps any dirt or chemical against your skin, where the gloves hold it in. Dry your hands well afterwards too.","why_not":{"0":"Never put a harmful product on your skin. Wash your hands before putting gloves on.","1":"Gloves over dirty hands trap dirt or chemical against your skin.","2":"Wash and dry your hands well. HSE says wash before putting on gloves."},"source_title":"Skin at work: Work-related skin diseases - Prevention - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/skin/employ/prevention.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-30","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You have gloss paint on your hands at the end of the day. A workmate hands you a rag soaked in thinners to clean it off. What does HSE say?","options":["Use the thinners. It is the quickest way to get paint off skin","Do not use solvents or harsh cleaners to clean your skin","Thinners are fine if you rinse your hands with cold water after","Leave the paint on and let it wear off"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says do not use aggressive cleaners or solvents to clean your skin. Solvents such as thinners strip the skin's natural oils and can cause dermatitis. Wash any contamination off promptly with a suitable skin cleaner and water, then dry your hands thoroughly.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says do not use solvents to clean your skin. They can cause dermatitis.","2":"Rinsing after does not make it safe. HSE says do not use solvents on your skin.","3":"Wash paint off promptly with a suitable skin cleaner and water. Do not leave it on."},"source_title":"Skin at work: Work-related skin diseases - Prevention - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/skin/employ/prevention.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-31","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are working on a roof in summer. What strength of sunscreen does HSE say you should put on any skin that is not covered?","options":["A high factor sunscreen of at least SPF30","Any sunscreen, even SPF5","None, if you already have a tan","Sunscreen is only needed on the beach"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says use a high factor sunscreen of at least SPF30 on any exposed skin. Also keep your top on, wear a hat that covers your ears and neck, and stay in the shade at breaks. A tan does not protect you; it is a sign the skin is already damaged.","why_not":{"1":"SPF5 is too weak. HSE says use at least SPF30.","2":"A tan does not protect you. It is a sign your skin is already damaged.","3":"You need sunscreen at work too. HSE says use SPF30 or higher on exposed skin."},"source_title":"Skin at work: Outdoor workers and sun exposure - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/skin/sunprotect.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-34","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"A telehandler driver asks his employer for help with a drug problem. What does HSE say may be necessary while he gets help?","options":["He keeps driving as normal, so nobody finds out","He must be sacked, because his job is safety-critical","He can keep driving if a workmate watches him","He may need to be moved to other work, at least for a while"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says that where someone in a safety-critical job, such as driving or operating lifting equipment, asks for help with drug or alcohol misuse, it may be necessary to move them to other work, at least for a time. This protects him and everyone around him while he gets support.","why_not":{"0":"Driving as normal could put him and others at risk. He may need other work for a while.","1":"HSE does not say he must be sacked. He may need to move to other work for a while.","2":"Being watched is not enough. HSE says he may need to move to other work for a while."},"source_title":"Managing drug and alcohol misuse at work: Look at safety-critical work - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/alcoholdrugs/look-at-safety-critical-work.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-35","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"A site manager knows that some workers smoke cannabis in the site compound at lunch time. He decides to turn a blind eye. What does the law say?","options":["It is not his problem, because it is their own break time","It is only an offence if he smokes it too","He could be committing a criminal offence by knowingly allowing it","It is allowed on a site, but not in an office"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Under the Misuse of Drugs Act 1971, a person who manages premises commits an offence if they knowingly allow certain drug activities there, including smoking cannabis or supplying drugs. Knowing about it and doing nothing can count as allowing it. He should deal with it and follow the company's drug and alcohol policy.","why_not":{"0":"Break time does not matter. Knowingly allowing it on the site can be an offence for him.","1":"He does not need to smoke it himself. Knowingly allowing it can be an offence.","3":"It is not allowed on any premises. Knowingly allowing it can be an offence."},"source_title":"Misuse of Drugs Act 1971, section 8","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1971/38/section/8"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-37","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"What does HSE say your employer must do about stress at work?","options":["Assess the risks to your health from stress at work and share the results with you","Nothing. Stress is a private matter","Only act if you have a note from your doctor","Give everyone a day off each month"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says your employer has a legal duty to assess the risks to your health from stress at work and to share the results of the risk assessment with you. Many employers use HSE's Management Standards to find and manage the main causes of stress.","why_not":{"1":"Stress at work is not just private. Your employer has a legal duty to assess the risks.","2":"No doctor's note is needed. Your employer must assess the risks of stress at work anyway.","3":"A monthly day off is not the duty. Your employer must assess the risks and share the results."},"source_title":"Help for employees on stress at work - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/stress/help-employee.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-38","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"HSE has looked at when accidents and injuries are more likely to happen at work. Which of these does it say?","options":["They are higher on the first day back after a holiday only","They are lower when shifts are long, because people get into a rhythm","Shift patterns make no difference to accidents","They are higher on night shifts and after a run of shifts in a row"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says accidents and injuries have been found to be more common on night shifts, after a run of shifts in a row, when shifts are long, and when there are not enough breaks. Tiredness builds up, so long or back-to-back shifts need to be planned with rest in mind.","why_not":{"0":"HSE does not say this. It says accidents are higher on night shifts and after runs of shifts.","1":"It is the other way round. HSE says accidents are higher when shifts are long.","2":"Shift patterns do make a difference. Accidents are higher on night shifts and after runs of shifts."},"source_title":"Fatigue - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/humanfactors/topics/fatigue.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-39","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"You are starting a run of night shifts. Why does HSE say night workers are particularly at risk of fatigue?","options":["Night work is always less busy, so people get bored","Sleep in the day is often lighter, shorter and more easily disturbed","It is colder at night, which makes people tired","Night workers always work longer hours than day workers"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says night workers are particularly at risk of fatigue because sleep during the day is often lighter, shorter and more easily disturbed, by daytime noise and by the body's natural reluctance to sleep in daylight. Over time this builds a 'sleep debt'. Plan for proper rest between shifts.","why_not":{"0":"Boredom is not the reason HSE gives. Day sleep is often lighter, shorter and more disturbed.","2":"Cold is not the reason HSE gives. Day sleep is often lighter, shorter and more disturbed.","3":"Longer hours is not the reason HSE gives. Day sleep is often lighter, shorter and more disturbed."},"source_title":"Fatigue - HSE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/humanfactors/topics/fatigue.htm"},{"id":"occupational-health-b3-40","topic":"Occupational health","sign":null,"question":"Under the Working Time Regulations, what is the normal limit on an adult worker's average weekly working time, including overtime?","options":["An average of 48 hours a week, unless you have agreed in writing to work more","An average of 40 hours a week, with no way to work more","An average of 60 hours a week","There is no limit if you are paid overtime"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The Working Time Regulations say a worker's working time, including overtime, must not be more than an average of 48 hours for each 7 days, worked out over a set period. The limit does not apply if your employer has first got your agreement in writing to work more.","why_not":{"1":"The limit is an average of 48 hours, not 40, and you can agree in writing to work more.","2":"60 hours is too high. The limit is an average of 48 hours a week.","3":"Overtime pay does not remove the limit. Overtime counts towards the 48-hour average."},"source_title":"The Working Time Regulations 1998, regulation 4","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/1833/regulation/4"},{"id":"safety-b1-01","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"The risk assessment says to wear safety glasses when drilling. You only have two holes to drill, which will take one minute. What do you do?","options":["Drill without them: the job is too short to matter","Put the safety glasses on before you start","Keep your head back, away from the drill","Put them on only if dust starts to fly"],"correct":1,"explanation":"If the risk assessment says a job needs PPE, you wear it every time, even for a very short job. A chip of brick or metal can hit your eye in less than a second. Keeping your head back, or waiting to see if dust flies, does not protect your eyes.","why_not":{"0":"If the risk assessment says PPE is needed, you wear it every time, even for a one-minute job.","2":"Keeping your head back does not protect your eyes, because a chip can still fly into them.","3":"A chip can hit your eye in under a second, so put the glasses on before you start."},"source_title":"Using personal protective equipment (PPE) to control risks at work: Selection and use","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/ppe/selection-and-use.htm"},{"id":"safety-b1-03","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"The coveralls you have been given are much too big. The sleeves hang down over your hands. What should you do?","options":["Cut the sleeves shorter with a knife","Roll the sleeves up and tape them","Ask for coveralls in your size","Wear them as they are: loose is cooler"],"correct":2,"explanation":"PPE has to fit the person wearing it. Changing it yourself to make it fit is not the answer. Long, loose sleeves can get caught in a turning drill or machine and pull your arm in. Ask for the right size.","why_not":{"0":"Changing PPE yourself to make it fit is not the answer, so ask for the right size.","1":"Rolling and taping is still changing it yourself, so ask for coveralls in your size.","3":"Loose, long sleeves can get caught in a turning machine and pull your arm in."},"source_title":"Using personal protective equipment (PPE) to control risks at work: Selection and use","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/ppe/selection-and-use.htm"},{"id":"safety-b1-05","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"By law, a Sikh worker who wears a turban does not have to wear a hard hat. What about the other PPE his job needs, such as safety boots and hi-vis?","options":["He does not need any other PPE either","He only needs it if he is a visitor","He can choose whether to wear it","He must still wear it"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The exemption is for head protection only. A Sikh who wears a turban must still wear all the other PPE the job needs, such as safety boots, gloves, eye protection and hi-vis. This is the same whether he works on the site or is visiting it.","why_not":{"0":"The exemption is only for head protection, so he must still wear all the other PPE the job needs.","1":"Whether he works there or is visiting, he must still wear the other PPE.","2":"It is not his choice, because only the hard hat is excused and the other PPE must still be worn."},"source_title":"Construction general (FAQs: personal protective equipment)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-ppe.htm"},{"id":"safety-b1-06","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You are about to plug a power tool into a plug-in RCD adaptor. What should you do first?","options":["Nothing: it was tested at the factory","Tape over its button so it cannot switch off by mistake","Press its test button and check that it trips","Take the RCD out and plug the tool straight into the wall socket"],"correct":2,"explanation":"An RCD can save your life by cutting the power very fast when there is a fault. But an RCD can be faulty too. HSE says the person using it should press the test button before use. If it does not trip, do not use it: it should be labelled as faulty or taken away.","why_not":{"0":"An RCD can be faulty too, so HSE says the user should press its test button before each use.","1":"Taping the button stops the RCD doing its job, so press the test button and check it trips.","3":"Taking the RCD out removes the thing that protects you from a shock, so test it instead."},"source_title":"Work using electrically powered equipment","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/electricity/electricequip.htm"},{"id":"safety-b1-07","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Your drill starts to smoke and smell of burning. You switch it off and unplug it. What should happen next?","options":["Take it out of use, label it 'DO NOT USE' and hand it in to be checked","Let it cool down, then carry on","Open it up and look for the fault yourself","Put it back in the store for someone else to use"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Smoke and a burning smell mean the drill is faulty. It could give someone a shock or start a fire. Take it out of use, label it 'DO NOT USE' and keep it away from others until a competent person has checked it. Do not try to repair it yourself.","why_not":{"1":"Cooling down does not fix the fault, and a faulty drill could give someone a shock or start a fire.","2":"Do not try to find or fix the fault yourself, because a competent person must check it.","3":"Putting it back lets someone else use a faulty tool, so label it 'DO NOT USE' and hand it in."},"source_title":"Electrical safety and you: A brief guide (INDG231)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg231.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b1-09","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"HSE says that touching AC electricity can injure you from about what voltage upwards?","options":["About 50 volts","About 230 volts","About 400 volts","Only above 1,000 volts"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Above about 50 volts AC, a shock can harm your breathing and your heart. It can also make you lose control, so you fall from a ladder or touch a moving machine. So normal mains electricity is well above the level that can hurt you.","why_not":{"1":"Normal mains at 230 volts is well above the level that can hurt you, which starts at about 50.","2":"Harm can start far lower than that, from about 50 volts AC.","3":"Harm can start far lower, from about 50 volts AC, so voltages well under 1,000 can still hurt you."},"source_title":"Electrical safety: Frequently asked questions (FAQs)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/electricity/faq.htm"},{"id":"safety-b1-10","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You find a site power board with its cover smashed and wires showing. What should you do first?","options":["Push the wires back in and close it up","Keep using it, but carefully","Warn people to keep away and, if you can do it safely, switch it off","Cover it with a board and carry on"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Damaged electrical equipment with wires showing can kill. First, warn everyone to stay away. Switch it off only if you can do that safely. Then report it so a competent person can make it safe. Never try to fix it yourself, and do not just hide it.","why_not":{"0":"Wires showing can kill, so never try to fix it yourself, but warn people and report it.","1":"Damaged equipment with wires showing can kill however careful you are, so warn people to stay away.","3":"Hiding it does not make it safe, so warn people, switch it off if safe, and report it."},"source_title":"Electrical safety: Frequently asked questions (FAQs)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/electricity/faq.htm"},{"id":"safety-b1-12","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A cut-off saw in the tool store has a tag on it saying 'DANGER - DO NOT USE'. Who can take the tag off?","options":["Anyone who needs to use the saw","The first person to arrive in the morning","Anyone, once the tag has been on for a week","Only an authorised person who is sure the saw is now safe"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A danger tag means someone has found a problem. Do not use the saw and do not take the tag off. Only an authorised person who has made sure the saw is safe again may remove it.","why_not":{"0":"Only an authorised person who is sure the saw is safe may take the tag off.","1":"Arriving first gives you no right to remove it, because only an authorised person may.","2":"Time does not make the saw safe, so only an authorised person who has checked it may remove the tag."},"source_title":"Providing and using work equipment safely: A brief guide (INDG291)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg291.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b1-14","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You bring your own hammer and trowel to use at work. Do the work equipment rules (PUWER) cover them?","options":["No: only tools your employer bought are covered","Only if they are electric","Yes: tools you bring for work are covered too","Only if they are less than a year old"],"correct":2,"explanation":"PUWER covers equipment used at work, from hammers and knives to dumpers. If your employer lets you bring your own tools, they are covered too, and your employer must make sure they are safe and right for the job. Keep them in good condition and report any damage.","why_not":{"0":"If your employer lets you bring your own tools, PUWER covers them too.","1":"PUWER covers simple hand tools like hammers, not just electric ones.","3":"The age of a tool does not matter, because tools you bring for work are covered."},"source_title":"Providing and using work equipment safely: A brief guide (INDG291)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg291.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b1-15","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A mortar mixer has jammed. Before anyone reaches in to clear it, what must be done?","options":["Leave it running slowly so the blockage shakes loose","Switch it off and isolate it from its power so it cannot start","Clear it with a shovel while the drum is still turning","Nothing, as long as you are quick"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A machine that is running, or that can start up again, can catch your arm. Before clearing a blockage, cleaning or adjusting a machine, it must be switched off and isolated from its power, or locked off, so nobody can start it while you work on it.","why_not":{"0":"A running machine can catch your arm, so it must be switched off and isolated first.","2":"Never clear a blockage while the drum turns, because it must be switched off and isolated first.","3":"Being quick does not stop a machine catching you, so it must be switched off and isolated."},"source_title":"Providing and using work equipment safely: A brief guide (INDG291)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg291.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b1-16","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Pallets of blocks have been left across the footpath, so people must walk on the haul road to get past. What should happen?","options":["The pallets should be moved so the footpath is clear again","Nothing: people can step onto the haul road for a moment","Put a hi-vis vest on the pallets so drivers can see them","Tell only the drivers, so they look out for people"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Footpaths keep people on foot away from vehicles. HSE says walkways must not be blocked in a way that makes people step onto the road used by vehicles. Report it so the materials are moved and the footpath is clear again.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says walkways must not be blocked so that people have to step onto the vehicle route.","2":"Marking the pallets does not help, because people on foot still have to walk where vehicles drive.","3":"Warning drivers is not enough, because the footpath must be cleared so people stay off the road."},"source_title":"Traffic management on site","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/vehiclestrafficmanagement.htm"},{"id":"safety-b1-18","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"The dumper driver offers you a lift across site, sitting on the side of the skip. What do you do?","options":["Get on, but hold on tight","Get on if the driver goes slowly","Say no: you may only ride in a proper seat made for a passenger","Get on if you are wearing a hard hat"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The law says nobody may ride on a site vehicle except in a safe place made for that purpose, such as a passenger seat. Sitting on a skip, bucket or step, you could easily fall off and be hit by the vehicle. Walk, and use the footpath.","why_not":{"0":"Holding on tight does not make the skip a safe seat, and you could fall and be hit.","1":"Going slowly does not change the law, which says you may only ride in a proper passenger seat.","3":"A hard hat does not stop you falling off, so you may only ride in a proper passenger seat."},"source_title":"Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 28 (Vehicles)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/28"},{"id":"safety-b1-20","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Who is killed most often in accidents with excavators?","options":["The excavator driver","People working near the excavator","Members of the public walking past the site","Delivery drivers sitting in their lorries"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says most excavator deaths are people working near the machine, not the driver. They are hit when it moves, trapped when it turns (slews), or struck by the bucket. That is why barriers are used to keep people out of the area where an excavator is working.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says most excavator deaths are people working near the machine, not the driver.","2":"HSE says most excavator deaths are people working near the machine on site, not passers-by.","3":"HSE says most excavator deaths are people working near the machine, not drivers in lorries."},"source_title":"Excavators: Overview","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/excavators-overview.htm"},{"id":"safety-b1-21","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"An excavator is turning (slewing) next to a wall. What gap does HSE say must be kept between any part of the machine, especially the heavy weight at the back, and the wall?","options":["At least 10 cm","At least 25 cm","More than 0.5 m (50 cm)","No gap is needed if the driver is careful"],"correct":2,"explanation":"When an excavator turns, its back end swings round. If the gap to a wall or vehicle is too small, a person standing there can be crushed. HSE says to keep a gap of more than 0.5 m, and to choose a machine with a short tail swing for tight spaces.","why_not":{"0":"10 cm is far too small and a person could be crushed, so HSE says keep more than 0.5 m.","1":"25 cm is still too small and a person could be crushed, so HSE says keep more than 0.5 m.","3":"Care alone is not enough, because HSE says a gap of more than 0.5 m must be kept."},"source_title":"Excavators: Overview","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/excavators-overview.htm"},{"id":"safety-b1-23","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Old timber with nails sticking out of it has been left on a walkway. What should happen?","options":["Leave it: everyone wears safety boots","Take the nails out or clear the timber away","Kick it to the side of the walkway","Pile it up and leave it until the end of the job"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The law says timber with nails sticking out must not be left anywhere it could hurt someone. A nail can go into your foot, or into your hand when you pick the timber up. Safety boots help, but they are no reason to leave it there.","why_not":{"0":"Boots help, but the law says timber with nails sticking out must not be left where it could hurt someone.","2":"Kicking it aside still leaves the nails where someone could step on them or grab them.","3":"It must not be left lying about at all, so take the nails out or clear it away now."},"source_title":"Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 18 (Good order and site security)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/18"},{"id":"safety-b1-25","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A bricklayer says clearing up the waste in his work area is only the labourer's job. What does HSE say?","options":["He is right: only labourers clear waste","Clearing waste should be a priority for every trade","Waste can wait until the end of the job","Only the site manager deals with waste"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Waste left lying around causes trips and can feed a fire. HSE says every trade should put clearing its waste first, and keep work areas tidy as the job goes along, not just at the end.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says clearing waste should be a priority for all trades, not just labourers.","2":"Waste left lying around causes trips, so keep work areas clear as you go, not just at the end.","3":"HSE says clearing waste is a priority for every trade, not a job left to the site manager."},"source_title":"Materials storage and waste management","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/storage.htm"},{"id":"safety-b2-02","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"It is a very hot day. You want to wear shorts, but the site rules say no shorts. What do you do?","options":["Wear long trousers: you must follow the site rule","Wear shorts: the heat is the bigger danger","Wear shorts if your boots come high up your legs","Wear shorts, but stay away from the site manager"],"correct":0,"explanation":"If a site does not allow shorts, you are expected to follow that rule. Clothes on your legs help protect them from cuts, grazes and splinters. On a building site, even a small cut in the skin can get infected. Tall boots only cover part of your legs.","why_not":{"1":"You must follow the site rule, and trousers protect your legs from cuts that can get infected.","2":"Tall boots only cover part of your legs, and the site rule says no shorts.","3":"Hiding from the manager is still breaking the rule, which you are expected to follow."},"source_title":"Construction general (FAQs: personal protective equipment)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-ppe.htm"},{"id":"safety-b2-03","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Your site does not allow rigger boots (pull-on safety boots). What reason does HSE give for some sites banning them?","options":["They are a looser fit, so people wearing them are more likely to twist or sprain an ankle","They let water in when it rains","They cost more than lace-up boots","They are too heavy to wear all day"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Some companies found that people in rigger boots were much more likely to twist or sprain an ankle. The boots are looser, so on uneven ground the foot can roll to the side more easily. HSE says this is a fair reason for a site rule, and you need to follow it.","why_not":{"1":"HSE's reason is not rain but that a looser fit makes twisted and sprained ankles more likely.","2":"HSE's reason is not cost but that looser boots make twisted and sprained ankles more likely.","3":"HSE's reason is not weight but that looser boots make it easier to twist an ankle."},"source_title":"Construction general (FAQs: personal protective equipment)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-ppe.htm"},{"id":"safety-b2-06","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A tipper lorry is tipping close to an overhead power line. The driver says, 'It's fine as long as the body doesn't touch the wires.' What is true?","options":["He is right: there is only danger if it touches","Getting too close is enough: the electricity can jump across the gap","It is safe as long as the engine is switched off","It is safe because lorry tyres are made of rubber"],"correct":1,"explanation":"You do not have to touch a power line to be in danger. If the tipper body or any plant gets close to the wires, the electricity can jump across the gap. This is called a 'flashover'. Switching off the engine or having rubber tyres does not stop it. Keep well away from the lines.","why_not":{"0":"You do not have to touch the wires, because getting close is enough for electricity to jump across.","2":"Switching off the engine does not stop electricity jumping across the gap from the wires.","3":"Rubber tyres do not stop electricity jumping from the wires, so keep well away from the lines."},"source_title":"Electricity – overhead power lines","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/overhead.htm"},{"id":"safety-b2-09","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Your 230 V tool is plugged in through an RCD. A workmate says, 'With an RCD you can't get hurt by a shock.' What is true?","options":["He is right: an RCD stops every shock","An RCD lowers the chance of injury, but a shock can still badly hurt or kill you","An RCD only works indoors","With an RCD, the tool never needs to be checked"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An RCD cuts the power very fast when it finds some kinds of fault, so it makes an injury less likely. But HSE says a shock can still cause very serious or deadly injuries. So an RCD is only a back-up. You still need safe tools and leads in good condition.","why_not":{"0":"An RCD lowers the chance of injury, but HSE says a shock can still seriously hurt or kill.","2":"An RCD works outdoors too, but it is only a back-up, so a shock can still badly hurt you.","3":"An RCD is only a back-up, so you still need safe tools and leads that are checked."},"source_title":"Work using electrically powered equipment","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/electricity/electricequip.htm"},{"id":"safety-b2-10","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You are about to fit a new disc to an angle grinder. What must be true about the speed marked on the disc?","options":["The grinder must never spin the disc faster than the speed marked on it","Nothing: any disc that fits the grinder is fine","The disc should be run faster than its marked speed so it cuts quicker","Speed marks only matter on bench grinders, not hand-held ones"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Every disc is marked with the top speed it can safely take. Running it faster is one of the main causes of discs breaking apart. Pieces of a burst disc fly out very fast, at about 80 mph or more. Check that the grinder's speed is not higher than the speed on the disc.","why_not":{"1":"Fitting is not enough, because the grinder must not spin the disc faster than its marked speed.","2":"Running a disc too fast is one of the main causes of it breaking apart.","3":"The speed mark matters on every abrasive disc, hand-held grinders included."},"source_title":"Safety in the use of abrasive wheels (HSG17)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg17.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b2-11","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You want to use a bigger disc than will fit under the guard of your angle grinder. A workmate says, 'Take the guard off and it will fit.' What do you do?","options":["Do not do it: keep the guard on and use only discs made for that grinder","Take the guard off, but wear a face shield","Take the guard off, but only for one quick cut","Take the guard off and press more gently"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says serious, often deadly, accidents have happened when a guard was taken off so a bigger disc could be fitted. The bigger disc then spins faster than it was made for and can burst, with no guard between you and the pieces. A face shield or a quick cut does not make this safe.","why_not":{"1":"A face shield is not enough, because a bigger disc can spin too fast and burst with no guard there.","2":"One quick cut is enough for a disc to burst, so never take the guard off.","3":"Pressing gently does not stop a too-big disc running too fast and bursting."},"source_title":"Safety in the use of abrasive wheels (HSG17)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg17.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b2-12","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Before fitting a cutting disc, you see that it is slightly bent (warped). What should you do?","options":["Do not use it: get a new disc","Use it, but press harder to keep it straight","Use it for small cuts only","Flatten it with a hammer first"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Check cutting discs for bending or other damage before you fit them. HSE says a warped disc must not be used. It can shake, rub on the sides of the cut, get hot and break apart while it is spinning. Hammering it or pressing harder makes things worse.","why_not":{"1":"Pressing harder makes it worse, because a warped disc can shake, get hot and break apart.","2":"HSE says a warped disc must not be used at all, even for small cuts.","3":"Hammering does not make it safe, because HSE says a warped disc should not be used."},"source_title":"Safety in the use of abrasive wheels (HSG17)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg17.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b2-13","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"The person who uses the site bench saw has gone for a break. A new starter who has never been shown how to use it offers to finish the job. What should happen?","options":["He should wait: only people who are trained and allowed to use the saw may use it","He can use it if he goes slowly","He can use it because the job is nearly finished","He can use it if he wears gloves"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says not to let anyone use machinery if they are not trained or allowed to use it. New starters can be more at risk and need instruction, training and supervision first. Going slowly, wearing gloves or the job being nearly done does not make up for not knowing how to use the saw safely.","why_not":{"1":"Going slowly does not make up for not knowing how to use the saw safely.","2":"A nearly finished job does not make it safe, because only trained and allowed people may use it.","3":"Gloves do not make up for having no training, so he must not use the saw."},"source_title":"Providing and using work equipment safely: A brief guide (INDG291)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg291.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b2-14","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You are working near a large machine. It sounds a horn and a light starts flashing. What is the warning most likely telling you?","options":["The machine is about to start: move away to a safe place","It is time for a break","The machine has broken down and is safe to go near","The operator wants you to come closer"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The work equipment law says that where needed, a warning you can hear or see must be given when a machine is about to start. It also says people in danger must have enough time and a way to get clear. So when you hear or see the warning, move away from the machine at once.","why_not":{"1":"A horn and flashing light warn that the machine is about to start, not that it is break time.","2":"The warning means the machine is about to start, so it is not safe to go near.","3":"The warning tells you to get clear, not to come closer."},"source_title":"Provision and Use of Work Equipment Regulations 1998, regulation 17 (Controls)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/2306/regulation/17"},{"id":"safety-b2-15","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Your workmate is cutting a kerb with a cut-off saw. You need to ask him something. What should you do?","options":["Wait until he has finished the cut and stopped the saw","Tap him on the shoulder while he is cutting","Walk up behind him and shout over the noise","Throw a small stone near him to get his attention"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE's list of things not to do around machines includes distracting the people using them. If he is startled while the saw is cutting, he could lose control of it and hurt himself or you. Wait until the saw has stopped and he can see you.","why_not":{"1":"Touching him while he cuts could startle him, and he could lose control of the saw.","2":"Shouting from behind can startle him, and HSE says do not distract people using machines.","3":"Throwing things distracts him and could make him lose control of the saw."},"source_title":"Providing and using work equipment safely: A brief guide (INDG291)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg291.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b2-17","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"The footpath on your site has to cross the road used by site vehicles. What does HSE say the crossing point should be like?","options":["Clearly signed and lit, where drivers and people on foot can see each other clearly","On a blind bend, where vehicles have to slow down","Behind stacked materials, out of the wind","Anywhere people like, as long as they run across"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Where a walkway crosses a vehicle route, HSE says there should be a clearly signed and lit crossing point. Drivers and people on foot must be able to see each other clearly. On a blind bend or behind stacked materials, nobody can see who is coming.","why_not":{"1":"On a blind bend, drivers and people on foot cannot see each other.","2":"Behind stacked materials, drivers and people on foot cannot see each other coming.","3":"People must cross at a clearly signed and lit crossing point, not anywhere they like."},"source_title":"Traffic management on site","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/vehiclestrafficmanagement.htm"},{"id":"safety-b2-19","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"The site dumper is parked and nobody is using it. You once drove a tractor on a farm. Can you use the dumper to move some sand?","options":["No: only drivers who are trained, competent and authorised for that dumper may drive it","Yes, if you drive slowly","Yes, because you have driven a tractor","Yes, if the key has been left in it"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says dumper drivers should be trained, competent and authorised to drive that dumper. Dumpers can tip over easily, and most dumper deaths happen when one overturns or hits someone on foot. Driving a tractor once does not count, and a key left in it is not permission.","why_not":{"1":"Driving slowly does not make it OK, because only trained, competent and authorised drivers may use it.","2":"A tractor is not that dumper, and you must be trained and authorised for the dumper itself.","3":"A key left in it is not permission, because you must be trained and authorised to drive it."},"source_title":"Dumpers","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/dumpers.htm"},{"id":"safety-b2-22","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A dumper skip has been loaded with soil piled high above its top edge. The driver cannot see over it. What should happen?","options":["Take some soil off: the load should not go above the top of the skip or block the driver's view","Nothing, if the driver goes slowly","Nothing, as it is only a short trip","Nothing, if the soil is dry"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says dumper loads should be spread evenly and not go above the top edge of the skip. The load must not block the driver's view. A driver who cannot see ahead could hit someone, even going slowly or on a short trip.","why_not":{"1":"Going slowly does not help a driver who cannot see ahead, because he could still hit someone.","2":"Even a short trip is dangerous if the driver cannot see, so take some soil off.","3":"Dry or wet, the load must not go above the skip's top edge or block the driver's view."},"source_title":"Dumpers","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/dumpers.htm"},{"id":"safety-b2-23","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"The traffic signs along the site haul road are covered in mud and hard to read. What should happen?","options":["They should be cleaned so they can be seen and do their job","Nothing: the drivers already know the rules","Take them down, because dirty signs are no use","Leave them until the end of the job"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says signs should be cleaned and looked after so they stay easy to see and do their job. A sign nobody can read warns nobody, and new drivers and visitors do not know the site rules. Taking signs away makes it worse.","why_not":{"1":"New drivers and visitors do not know the rules, so signs must be kept clean and easy to see.","2":"Taking signs away makes it worse, so clean them so they can do their job.","3":"A sign nobody can read warns nobody, so clean them now, not at the end of the job."},"source_title":"Workplace transport safety: A brief guide (INDG199)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg199.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b2-24","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You are clearing broken blocks from the second floor. A workmate says, 'Just throw them out of the window into the skip.' What is right?","options":["Do not throw them: bring them down safely, for example by a waste chute or in a bin","Throw them if you shout a warning first","Throw them if nobody seems to be below","Throw them, but only the small pieces"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law says materials must not be thrown or tipped from height where they could hurt someone. Even a small piece of block can badly hurt a person below, and you cannot always see who is there. Use a proper way down, such as an enclosed waste chute or a bin.","why_not":{"1":"Shouting does not make it safe, because the law says do not throw things from height that could hurt someone.","2":"You cannot always see who is below, so bring the blocks down safely instead.","3":"Even a small piece of block can badly hurt someone below, so do not throw anything."},"source_title":"Work at Height Regulations 2005, regulation 10 (Falling objects)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2005/735/regulation/10"},{"id":"safety-b2-25","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You need to run an extension lead across a walkway to reach your work. What should you do with the cable?","options":["Put it where people will not trip on it and it is less likely to get damaged","Lay it across the middle of the walkway, where people can see it","Leave it in loose loops on the floor so it has plenty of slack","Run it through a doorway and shut the door on it"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says trailing cables should be put where they are not a trip hazard and are less likely to be damaged. A cable across a walkway can trip people, and loose loops are easy to catch a foot in. A door shut on a cable can crush it and damage the cover, which can lead to a shock.","why_not":{"1":"A cable across the middle of a walkway can trip people, even if they can see it.","2":"Loose loops on the floor are easy to catch a foot in and trip over.","3":"A door shut on a cable can crush it and damage the cover, which can lead to a shock."},"source_title":"Work using electrically powered equipment","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/electricity/electricequip.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-02","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Your hi-vis jacket is caked in mud and the shiny strips on it are hard to see. What should happen?","options":["Get it cleaned, or swap it for a clean one, so the strips show up again","Nothing: the orange colour still shows","Wear it inside out until the end of the job","Leave it until the job is finished, then wash it"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says hi-vis jackets, trousers and coveralls must be cleaned regularly so the reflective strips stay easy to see. Clothing like this does not work nearly as well when it is dirty. If mud covers the strips, drivers may not see you.","why_not":{"1":"The shiny strips matter too, so HSE says hi-vis must be kept clean so they are easy to see.","2":"Inside out, the strips cannot be seen at all, so get it cleaned or swapped for a clean one.","3":"Drivers may not see you today, and HSE says hi-vis must be cleaned regularly, not just at the end."},"source_title":"Using personal protective equipment (PPE) to control risks at work: Maintenance","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/ppe/maintenance.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-03","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A man in the site car park is selling very cheap hard hats from the back of his van. They have no labels. Why does HSE say you should not use one?","options":["They might be the wrong colour for your trade","There are fake hard hats on sale: head protection should only come from a reputable supplier","You may only wear a hard hat with your company's name on it","Hard hats only count if you paid full price"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE warns that there are fake hard hats on the market. A fake can look right but fail to protect your head. Head protection should only come from a reputable supplier, so ask your employer for a proper one.","why_not":{"0":"Colour is not the problem, because a fake hard hat can look right but fail to protect your head.","2":"HSE does not ask for your company's name on it, only that it comes from a reputable supplier.","3":"Price does not prove a hard hat is real, so get head protection from a reputable supplier."},"source_title":"Construction general (FAQs: personal protective equipment)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-ppe.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-04","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"The job needs gloves to protect your hands. A workmate says rubbing barrier cream on your hands will do instead. What is right?","options":["Barrier cream is not a substitute for proper PPE: wear the gloves","Barrier cream is fine if you put on a thick layer","Use barrier cream in the morning and gloves after lunch","Barrier cream is better because you can feel what you are doing"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says barrier creams are not a substitute for proper PPE. If the job needs gloves, wear the gloves. Putting on more cream does not change that.","why_not":{"1":"A thicker layer does not help, because HSE says barrier cream is not a substitute for proper PPE.","2":"The job needs gloves, so wear them the whole time, because cream is not a substitute for PPE.","3":"Feeling what you do does not protect your hands, and barrier cream is not a substitute for gloves."},"source_title":"Using personal protective equipment (PPE) to control risks at work: Using the right type of PPE","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/ppe/using-the-right-type-of-ppe.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-05","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You have left your ear plugs at home. A workmate offers you the reusable pair he wore yesterday. What should you do?","options":["Rinse them under the tap and use them","Use them: sharing is fine if he is not ill","Ask for your own pair: PPE like this should be for one person's use only, to keep it clean","Work without hearing protection just for today"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The law says that where it is needed to keep PPE clean and free of risk to health, it must be given to one person for their use only. Ear plugs go inside your ears, so do not share them. Ask for your own pair before you start, and do not work without hearing protection when the job needs it.","why_not":{"0":"Rinsing does not make them yours, and ear plugs go inside your ears, so do not share them.","1":"Ear plugs should be for one person only, to keep them clean, even if he is not ill.","3":"Do not work without hearing protection when the job needs it, so ask for your own pair."},"source_title":"Personal Protective Equipment at Work Regulations 1992, regulation 4 (Provision of personal protective equipment)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1992/2966/regulation/4"},{"id":"safety-b3-06","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You have finished with your safety goggles for the day. Your employer has given you a locker to keep your PPE in. What should you do with them?","options":["Leave them on the workbench ready for the morning","Put them back in the locker provided","Hang them on the mixer so you can find them","Leave them on the van dashboard"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The law says workers who are given PPE must take reasonable steps to put it back in the place provided for it after use. Kept there, it stays clean, safe from damage and ready for next time.","why_not":{"0":"The law says you must put PPE back in the place provided for it after use.","2":"On the mixer they can get dirty or damaged, so put them back in the locker provided.","3":"In the van they can get dirty or damaged, so put them back in the locker provided."},"source_title":"Personal Protective Equipment at Work Regulations 1992, regulation 10 (Use of personal protective equipment)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1992/2966/regulation/10"},{"id":"safety-b3-07","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"The buckle on your safety harness keeps sticking. A workmate offers to sort it out with pliers and some oil. What should happen?","options":["Report it: a harness may need to go back to the maker to be repaired","Let him fix it if he is careful","Use it as it is and fix it at the weekend","Swap the buckle for one off an old bag"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Some simple care you can do yourself, like putting a new lace in your boot. But HSE says specialist PPE, such as a harness for working at height, may need to go back to the manufacturer if it needs repair. A home-made fix could fail when you need it most, so report it.","why_not":{"1":"A home-made fix could fail when you need it most, and a harness may need to go back to the maker.","2":"A sticking buckle could fail when you need it, so report it now instead of using it.","3":"A buckle from a bag is not made for a harness and could fail, so report it instead."},"source_title":"Using personal protective equipment (PPE) to control risks at work: Maintenance","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/ppe/maintenance.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-09","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Before you use an angle grinder, you notice brown scorch marks on its plug. What do these marks most likely mean?","options":["It is just dirt from the site","The plug is new and still bedding in","The fuse is doing its job properly","It is overheating: stop it being used and get it checked"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE's simple visual check includes looking for burn marks or staining, which suggest the equipment is overheating. If you are worried about a tool, stop it from being used and ask a competent person to check it properly.","why_not":{"0":"Burn marks are not dirt, because HSE says they suggest the tool is overheating.","1":"New plugs do not get scorch marks from 'bedding in', because HSE says burn marks suggest overheating.","2":"Scorch marks do not mean the fuse is working, because they suggest the tool is overheating."},"source_title":"Work using electrically powered equipment","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/electricity/electricequip.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-10","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"An RCD that is there to protect people from electric shock should trip at what current, according to HSE?","options":["At least 100 milliamps (mA)","Not more than 30 milliamps (mA)","13 amps, the same as the fuse","Any setting, as long as it has a test button"],"correct":1,"explanation":"To protect people, an RCD should have a tripping current of not more than 30 mA. RCDs that trip at higher currents are used to protect against fire, not to protect people from shock. A 13 amp fuse is a different thing.","why_not":{"0":"100 mA is too high to protect people, because RCDs that trip higher are for fire protection.","2":"A 13 amp fuse is a different thing, and an RCD for people should trip at 30 mA or less.","3":"The setting matters, because to protect people it should trip at not more than 30 mA."},"source_title":"Work using electrically powered equipment","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/electricity/electricequip.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-11","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Site power tools run at 110 V from a transformer that is 'centre tapped to earth'. What does this do?","options":["It means the tools never need checking","It makes the cables waterproof","It halves the voltage between a live wire and earth","It doubles the power of the tools"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says a transformer whose output is centre tapped to earth halves the voltage between a live wire and earth. Lower voltage means less risk of a serious shock, which is why this kind of supply is used on building sites. The tools and leads still need checking.","why_not":{"0":"The tools and leads still need checking, even on a lower voltage.","1":"It does nothing to make cables waterproof, because it halves the voltage between live and earth.","3":"It does not double the power, because it halves the voltage between a live wire and earth."},"source_title":"Work using electrically powered equipment","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/electricity/electricequip.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-12","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"There are not enough sockets in the site cabin, so someone has plugged several adaptors into one socket to run a kettle, a heater and phone chargers. What is the danger?","options":["Overloading a socket with adaptors can cause a fire","The chargers will charge too slowly","None, as long as the kettle and heater are not on at the same time","It only matters if the adaptors are different makes"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says overloading socket outlets by using adaptors can cause fire. Enough sockets should be provided so people do not need to stack adaptors. If you see this, tell your supervisor.","why_not":{"1":"The real danger is fire, not slow charging, because overloading a socket with adaptors can cause a fire.","2":"Stacking adaptors on one socket is still a fire risk, so enough sockets should be provided.","3":"The makes do not matter, because overloading one socket with adaptors can cause a fire."},"source_title":"Electrical safety and you: A brief guide (INDG231)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg231.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b3-13","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You are about to plug a 110 V saw into an extension lead. Where possible, what does HSE say you should do first?","options":["Hold the trigger on so you know straight away that it works","Plug it in quickly so there is no spark","Make sure the saw is switched off, and switch off the socket too","Nothing: you only need to switch off before unplugging"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Where possible, the tool and the socket should both be switched off before you plug in or unplug. If the tool is switched on when it gets power, it can start without warning.","why_not":{"0":"If the tool is on when it gets power, it can start without warning, so switch it off first.","1":"Speed is not the answer, so switch off the saw and the socket before plugging in.","3":"Switch off before plugging in too, not just before unplugging, or the tool can start suddenly."},"source_title":"Electrical safety and you: A brief guide (INDG231)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg231.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b3-14","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You are asked to take out an old light fitting. The supervisor says, 'The electrician told me that circuit is off.' What does HSE say?","options":["Being told it is off is enough","Flick the light switch: if the light stays off, the wires are dead","Someone who knows what they are doing must prove it dead with proper test equipment before work starts","Touch the wires quickly with the back of your hand to check"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says being told that electrical equipment is dead is never acceptable. Once it has been isolated, it must be proven dead before work starts, by someone who understands what they are doing and uses proper test equipment. A light that will not come on does not prove the wires are dead.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says being told it is dead is never enough, so it must be proven dead with test equipment.","1":"A light staying off does not prove the wires are dead, so proper test equipment must be used.","3":"Never touch wires to check them, because someone competent must prove them dead with proper test equipment."},"source_title":"Electrical safety on construction sites (HSG141)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg141.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b3-15","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A temporary power cable has to cross the haul road that dumpers and lorries use. What does HSE say should be done?","options":["Lay it flat on the road so the wheels roll over it","Cover it with a sheet of plywood","Tape it down to the road with hazard tape","Put it in a duct at least 0.5 m below the surface, with a cable marker at each end"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Cables should be placed where they are not likely to be damaged. HSE says a cable crossing a road should go in a duct with a cable marker at each end, and if vehicles use the road the duct should be at least 0.5 m below the surface. A cable lying on the road, even under a board or tape, can be crushed by wheels.","why_not":{"0":"Wheels rolling over a cable can crush it, so HSE says put it in a duct below the road.","1":"A board on the road does not stop wheels crushing the cable, so use a duct at least 0.5 m down.","2":"Tape does not protect a cable from wheels, so it should go in a duct at least 0.5 m down."},"source_title":"Electrical safety on construction sites (HSG141)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg141.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b3-16","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You are up a stepladder using a mains drill that has a fault. Why could even a small electric shock be very dangerous here?","options":["It could make you fall off the stepladder","Small shocks cannot hurt anyone","It will make the stepladder live for the rest of the day","It will blow the fuse in the site cabin"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says even shocks that do not kill can cause severe and lasting injury. A shock from faulty equipment can make you fall from a ladder, scaffold or other platform. Faulty tools should not be used.","why_not":{"1":"Even small shocks can cause severe and lasting injury, and they can make you fall.","2":"The danger is that a shock can make you fall off, not that the stepladder stays live.","3":"The real danger to you is falling off the stepladder, not a blown fuse."},"source_title":"Electrical safety and you: A brief guide (INDG231)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg231.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b3-17","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A machine on site has an interlocked guard. What does that mean?","options":["The guard is bolted on and never opens","The machine cannot start until the guard is closed, and the guard cannot be opened while the machine is still moving","The guard can be unclipped when it gets in the way","The guard locks the machine away in the store at night"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An interlock links the guard to the machine. HSE explains that the machine cannot start before the guard is closed, and the guard cannot be opened while the machine is still moving. Never try to get round an interlock.","why_not":{"0":"That is a fixed guard, but an interlocked guard can open once the machine has stopped.","2":"Never get round a guard, because an interlock stops the machine running while the guard is open.","3":"An interlock has nothing to do with storage, because it links the guard to the machine starting."},"source_title":"Providing and using work equipment safely: A brief guide (INDG291)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg291.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b3-18","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You are cutting a thin strip of wood on a saw bench. To feed the last part through, your fingers would have to go close to the blade. What should you use?","options":["Your thumb, keeping it flat on the wood","A thick glove so the blade slides off","Nothing: just feed it faster so your hand is near the blade for less time","A push stick"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Where a guard cannot fully protect you, HSE says to use things like jigs, holders and push sticks. A push stick keeps your hand away from the blade. Going faster, or wearing a glove, does not keep your fingers away from it.","why_not":{"0":"Your thumb is still near the blade, but a push stick keeps your hand away.","1":"A glove does not stop a blade, so use a push stick to keep your fingers away.","2":"Feeding faster still puts your hand near the blade, so use a push stick."},"source_title":"Providing and using work equipment safely: A brief guide (INDG291)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg291.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b3-20","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Offcuts and sawdust are piling up on the floor around the site saw bench. What does HSE say about the area around a machine?","options":["It only needs clearing at the end of the job","Sawdust on the floor is fine because it soaks up spills","It should be kept clean and tidy, free from things to trip or slip on, and well lit","Only the machine itself needs to be kept clean"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says the work area around a machine should be kept clean and tidy, free from obstructions and slip and trip hazards, and well lit. Clear it as you go, not just at the end of the job.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says the area round a machine should be kept clean and tidy, so clear it as you go.","1":"Sawdust on the floor is a slip and trip hazard, so keep the area clean and tidy.","3":"HSE says the whole work area around the machine must be kept clean and tidy, not just the machine."},"source_title":"Providing and using work equipment safely: A brief guide (INDG291)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg291.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b3-21","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A hired floor saw arrives on site. Machines like this have to be inspected, but it has come with no inspection report, tag or label. What should happen?","options":["Use it: hire firms always check their machines","Do not use it until you know the inspection has been done","Use it, and ask for the report at the end of the week","Write today's date on it yourself"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says work equipment that needs inspection should not be used unless you know the inspection has taken place. Equipment from a hire company should come with proof of its last inspection, such as a report, or a tag, colour code or label on smaller items.","why_not":{"0":"Do not assume, because HSE says hired equipment should come with proof of its last inspection.","2":"Do not use it first and ask later, because you must know the inspection is done before use.","3":"Writing a date on it yourself is not an inspection, so it must really be inspected."},"source_title":"Inspection of work equipment","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/work-equipment-machinery/inspection.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-22","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A cement mixer fell off the back of a lorry while it was being unloaded. It still seems to run. What should happen before it is used?","options":["Use it: if it runs, it is fine","Wait until its next planned inspection date","Give it a wipe down and carry on","It should be inspected, because something has happened that could have made it unsafe"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Equipment should be inspected each time something happens that could have made it unsafe, such as known or suspected serious damage. A machine that still runs can have damage you cannot see, so it should be checked before anyone uses it.","why_not":{"0":"A machine that runs can still have damage you cannot see, so it must be inspected first.","1":"Do not wait, because suspected serious damage means it should be inspected before anyone uses it.","2":"Wiping it does not find hidden damage, so it must be inspected after a fall like that."},"source_title":"Inspection of work equipment","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/work-equipment-machinery/inspection.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-23","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You need to lever up a heavy paving slab. The nearest thing to hand is a wood chisel. What should you do?","options":["Get a tool made for the job, such as a slab lifter or a crowbar","Use the chisel: it will do for one slab","Knock the chisel under the slab with a hammer","Use a screwdriver instead of the chisel"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law says work equipment must only be used for jobs, and in conditions, that it is suitable for. A chisel or screwdriver used as a lever can snap or slip and hurt you. Use a tool made for the job.","why_not":{"1":"Tools must only be used for jobs they suit, and a chisel used as a lever can snap or slip.","2":"Hammering a chisel under the slab is still using the wrong tool, and it could snap and hurt you.","3":"A screwdriver is no better as a lever, because it can snap or slip too."},"source_title":"Provision and Use of Work Equipment Regulations 1998, regulation 4 (Suitability of work equipment)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/2306/regulation/4"},{"id":"safety-b3-24","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You have just fitted a new disc to a grinder. What should you do before you start cutting?","options":["Start cutting straight away while the disc is fresh","Run it at full speed for a short time, with everyone standing clear","Run it slowly against the work to wear it in","Ask a workmate to hold his ear close and listen to it"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says new or re-fitted wheels should be run free at full working speed for a short time before they are used, with everyone standing clear during this trial run. The guards must be in place.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says run a new disc free at full speed for a short time first, with everyone standing clear.","2":"Do not wear it in on the work, but run it free at full speed first, with everyone clear.","3":"Everyone should stand clear during the trial run, not lean in close to listen."},"source_title":"Safety in the use of abrasive wheels (HSG17)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg17.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b3-26","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"How should thin cutting discs be stored?","options":["Hung on a nail on the wall of the van","Standing on their edge on the floor of a damp container","Laid flat on a firm, flat surface, away from heat and damp","Loose in a toolbag with the other tools"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE says thin cutting-off wheels should be laid flat on a rigid, flat surface away from too much heat and moisture, so that they do not warp. A warped disc should not be used.","why_not":{"0":"Hanging them can let them warp, so HSE says lay them flat on a firm, flat surface.","1":"Damp and standing on edge can make them warp, so lay them flat, away from heat and damp.","3":"Loose in a toolbag they can bend or get damaged, so lay them flat on a firm surface."},"source_title":"Safety in the use of abrasive wheels (HSG17)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg17.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b3-27","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"HSE says most transport accidents on building sites come down to one main problem. What is it?","options":["Vehicles running out of fuel","People on foot and vehicles not being kept apart well enough","Too many signs on site","Drivers listening to the radio"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says the majority of construction transport accidents come from not keeping pedestrians and vehicles apart well enough. Separate walkways, barriers and proper crossing points all help.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says the main problem is people on foot and vehicles not being kept apart, not fuel.","2":"Signs are not the problem, because HSE says it is not keeping people and vehicles apart.","3":"HSE does not blame the radio, but people on foot and vehicles not being kept apart well enough."},"source_title":"Traffic management on site","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/vehiclestrafficmanagement.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-29","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You drive your van to site. The quickest place to park is right next to where the excavator is working. What does HSE say about parking for workers?","options":["Park wherever is closest to your job","Park in the work area, but leave your hazard lights on","Parking is only a problem for lorries","Car and van parking should be away from the work area"],"correct":3,"explanation":"To cut down the number of vehicles moving about where people are working, HSE says parking for workers and visitors should be away from the work area. Use the parking area you have been shown.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says car and van parking should be away from the work area.","1":"Hazard lights do not make it safe, because parking should be away from the work area.","2":"HSE says parking for workers' cars and vans should also be away from the work area."},"source_title":"Traffic management on site","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/vehiclestrafficmanagement.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-30","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A dumper is tipping spoil into an excavation. What does HSE say should be in place to stop it going over the edge?","options":["Wheel stops placed a safe distance back from the edge","A line of cones right on the edge","Nothing, as long as the driver is careful","A workmate standing at the edge to shout when to stop"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says wheel stops should be put a safe distance from the edges of excavations and pits, so dumpers do not fall in when tipping. Cones will not stop a dumper, and nobody should be standing at the edge.","why_not":{"1":"Cones will not stop a dumper, so HSE says use wheel stops a safe distance back.","2":"Care alone is not enough, because HSE says wheel stops should stop dumpers going over the edge.","3":"Nobody should stand at the edge, because HSE says wheel stops should be in place."},"source_title":"Dumpers","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/dumpers.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-31","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"You drive the site dumper. This morning the brakes feel weaker than usual. What should you do?","options":["Drive slowly and mention it at the end of the week","Report it: a safety fault means the dumper comes out of use until it is put right","Keep using it, but only on flat ground","Pump the brakes harder and carry on"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says drivers should report defects or problems. Problems should be put right quickly, and the dumper taken out of service if the fault is safety critical. Brakes are safety critical.","why_not":{"0":"Brakes are safety critical, so report it now and the dumper comes out of use until it is fixed.","2":"Weak brakes are a safety fault on any ground, so the dumper should come out of use.","3":"Pumping the brakes does not fix the fault, so report it and the dumper is taken out of use."},"source_title":"Dumpers","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/dumpers.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-32","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A telehandler is working with its boom raised. Where does HSE warn there may be a big blind spot for the driver?","options":["Directly above the cab","Only behind the left back wheel","To the front right-hand side","There are no blind spots when the boom is up"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE warns that with the boom raised there may be a big blind spot to the front right-hand side of a telehandler. Seeing behind can also be a big problem. Keep well clear, and make sure the driver knows you are there before you go near.","why_not":{"0":"HSE warns of a big blind spot to the front right-hand side, not above the cab.","1":"It is not only behind: HSE warns of a big blind spot to the front right-hand side when the boom is up.","3":"HSE warns there may be a big blind spot to the front right when the boom is up."},"source_title":"Telescopic handlers","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/telescopic.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-33","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A telehandler driver is driving across site with a pallet of blocks lifted high on the forks, so he can put it straight onto the scaffold when he gets there. What does HSE say?","options":["That is fine if he drives slowly","It is safer, because the load is up out of people's way","It is fine on a flat road","Moving with a raised load is dangerous and should always be avoided"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says moving with a raised load is dangerous and should be avoided at all times. HSE lists it among the precautions against people being struck and telehandlers overturning. Driving slowly or on a flat road does not make it safe.","why_not":{"0":"Driving slowly does not make it safe, because HSE says avoid moving with a raised load at all times.","1":"It is not safer, because HSE says moving with a raised load is dangerous and should always be avoided.","2":"A flat road does not make it safe, because moving with a raised load should always be avoided."},"source_title":"Telescopic handlers","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/telescopic.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-35","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"An excavator is loading soil into a tipper lorry. A labourer climbs into the back of the lorry to spread the soil out as it lands. What is right?","options":["That is fine if he wears a hard hat","He can stay if he keeps to the front end of the body","He must get out: nobody should stay on a vehicle while loose material is loaded unless a safe place of work is provided","It is fine as long as the lorry driver stays in the cab"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The law says nobody should stay on a vehicle while loose material is being loaded or unloaded, unless a safe place of work is provided and kept safe for them. A person in the back of a tipper could be hit by the bucket or the soil.","why_not":{"0":"A hard hat will not stop the bucket or soil hitting him, so he must get out.","1":"The front of the body is still on the vehicle during loading, so he must get out.","3":"Where the driver is does not matter, because nobody should stay in the back while it is loaded."},"source_title":"Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 28 (Vehicles)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/28"},{"id":"safety-b3-37","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"A delivery driver is waiting while a telehandler unloads his lorry. Where should he be?","options":["On the back of the lorry, helping to guide the forks","In a safe place set aside for drivers to wait","Standing right next to the lorry to watch the load","Anywhere, as long as he wears hi-vis"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says there should be a safe place where drivers can wait all through loading and unloading. The loading area should be kept clear of anyone not involved. Hi-vis helps people see you, but it does not keep you out of the way.","why_not":{"0":"He should not be in the loading area, because HSE says there should be a safe place for drivers to wait.","2":"Standing next to the lorry puts him in the way of the load, so he should wait in a safe place.","3":"Hi-vis helps people see you, but it does not keep you out of the way."},"source_title":"Workplace transport safety: A brief guide (INDG199)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg199.pdf"},{"id":"safety-b3-38","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Materials are being stored on top of a site container, and people climb up to stack and collect them. What does HSE say is needed?","options":["A sign saying 'Take care'","Nothing: a container roof is not high enough to matter","Only that the person going up is young and fit","Guard rails, if people could fall while stacking or collecting"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says that if materials are stored at height, for example on top of a container, guard rails must be in place if people could fall while stacking or collecting materials or equipment. A sign does not stop anyone falling.","why_not":{"0":"A sign does not stop anyone falling, so HSE says guard rails are needed.","1":"HSE gives a container roof as its example of where guard rails are needed if people could fall.","2":"Being young and fit does not stop a fall, so guard rails are needed."},"source_title":"Materials storage and waste management","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/storage.htm"},{"id":"safety-b3-39","topic":"Safety","sign":null,"question":"Cardboard packaging and timber offcuts are piling up in a corner of the building you are working in. Why does HSE say waste like this should be cleared away regularly?","options":["To cut the risk of fire","Only to make the site look tidy for visitors","So there is a big enough pile to burn at the end of the week","It does not need clearing until the job is finished"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says all flammable waste, such as packaging and timber offcuts, should be cleared away regularly to reduce fire risks. Clearing it also takes away things people can trip over.","why_not":{"1":"It is not just about looks, because HSE says clearing flammable waste cuts the risk of fire.","2":"Letting a big pile build up raises the fire risk, so clear it away regularly.","3":"HSE says flammable waste should be cleared away regularly, not left until the end."},"source_title":"Materials storage and waste management","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/storage.htm"},{"id":"safety-s08","topic":"Safety","sign":"no-naked-flames","question":"This sign is on the door of the paint store. You have been asked to fix a pipe inside with a gas torch. What do you do?","options":["Use the torch, but keep a fire extinguisher next to you","Do not use the torch in there. Tell your supervisor","Use the torch, because the sign is only about smoking","Use the torch if you leave the door open"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A round sign with a red ring and a red line across means you must NOT do something. The black picture shows what is not allowed. Here it shows a cigarette and a lit match, so smoking and naked flames are both banned. A gas torch has a naked flame. Keeping an extinguisher close or opening the door does not make it OK. Stop and tell your supervisor so the job can be done another way.","why_not":{"0":"An extinguisher fights a fire once it starts; this ban stops one starting.","2":"No smoking alone would show just a cigarette; this adds a lit match.","3":"The match-and-cigarette sign has no open-door exception."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1, Part II, paragraph 3.1 (prohibitory signs)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/schedule/1"},{"id":"safety-s14","topic":"Safety","sign":"wear-eye-protection","question":"This sign is in the area where you are about to use a grinder for half a minute. What must you do?","options":["Put on eye protection before you start","Nothing, because the job is so quick","Wear eye protection only if there is a lot of dust","Keep your face turned away instead"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A round blue sign with a white picture tells you something you MUST do. Here it shows a head wearing goggles, so eye protection must be worn. How short the job is does not matter. Turning your face away is not the same as protecting your eyes.","why_not":{"1":"The goggles sign has no time limit; seconds of grinding still need eyewear.","2":"A dust-only rule would need words on a plate; there are none.","3":"Grinder sparks and fragments fly unpredictably and can reach eyes from the side."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1, Part II, paragraph 3.3 (mandatory signs)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/schedule/1"},{"id":"specialist-b1-01","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You are working in an old building. How can you tell if there are asbestos fibres in the air?","options":["You cannot tell. You cannot see, smell or feel them","The air smells of chemicals","You will see a white cloud of dust","You will start coughing straight away"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Asbestos fibres are far too small to see, and they have no smell. You cannot feel them in the air or on your clothes either. So your senses will not warn you. A smell, a dust cloud or a cough does not tell you whether asbestos is there or not.","why_not":{"1":"Asbestos fibres have no smell. You cannot see, smell or feel them.","2":"Asbestos fibres are too small to see. You cannot tell they are there by looking.","3":"Your body gives no warning. You cannot see, smell or feel asbestos fibres."},"source_title":"HSE: A worker's guide to asbestos safety","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/asbestos/workers.htm"},{"id":"specialist-b1-02","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Your firm has jobs in four buildings. In which one are you most likely to find asbestos?","options":["A school built in 1972","A house built in 2015","A warehouse built in 2021","An office block built in 2008"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Asbestos was used in buildings until it was banned in 1999. Buildings put up after 2000 are unlikely to contain it. The school is the only one here built before 2000, so it is the most likely to have asbestos.","why_not":{"1":"This house was built after 2000. Buildings built after 2000 are unlikely to have asbestos.","2":"This warehouse was built after 2000. Buildings built after 2000 are unlikely to have asbestos.","3":"This office was built after 2000. Buildings built after 2000 are unlikely to have asbestos."},"source_title":"HSE: Introduction to asbestos safety: Overview","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/asbestos/introduction/index.htm"},{"id":"specialist-b1-04","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"An asbestos survey was done before your job started. It found no asbestos in the room you are working in. What should you keep in mind?","options":["Asbestos could still be hidden, so stay alert and stop if you find a material you are unsure about","The room is now certain to be free of asbestos","You can drill and cut anywhere in the room without thinking about asbestos","If you find a material you are unsure about, carry on, because the survey is always right"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A survey may not find every place where there is asbestos. Some can be hidden inside walls, floors or ceilings. So keep watching as you work. If you find a material you think may contain asbestos, stop work and tell your employer.","why_not":{"1":"A survey might not find all the asbestos. Some could still be hidden in the room.","2":"Asbestos could still be hidden, even after a survey. Keep watching for it as you work.","3":"Surveys can miss asbestos. If you find a material you are unsure about, stop work."},"source_title":"HSE: A worker's guide to asbestos safety","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/asbestos/workers.htm"},{"id":"specialist-b1-05","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You are digging by hand to uncover a buried electricity cable. How should you use your spade?","options":["Ease it into the ground gently with your foot","Stab it down hard so it cuts through stones","Drop it into the ground with force so it bites deep","Change to a pick so you can dig faster in the soft soil"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Near buried cables, use a spade or shovel (insulated, and ideally with a curved edge) and push it in gently with your foot. Stabbing or dropping it in hard could cut straight into the cable. HSE says picks should not be used in soft soil near buried services.","why_not":{"1":"Stabbing the spade in hard could cut into the cable. Ease it in gently with your foot.","2":"Dropping the spade in with force could cut the cable. HSE says ease it in gently.","3":"HSE says picks should not be used in soft soil near buried services."},"source_title":"Avoiding danger from underground services, HSG47 (HSE), paragraph 110","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg47.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b1-06","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You are in a trench where a gas pipe has been uncovered. How should you climb out?","options":["Use the proper way out, and do not stand on or hold the pipe","Step on the pipe, because it is strong enough","Hold the pipe to pull yourself up","Stand on the pipe only if it is made of metal"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Pipes and cables that have been uncovered must never be used as a step or a handhold. Your weight can damage them, and a damaged gas pipe can leak. Use the ladder or other safe way out of the trench.","why_not":{"1":"Uncovered pipes must never be used as a foothold. Your weight could damage the pipe.","2":"Uncovered pipes must never be used as a handhold. Use the proper way out instead.","3":"No uncovered pipe should be used as a foothold, whatever it is made of."},"source_title":"Avoiding danger from underground services, HSG47 (HSE), paragraph 115","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg47.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b1-08","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You drive a tipper lorry on site. Your route goes under an overhead power line, through a crossing marked with goalposts. What must you do?","options":["Lower the tipper body and keep below the height of the goalpost crossbar","Drive through fast so you spend less time under the line","Raise the tipper body a little so the load does not slide off","Drive round the side of the goalposts to save time"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Goalposts mark the safe place to cross under the line. Signs at the crossing give the height of the crossbar. Drivers must lower jibs, booms and tipper bodies and stay below that height while crossing. Driving round the goalposts takes you out of the safe crossing.","why_not":{"1":"Speed does not keep you safe. You must lower the tipper and stay below the crossbar height.","2":"A raised tipper could reach the line. Drivers must lower tipper bodies when crossing.","3":"The goalposts mark the safe crossing. Going round them takes you out of it."},"source_title":"HSE: Avoiding danger from overhead power lines (GS6), paragraph 21","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/gs6.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b1-10","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A signaller is guiding a machine. Suddenly the signaller raises both arms straight up, with the palms facing forwards. What does this mean?","options":["Danger: emergency stop","Start: get ready for my signals","Raise the load","The job is finished"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In the law on safety signs and signals, both arms up with the palms facing forwards is the danger signal: stop at once. Start is both arms held out to the sides. Raise is the right arm pointing up and moving in a slow circle. Job finished is both hands held together at chest height.","why_not":{"1":"Start is shown with both arms held out to the sides, not raised up.","2":"Raise is shown with the right arm pointing up, making a slow circle.","3":"Job finished is shown with both hands held together at chest height."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1, Part IX, paragraph 3","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/schedule/1"},{"id":"specialist-b1-11","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You are driving a machine and a signaller is guiding you. The signaller gives you a signal that you cannot follow safely. What should you do?","options":["Stop the move and ask for new instructions","Follow the signal anyway, because the signaller is in charge","Carry on in the way you think is best, without telling anyone","Follow the signal, but do it slowly"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law says the operator must stop the move and ask for new instructions when they cannot carry out an order safely. The signaller guides you, but you must not do something you know is unsafe. Doing it slowly is still unsafe, and changing the plan without telling the signaller puts people in danger.","why_not":{"1":"If you cannot follow a signal safely, the law says you must stop and ask for new instructions.","2":"Do not go your own way. Stop the move and ask the signaller for new instructions.","3":"Doing it slowly is still unsafe. You must stop the move and ask for new instructions."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1, Part IX, paragraph 2.5","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/schedule/1"},{"id":"specialist-b1-12","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"The law on safety signs and signals says how the alarm that tells people to evacuate must sound. What does it say?","options":["It must be continuous (it keeps sounding without a break)","It must be three short beeps, then silence","It must be one short beep","It must be a quiet tone so it does not scare people"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The Safety Signs and Signals Regulations say the signal to evacuate must be continuous. Alarm sounds must also be much louder than the noise around them, so a quiet tone is wrong. Your site induction should tell you what your own site's alarm sounds like.","why_not":{"1":"The law says the evacuation signal must be continuous, not beeps with gaps.","2":"One short beep is not continuous. The evacuation signal must keep sounding.","3":"Alarms must be much louder than the noise around them, so people can hear them."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1, Part VII","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/schedule/1"},{"id":"specialist-b1-14","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You are about to cut blocks indoors. The dust might set off a smoke detector in the room. What should you do?","options":["Keep the dust down or extract it, and leave the detector working","Put a bag over the detector until you finish","Take the detector down for the day","Tell everyone to ignore the alarm if it goes off"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says it is not acceptable to cover or switch off smoke detectors because work makes dust. A covered detector cannot warn anyone of a real fire. Control the dust instead, for example with water or extraction. Ignoring an alarm is never safe.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says covering smoke detectors because of dust is not acceptable. Keep the dust down instead.","2":"Taking the detector down means it cannot warn of a real fire. Control the dust instead.","3":"Ignoring an alarm is never safe. It could be a real fire."},"source_title":"HSE: Fire safety in construction (HSG168), paragraph 283","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg168.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b1-15","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You have been given a permit to work for a dangerous job. Which is true?","options":["The permit alone does not make the job safe. You still have to carry out the precautions it sets out","Once you have the permit, the job is safe","The permit means you can do the job in any way you like","A permit is only paperwork, so you do not need to read it"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A permit to work sets out how a dangerous job must be done safely and who may do it. Having the paper does not make the job safe. The job is only safe when the people preparing, supervising and doing the work carry out the precautions.","why_not":{"1":"A permit does not make a job safe by itself. The precautions must be carried out.","2":"A permit sets out how the job must be done safely. You must follow it.","3":"A permit is not just paperwork. You must read it and carry out its precautions."},"source_title":"HSE: Guidance on permit-to-work systems (HSG250), paragraph 5","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg250.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b1-18","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Which of these jobs counts as hot work?","options":["Cutting metal with a disc cutter (grinder)","Painting a wall with a roller","Laying paving slabs","Digging a trench with a spade"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Hot work is any work that makes flames, sparks or heat. Grinding and cutting metal throws out hot sparks that can start a fire, so it is hot work. Welding, torch-on roofing and using heat guns are hot work too.","why_not":{"1":"Painting with a roller makes no flames, sparks or heat, so it is not hot work.","2":"Laying slabs makes no flames, sparks or heat, so it is not hot work.","3":"Digging with a spade makes no flames, sparks or heat, so it is not hot work."},"source_title":"HSE: Fire safety in construction (HSG168), paragraphs 118 and 120","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg168.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b1-19","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You have just finished welding on site. How long should someone keep a constant watch on the area for fire?","options":["At least an hour after the hot work ends","Five minutes","Until you have packed your tools away","No watch is needed if you cannot see any flames"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Materials can smoulder for a long time before they burst into flames. HSE says someone should watch the area all the time for at least an hour after hot work ends, then check it again two hours after. That is why hot work should finish at least two hours before the end of the working day.","why_not":{"1":"Five minutes is far too short. Watch the area for at least an hour after hot work.","2":"Packing up is too quick. Things can smoulder, so watch for at least an hour.","3":"Things can smoulder without flames. Someone should watch for at least an hour after hot work."},"source_title":"HSE: Fire safety in construction (HSG168), paragraphs 122 and 123","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg168.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b1-22","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You will be working on your own in a far corner of a large site all afternoon. What should be agreed before you start?","options":["How and when you will keep in touch, for example check-in calls at set times","Nothing, as long as your phone is in your bag","That you will only call if you finish early","That someone will look for you the next morning if you are missing"],"correct":0,"explanation":"When you work alone, nobody may be near to help if something goes wrong. HSE says there should be regular contact at times agreed in advance, for example by phone or radio, and a sure way to check that you got back safely when the work is done. Just having a phone is not enough if nobody is expecting to hear from you.","why_not":{"1":"A phone in your bag is not enough. Agree set times to keep in touch.","2":"You need regular contact at agreed times, not just one call if you finish early.","3":"Next morning is far too late. Agree regular contact and a check that you got back safely."},"source_title":"HSE: Protecting lone workers: How to manage the risks of working alone (INDG73)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg73.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b1-23","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A workmate has a headache and looks in the site first-aid box for painkillers. What does HSE recommend about tablets and medicines?","options":["They should not be kept in the first-aid box","Every first-aid box must have painkillers in it","Only the site manager may put them in the box","They may be kept in the box if they are in date"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE recommends that tablets and medicines are not kept in the first-aid box. A first-aid box holds things like plasters, dressings, bandages, eye pads and disposable gloves. There is no fixed list in law of what every box must hold; it depends on the risks of the work.","why_not":{"1":"There is no fixed list for the box, and HSE advises against keeping tablets in it.","2":"HSE recommends that tablets and medicines are not kept in the first-aid box at all.","3":"Being in date makes no difference. HSE advises not keeping medicines in the box."},"source_title":"HSE: First aid at work: Your questions answered (INDG214), Q4","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg214.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b1-24","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You are new on site. How would you usually find out who the first-aiders are and where the first-aid box is?","options":["From notices your employer puts up that show this","You only find out after an accident happens","You do not need to know. Only the first-aiders need to know","From the HSE website"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Your employer has to tell you about the first-aid arrangements. Notices showing who and where the first-aiders are, and where the first-aid box is, are usually enough. If you have trouble reading English, your employer should give you this information in another way.","why_not":{"1":"Your employer must tell you about first aid before anything goes wrong, usually with notices.","2":"Everyone needs to know. Your employer must tell all workers about the first-aid arrangements.","3":"Each site has its own first-aiders. Your employer tells you, usually with notices on site."},"source_title":"HSE: First aid at work: Your questions answered (INDG214), Q9","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg214.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b1-25","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A small site has no trained first-aider, but it has an 'appointed person' for first aid. What is the appointed person's job?","options":["To look after the first-aid equipment and call the emergency services when needed","To give full first-aid treatment, the same as a trained first-aider","To decide whether injured people can carry on working","To treat injuries with medicines from the first-aid box"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An appointed person takes charge of the first-aid arrangements. They look after the first-aid equipment and call the emergency services when needed. They do not need first-aid training, so they are not the same as a trained first-aider.","why_not":{"1":"An appointed person does not need first-aid training, so they are not a trained first-aider.","2":"Their job is to look after first-aid kit and call for help, not to judge who can work.","3":"An appointed person needs no first-aid training. Their job is the kit and calling for help."},"source_title":"HSE: First aid at work: Your questions answered (INDG214), Q5","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg214.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-01","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A workmate says: 'I breathed in some asbestos dust last year and I still feel fine, so it did me no harm.' What is true?","options":["Asbestos diseases usually take many years (often decades) to show, and they cannot be cured","If you still feel fine after a year, you are safe","Asbestos only harms you if you feel ill on the same day","Asbestos diseases are easy to cure if a doctor finds them early"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Feeling well now proves nothing. The diseases caused by breathing asbestos fibres usually take decades to develop, and by then they cannot be cured. That is why you must never take chances with asbestos dust, even if nobody seems to get ill.","why_not":{"1":"Asbestos diseases usually take decades to show, so feeling fine after a year proves nothing.","2":"Asbestos harm does not show on the same day. The diseases usually take decades to develop.","3":"Asbestos diseases cannot be cured."},"source_title":"HSE: A worker's guide to asbestos safety","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/asbestos/workers.htm"},{"id":"specialist-b2-02","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"The ceiling in a room you are painting is made of asbestos board. It is in good condition, and your job does not touch it. Which is true?","options":["The risk is low while it is left alone. The danger comes if it is drilled, broken or damaged","It gives off harmful fibres all the time, so nobody can ever work in the room","Because it is in good condition, it is safe to drill a few small holes in it","You should break it down and bag it up, to make the room safe"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Asbestos that is in good condition and left alone is a low risk. Fibres are released when it is disturbed, for example drilled, cut, broken or damaged, and that is when people breathe them in. So leave it alone, and never drill or break it yourself.","why_not":{"1":"Asbestos in good condition that is left alone is a low risk. The danger comes when it is disturbed.","2":"Drilling disturbs the asbestos and releases fibres, even if it is in good condition.","3":"Breaking it up releases fibres. Left alone and in good condition, the risk is low."},"source_title":"HSE: A worker's guide to asbestos safety","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/asbestos/workers.htm"},{"id":"specialist-b2-04","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"The plan of buried services shows a cable 1 metre down. Your hole only needs to be 40 cm deep. What should you think?","options":["Plans are not always accurate, and the ground level may have changed, so the cable could be much nearer the surface","The cable must be well below your hole, so you can dig any way you like","Cables are never less than 1 metre deep","The plan is an official record, so it is always right"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Service plans help, but they are not always drawn accurately, and you should not trust them for distances or depths. The ground may also have been raised or lowered since the plan was made. A cable shown at 1 metre could be much shallower, so you still locate it and dig with care.","why_not":{"1":"Plans are not always accurate, so the cable could be much shallower. Dig with care.","2":"There is no such rule. A cable can be much nearer the surface than 1 metre.","3":"Plans are not always drawn accurately. HSE says do not rely on them for depths."},"source_title":"Avoiding danger from underground services, HSG47 (HSE), paragraph 41","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg47.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-06","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"There is some yellow cable warning tape left over after backfilling a trench. A workmate wants to tie it round another open trench as a barrier. What is right?","options":["Do not use it as a barrier. Warning tape is only for laying back above buried services","Use it, because yellow tape is easy to see","Use it, but only overnight","Throw it into the trench when it is backfilled, to get rid of it"],"correct":0,"explanation":"When a trench is backfilled, warning tape and tiles go back above the services they mark. The tape must not be used for anything else, such as guarding a trench, and it must not be thrown into a trench while backfilling. Tape in the wrong place could fool someone digging later.","why_not":{"1":"Warning tape is only for marking buried services. It must not be used to guard a trench.","2":"Warning tape must not be used for anything else, at any time of day.","3":"HSE says do not throw warning tape into a trench when backfilling."},"source_title":"Avoiding danger from underground services, HSG47 (HSE), paragraph 130","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg47.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-08","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You have to carry a long ladder across ground where there are overhead power lines. How should you carry it?","options":["Flat (horizontal) and low, close to the ground","Upright, because it is easier to hold","On your shoulder, with the front end pointing up so it clears people's heads","Upright, but walk fast under the wires"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Carry long things such as ladders, poles and tubes flat and close to the ground near overhead lines. Then no part of them can reach up towards the wires. Carried upright or pointing up, the top end could reach the wires, or get close enough for electricity to jump across.","why_not":{"1":"Upright, the top of the ladder could reach the wires. Carry it flat and low.","2":"Pointing the end up could bring it near the wires. Carry it flat and close to the ground.","3":"Walking fast does not help. An upright ladder could still reach the wires."},"source_title":"HSE: Avoiding danger from overhead power lines (GS6), paragraph 25","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/gs6.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-09","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"On site you see a low beam painted with yellow and black stripes set at an angle. What do the stripes tell you?","options":["Take care: there is a danger here, such as something you could walk into, a drop, or something that could fall","This is a safe place to stand","This is the way to the fire exit","This area is for vehicles only"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Slanting yellow and black stripes (or red and white ones) mark places where you could hit an obstacle, fall, or be hit by something falling. A low beam is marked this way so people see it and do not walk into it.","why_not":{"1":"These stripes warn of danger, like hitting something or falling. They do not mark a safe place.","2":"Escape routes are shown with green signs, not yellow and black stripes.","3":"The stripes warn of a risk of hitting something or falling, not a vehicle area."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1, Part V, paragraph 1","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/schedule/1"},{"id":"specialist-b2-10","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You are the signaller guiding a crane lift. Halfway through, a workmate asks you to help him carry some boards. What should you do?","options":["Say no. While you are signalling, your only job is to direct the lift and keep people nearby safe","Help him quickly. The crane driver can wait with the load in the air","Help him, and keep giving signals with your free hand","Tell the crane driver to carry on without signals until you come back"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law says a signaller's duties must be only directing the moves and keeping the workers nearby safe. If you stop watching to do another job, nobody is guiding the load. Finish the lift first, or let a different person help with the boards.","why_not":{"1":"A signaller's only job is directing the lift and keeping workers safe. Do not leave it.","2":"You cannot guide the lift properly while doing another job. Directing it must be your only task.","3":"Nobody would be guiding the load. Your only job is directing the lift until it is done."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1, Part IX, paragraph 2.3","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/schedule/1"},{"id":"specialist-b2-11","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Workers in a very noisy area wear ear defenders and may not hear the alarm. What does the law on safety signs and signals say?","options":["Extra measures must be taken, such as adding flashing lights, so the warning still reaches them","Nothing. It is up to them to listen harder","They should take their ear defenders off so they can hear the alarm","The alarm can be switched off in that area"],"correct":0,"explanation":"If workers cannot hear or see a sign or signal properly, for example because of the PPE they wear, the law says extra measures must be taken to back it up or replace it. Flashing lights can be used together with an alarm, so people who cannot hear it still get the warning. Taking ear defenders off would expose them to the noise.","why_not":{"1":"The law says extra measures must be taken when PPE stops workers hearing a signal.","2":"Taking ear defenders off would expose them to the noise. The law says add extra measures instead.","3":"Switching the alarm off would leave them with no warning. Extra measures must be added instead."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1, Part I, paragraphs 3.2 and 11","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/schedule/1"},{"id":"specialist-b2-12","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Why do construction sites hold practice evacuations (emergency drills)?","options":["The law says the emergency plan must be tested by putting it into practice from time to time","To check which workers are slow","Only because the insurance company asks for them","To give workers a break, so it does not matter what you do"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Sites must have plans for dealing with emergencies, including getting everyone off site. The law says everyone must know the plan, and the plan must be tested by actually carrying it out at suitable times. A drill shows whether people know the way out and the plan works, so treat it like the real thing.","why_not":{"1":"Drills are to test the emergency plan, not to check who is slow.","2":"The law itself says the emergency plan must be tested by putting it into practice.","3":"A drill tests the emergency plan, so treat it like the real thing."},"source_title":"The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 30(3)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/30/made"},{"id":"specialist-b2-13","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"The escape route out of a basement on site has no daylight. What may the law require on this route?","options":["Emergency lighting, so the route can still be used if the normal lights go out","Nothing, because workers can use the torch on their phones","Nothing, as long as the route is short","A notice telling people to stay put if the lights go out"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An emergency route must be usable at any time. Where needed, such as a route with no daylight, it must have emergency lighting that keeps working if the power fails. You cannot count on everyone having a phone torch in a fire or power cut.","why_not":{"1":"You cannot count on phone torches. Where needed, the law requires emergency lighting on escape routes.","2":"A short route must still be usable at any time, so it may need emergency lighting.","3":"Escape routes must be usable at any time. Emergency lighting helps people get out, not stay put."},"source_title":"The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 31(4)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/31/made"},{"id":"specialist-b2-14","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A fire door on a staircase keeps swinging shut while you carry materials through. What should you do?","options":["Let it close. Fire doors must be kept shut and never wedged open","Wedge it open with a block until you finish","Take it off its hinges for the day","Tie it open, as long as the fire alarm is working"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Fire doors stop fire and smoke spreading, and they protect the stairs people use to escape. They only work when they are closed, so they must never be wedged or tied open. If a door has to stay open, it needs a special closer that lets it shut by itself when the alarm sounds.","why_not":{"1":"Fire doors must be kept closed. HSE says never wedge them open.","2":"A fire door off its hinges cannot stop fire and smoke. Keep fire doors closed.","3":"A working alarm does not change this. Fire doors must be kept shut."},"source_title":"HSE: Fire safety in construction (HSG168), paragraph 245","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg168.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-15","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Your job needs a permit to work. The permit is still being written, and someone suggests you make a start to save time. What should you do?","options":["Wait. Do not start until the permit has been issued, you understand it, and its precautions are in place","Start the easy parts now and finish when the permit arrives","Start, as long as you sign the permit later","Start, because the permit is only paperwork"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A permit to work controls dangerous jobs. You must not start the job until the permit has been properly authorised and issued, you understand what it says, and the precautions it needs have been taken. Starting early means working without those protections.","why_not":{"1":"Do not start any part of the job until the permit is issued and understood.","2":"The permit must be issued and its precautions in place before you start, not signed later.","3":"A permit is not just paperwork. Starting without it means working without its protections."},"source_title":"HSE: Guidance on permit-to-work systems (HSG250), paragraph 40","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg250.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-18","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You have a hot-work permit for cutting steel in one area this morning. After lunch you are asked to weld in a different part of the site. Can you use the same permit?","options":["No. A hot-work permit covers one set job, in one place, for a set time. You need a new one","Yes. Once you have a hot-work permit, you can do hot work anywhere on site","Yes, as long as it is still the same day","Yes, if you write the new place on it yourself"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A hot-work permit is for a clearly set piece of work, at one place, for a set time. It ends when that job is done or the time runs out. It must never be a free pass to do hot work anywhere on site at any time, because each place has its own fire risks.","why_not":{"1":"A hot-work permit is never a free pass to do hot work anywhere on site.","2":"The permit is for one job in one place. A new place needs a new permit.","3":"You cannot change a permit yourself. A new job in a new place needs a new permit."},"source_title":"HSE: Fire safety in construction (HSG168), paragraph 128","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg168.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-20","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Your overalls are covered in dust after cutting. A workmate says you can blow it off quickly with the oxygen hose from the cutting set. What should you do?","options":["Refuse. Clothes with oxygen in them catch fire very easily and burn fiercely","Do it, as long as the cutting torch is turned off","Do it, as long as your overalls are fire-retardant","Do it outside, where the oxygen will blow away"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Never use oxygen to blow dust off clothes. Clothing with oxygen in it catches fire easily and burns very fiercely, even fire-retardant clothing. One spark later on could set you alight.","why_not":{"1":"Turning off the torch does not help. Clothes with oxygen in them can catch fire easily later.","2":"Even fire-retardant clothing burns fiercely when it has oxygen in it.","3":"Never use oxygen to blow dust off clothes, wherever you are."},"source_title":"HSE: Safety in gas welding, cutting and similar processes (INDG297), Fire/burns from misuse of oxygen","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg297.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-21","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You have been asked to do a small job on your own. Your workmate says working alone is against the law. Is he right?","options":["No. Working alone is often safe, but the employer must think about the risks and deal with them before you start","Yes. It is always against the law to work alone","No. There are no rules at all about working alone","Yes, unless the job takes less than an hour"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law does not ban working alone, and it is often safe. But before anyone works alone, the employer must think about the risks and deal with them, for example how you will keep in touch and get help. Some very risky jobs may need another person there.","why_not":{"1":"Working alone is not against the law, and it is often safe.","2":"There are rules. The employer must think about the risks of working alone and deal with them.","3":"Time is not the test. Working alone is not banned, but the risks must be dealt with first."},"source_title":"HSE: Protecting lone workers: How to manage the risks of working alone (INDG73), Is it legal to work alone and is it safe?","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg73.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-22","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"It is your first week in a new job. You are asked to work alone at a far-off site. What does HSE advise?","options":["New workers may need to have someone with them at first, before they work alone","New workers should work alone straight away, so they learn faster","It is fine as long as you have a phone, whatever your experience","Once you have done a site induction, you never need anyone with you"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says lone workers may need someone with them at first if they are new to the job, still being trained, doing a job with special risks, or facing something new. Working alone needs enough experience to understand the risks and what to do if something goes wrong.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says new workers may need someone with them at first, before working alone.","2":"A phone is not enough. Being new to the job may mean you need someone with you at first.","3":"An induction is not enough. New workers or new situations may need someone with them."},"source_title":"HSE: Protecting lone workers: How to manage the risks of working alone (INDG73), Supervision","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg73.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-23","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You find a workmate lying still on the floor next to a damaged electric cable. What should you do first?","options":["Check that you will not be in danger yourself, and make the area safe before you go near","Grab him straight away and pull him away from the cable","Throw water over him to wake him up","Walk away and report it at the next break"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In an emergency, first make sure you do not put yourself in danger, and make the area safe. Here, that means the electricity must be off before anyone touches him. Then check the casualty and send for help without delay. If you are hurt too, there are two casualties and nobody to help.","why_not":{"1":"He may still be touching live electricity. Make sure the area is safe before you touch him.","2":"Throwing water near a damaged cable could put you in danger. Make the area safe first.","3":"Do not delay. Make the area safe, check him and send for help straight away."},"source_title":"HSE: Basic advice on first aid at work (INDG347), Priorities","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg347.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-24","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A chemical splashes into a workmate's eye. How long should the eye be rinsed with clean water?","options":["For at least 10 minutes, while gently holding the eyelids open","For a few seconds, until it stops stinging","Do not use water. Rub the eye clean instead","For one minute, then go back to work"],"correct":0,"explanation":"If a chemical gets in the eye, flush it with clean water (or sterile eyewash) for at least 10 minutes, holding the eyelids gently open. Then the casualty should hold a pad over the eye and go to hospital. All eye injuries can be serious.","why_not":{"1":"A few seconds is far too short. Rinse the eye for at least 10 minutes.","2":"Rubbing is wrong. Flush the eye with clean water for at least 10 minutes.","3":"One minute is too short. Rinse the eye for at least 10 minutes."},"source_title":"HSE: Basic advice on first aid at work (INDG347), Eye injuries","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg347.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b2-25","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A worker has fallen and may have hurt his back or neck. He is not in any more danger where he is lying. What should you do?","options":["Do not move him, and get expert help","Sit him up so he can breathe more easily","Help him walk to the site cabin","Lift him onto a pallet and carry him out"],"correct":0,"explanation":"If someone may have a broken bone or an injury to the back or neck (spine), get expert help. Do not move them unless they are in immediate danger where they are. Moving them could make the injury much worse.","why_not":{"1":"Sitting him up moves him and could make a back or neck injury worse.","2":"Do not move him unless he is in danger. Walking could make the injury much worse.","3":"Do not move him unless he is in immediate danger. Get expert help instead."},"source_title":"HSE: Basic advice on first aid at work (INDG347), Broken bones and spinal injuries","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg347.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-01","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You find an old grey board while stripping out a room. A workmate says: 'Asbestos is always white or brown, so grey board is fine.' What is true?","options":["You cannot tell if something is asbestos just by its colour","Grey board never has asbestos in it","Only white board can have asbestos in it","If it is painted, it cannot be asbestos"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Asbestos comes in more than one colour, and it is often mixed with other materials that change how it looks. So the colour tells you nothing for certain. If you are not sure, it needs to be checked.","why_not":{"1":"Colour does not tell you. Asbestos can be mixed with other materials that change how it looks.","2":"You cannot tell asbestos by its colour. It can look many different ways.","3":"Paint proves nothing. You cannot tell if something is asbestos just by how it looks."},"source_title":"HSE asbestos essentials em1: What to do if you discover or accidentally disturb asbestos during your work","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/guidance/em1.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-03","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You accidentally break an old board that may contain asbestos. A little dust lands on your sleeves. What does HSE advise?","options":["Stay where you are, put on your mask if you can, wipe your outer clothes with a damp cloth, then take them off before you move away","Walk to the welfare cabin straight away and brush the dust off there","Shake your jacket hard so the dust falls off","Carry on working, because a little dust does not matter"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Moving around or shaking your clothes spreads the dust to other places and other people. HSE says stay put, avoid breathing the dust, put on your mask if possible, damp-wipe your outer clothes, and take them off before you move away.","why_not":{"1":"Walking away spreads the dust. HSE says stay put and take off outer clothes before moving away.","2":"Shaking your jacket throws dust into the air. Damp-wipe your outer clothes instead.","3":"Even a little dust matters. Stay put, damp-wipe your clothes and take them off before moving."},"source_title":"HSE asbestos essentials em1: What to do if you discover or accidentally disturb asbestos during your work","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/guidance/em1.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-04","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You are trained for a small non-licensed asbestos job. There are no proper respirators left, so a workmate says to wear two ordinary dust masks, one on top of the other. What does HSE say?","options":["Two dust masks are useless. You need a proper respirator of the right type","Two masks give double protection, so it is fine","It is fine as long as the job is short","One dust mask is enough if you wet the material first"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An ordinary dust mask does not protect you from asbestos, and wearing two of them does not fix that. You need a proper respirator of the type HSE says is suitable for asbestos work. If you have not got one, do not start.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says doubled-up dust masks are useless. You need a proper respirator.","2":"A short job does not change it. Dust masks are useless for asbestos; you need a respirator.","3":"A dust mask is not suitable, even with wetting. You need a proper respirator."},"source_title":"HSE asbestos essentials em6: Personal protective equipment (including RPE)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/guidance/em6.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-06","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"After a fire in an old building, there is a lot of broken asbestos material on the floor. You are not a licensed asbestos contractor. Your boss asks you to clear it up. What is right?","options":["Do not do it. Clearing up a large amount of asbestos debris is work for a licensed contractor","Do it, as long as you wet it first","Do it, because the fire will have made the asbestos safe","Do it, as long as you finish before anyone else arrives"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Clearing up a lot of asbestos debris, for example after a fire or a flood, is higher-risk work. Only a contractor licensed by HSE may do it. If you are ever unsure whether a job needs a licence, treat it as if it does and stop until you are told otherwise.","why_not":{"1":"Wetting it does not make it allowed. Large amounts of asbestos debris need a licensed contractor.","2":"Fire does not make asbestos safe. Clearing up after a fire is licensed work.","3":"Timing does not matter. Clearing large amounts of asbestos debris is work for a licensed contractor."},"source_title":"HSE: A worker's guide to asbestos safety","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/asbestos/workers.htm"},{"id":"specialist-b3-08","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A buried cable has been found under a grass verge. You need to mark its line on the ground. What should you use?","options":["Wooden pegs, put in to one side of the cable's line","Long steel pins, hammered in right on top of the cable","Metal spikes, because they are easy to see","Nothing, because everyone will remember where it is"],"correct":0,"explanation":"On grass or bare ground, mark the line with wooden pegs, set a little to one side of the cable. Steel pins, spikes or long pegs could go deep enough to hit a cable that is near the surface.","why_not":{"1":"Steel pins could hit a cable near the surface. Use wooden pegs to one side.","2":"Metal spikes could damage a shallow cable. HSE says use wooden pegs on grass.","3":"The line of the cable must be marked. On grass, use wooden pegs to one side."},"source_title":"HSE HSG47: Avoiding danger from underground services","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg47.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-09","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A cable runs under a concrete path. You need to break up the concrete with a hand-held breaker. Where it can be done, how far from the marked line of the cable should you keep the breaker?","options":["At least 500 mm (half a metre)","At least 50 mm","At least 150 mm","There is no need to keep away, because the concrete protects the cable"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Breaking up hard surfaces with hand-held power tools often leads to cable strikes. Where you can, keep the tool at least 500 mm from the marked line. Then find the cable by careful hand digging under the surface.","why_not":{"1":"50 mm is far too close. Where you can, keep the breaker at least 500 mm away.","2":"150 mm is too close. Where you can, keep the breaker at least 500 mm away.","3":"Breaking up hard surfaces often causes cable strikes. Keep at least 500 mm away where you can."},"source_title":"HSE HSG47: Avoiding danger from underground services","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg47.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-11","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You uncover an old iron pipe in a trench. Nobody is sure whether it carries water or gas. How should you treat it until you know?","options":["As a gas pipe","As a water pipe, because most old pipes carry water","As an empty pipe, because it looks old","As safe to cut, because gas is never carried in iron pipes"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Iron and steel water pipes can look just like gas pipes. Until it is known for certain what the pipe is, treat it as a gas pipe.","why_not":{"1":"Iron water pipes and gas pipes can look the same. Treat it as gas until you know.","2":"Looking old proves nothing. Treat a pipe like this as a gas pipe.","3":"Gas can be carried in iron or steel pipes. Treat it as a gas pipe until you know."},"source_title":"HSE HSG47: Avoiding danger from underground services","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg47.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-12","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A high-voltage overhead line crosses the site. Its wires seem to have a thin plastic coating. How should you treat them?","options":["As if they were bare wires with no insulation","As insulated, so it is safe to touch them","As safe, as long as you wear gloves","As dead, because coated wires do not carry power"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Most high-voltage lines are bare, but some have a light plastic coating. That coating does not make them safe. HSE says to treat every high-voltage line as if it had no insulation at all.","why_not":{"1":"A thin coating does not make it safe. Treat all high-voltage lines as bare wires.","2":"Gloves do not make it safe. Treat all high-voltage lines as if they have no insulation.","3":"Coated lines can still carry power. Treat all high-voltage lines as bare wires."},"source_title":"HSE GS6: Avoiding danger from overhead power lines","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/gs6.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-13","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Your work will be near an overhead power line. HSE says the risks must be managed if you work within a certain distance of the line, measured along the ground from below the nearest wire. What is that distance?","options":["10 metres","2 metres","4 metres","Only when you are right underneath the wires"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says to manage the risks for any work within 10 metres of the line, measured along the ground from below the nearest wire. This applies even to short or temporary jobs.","why_not":{"1":"2 metres is too short. HSE says manage the risks within 10 metres of the line.","2":"4 metres is too short. HSE says manage the risks within 10 metres of the line.","3":"You do not need to be underneath. Risks must be managed for work within 10 metres."},"source_title":"HSE GS6: Avoiding danger from overhead power lines","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/gs6.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-15","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Your supervisor needs to know how high an overhead power line is above the ground. How should it be measured?","options":["By a trained person, using a measuring device that does not touch the wires","By holding a long tape measure up to the wires","By raising a scaffold pole until it nearly touches the wires","By guessing from the ground and adding a bit to be safe"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Anything held up towards the wires could touch them or get close enough for electricity to jump across. The height should be measured by someone trained to do it, with a device that does not need to touch the line.","why_not":{"1":"Holding a tape up to the wires is dangerous. A trained person should use a non-contact device.","2":"A pole raised near the wires could touch them. The height must be measured without touching.","3":"Guessing is not good enough. A trained person should measure it with a non-contact device."},"source_title":"HSE GS6: Avoiding danger from overhead power lines","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/gs6.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-16","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A warning light on site can either shine steadily or flash. What does it mean when it flashes instead of shining steadily?","options":["There is more danger, or a more urgent need to act","The danger is over","The bulb is about to fail","There is less danger than when it shines steadily"],"correct":0,"explanation":"When a light can do both, the law says the flashing signal is used for the higher danger, or when action is needed more urgently. A steady light means a lower level of warning.","why_not":{"1":"A flashing light means more danger, not that the danger is over.","2":"Flashing is a set signal meaning higher danger. It is not about the bulb.","3":"It is the other way round. Flashing means more danger than a steady light."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/schedule/1"},{"id":"specialist-b3-17","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A signaller will guide a lorry driver using spoken instructions over a radio. The driver does not understand the signaller's language well. What does the law on safety signs and signals say?","options":["The people using spoken signals must know the language well enough to say and understand the messages correctly","It does not matter, as long as the radio is loud enough","The driver can guess what is meant from the signaller's tone of voice","Spoken signals can be given by anyone, in any language"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Spoken signals only work if both people understand them properly. The law says everyone involved must know the language well enough to say and understand the messages, so they can act safely.","why_not":{"1":"Loud is not enough. Both people must know the language well enough to understand the messages.","2":"Guessing is not safe. The law says they must know the language well.","3":"The law says people using spoken signals must have a good knowledge of the language used."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/schedule/1"},{"id":"specialist-b3-19","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A signaller is guiding a crane. The load has to go behind a building, where the signaller cannot see it from a safe spot. What should happen?","options":["Another signaller should be used, so the whole move can be seen safely","The signaller should guess where the load is","The crane driver should carry on without any signals","The signaller should stand under the load to get a better view"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The signaller must be able to see the whole move without being put in danger. If one person cannot do that, one or more extra signallers should be used.","why_not":{"1":"Guessing is not safe. The signaller must be able to see the whole move.","2":"Moving with no signals is unsafe. Another signaller should be used.","3":"Standing under the load puts the signaller in danger. They must watch from a safe spot."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/schedule/1"},{"id":"specialist-b3-22","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"How often does HSE say a site fire alarm should be tested by a named, competent person, to check it works and can be heard in the work areas?","options":["Every week","Once a year","Only when it is first put in","Only after a real fire"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A fire alarm is no use if it does not work or cannot be heard where people are working. HSE says it should be tested every week by a named, competent person, as well as after it is put in or added to.","why_not":{"1":"Once a year is not enough. HSE says test the fire alarm every week.","2":"Testing only once is not enough. HSE says test it every week.","3":"Waiting for a real fire is too late. HSE says test it every week."},"source_title":"HSE HSG168: Fire safety in construction","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg168.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-23","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"To stop theft, someone has padlocked the fire exit door of a building where people are still working. What does HSE say about fire exit doors?","options":["They must open at once from the escape side, in one action, without a key","A padlock is fine if the key hangs next to the door","A padlock is fine if the site manager has the key","A padlock is fine during working hours"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In a fire, people must be able to get out fast, even in smoke. A fire exit door must open straight away from the inside with one simple action, and without needing a key.","why_not":{"1":"A key should not be needed at all. Fire exits must open in one action without a key.","2":"People cannot wait for a key in a fire. Fire exits must open without a key.","3":"Working hours are when people need to get out. Fire exits must open without a key."},"source_title":"HSE HSG168: Fire safety in construction","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg168.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-24","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Part of the fire detection system in a room was switched off so that your work would not set it off by mistake. You stop for lunch. What should happen to it?","options":["It must be switched back on when work stops, including for breaks and at the end of the day","Leave it off until the whole job is finished next week","Leave it off over lunch, because nobody is working","Only the fire and rescue service can switch it back on"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A fire can start while nobody is watching, for example over a break. So any part of the alarm or detection system that was switched off for the work must be switched back on whenever the work stops, including for breaks.","why_not":{"1":"It must be switched back on whenever work stops, including at the end of each day.","2":"Breaks count. It must be switched back on when you stop for lunch.","3":"It is not left to the fire service. The site must switch it back on whenever work stops."},"source_title":"HSE HSG168: Fire safety in construction","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg168.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-25","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"The fire alarm goes off while you are halfway through a concrete pour. Your workmate says it is probably just a drill and you should finish first. What should you do?","options":["Leave straight away. When the alarm sounds, everyone must get out without delay","Finish the pour first, then leave","Wait to see if you can smell smoke before you leave","Stay where you are, because drills do not count"],"correct":0,"explanation":"When the fire alarm sounds, everyone must leave at once. HSE warns that people often hang back because they are in the middle of a job or think the alarm is a drill or false. That delay can cost lives.","why_not":{"1":"Finishing the job first causes delay. When the alarm sounds, everyone must leave at once.","2":"Do not wait for smoke. Everyone must leave without delay when the alarm sounds.","3":"Treat every alarm as real. Everyone must get out without delay."},"source_title":"HSE HSG168: Fire safety in construction","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg168.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-26","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Your supervisor is allowed to issue permits to work. He wants to write out and issue a permit for a job he will be doing himself. What does HSE say?","options":["A person should not issue a permit to themselves","That is fine, because it saves time","That is fine, as long as he signs it twice","That is fine for short jobs"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A permit is a check by one person on the work another person will do. HSE says nobody should issue a permit to themselves. For very high-risk jobs, a second person should also see the permit before it is issued.","why_not":{"1":"Saving time is no reason. HSE says nobody should issue a permit to themselves.","2":"Signing twice does not help. A person should not issue a permit to themselves.","3":"Short jobs are no different. Nobody should issue a permit to themselves."},"source_title":"HSE HSG250: Guidance on permit-to-work systems","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg250.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-27","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Work under a permit was stopped (suspended) yesterday while you waited for spare parts. The parts have now arrived. What must happen before you start again?","options":["The person who issues the permits must check it is safe, then renew the permit or issue a new one","Just carry on, because the permit was never cancelled","Start again, as long as it is still the same week","Start again if a workmate says the area looks the same as yesterday"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A suspended permit stays live until it is cancelled, and things may have changed since work stopped. Work should not restart until the person who issues permits has checked it is safe and renewed the permit, or issued a new one.","why_not":{"1":"A suspended permit stays live, and things may have changed. It must be checked and renewed first.","2":"The week does not matter. The permit issuer must check it is safe and renew the permit.","3":"A workmate's look is not enough. The permit issuer must check it is safe first."},"source_title":"HSE HSG250: Guidance on permit-to-work systems","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg250.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-28","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A job under a permit to work is not finished when your shift ends. The night shift will take over. What should the handover make sure of?","options":["That the night shift knows about the unfinished permit job, how far it has got and the state of the plant","Nothing. The night shift can read the permit if they happen to find it","That the permit is thrown away, so the night shift can start fresh without one","Only that the supervisor is told, not the workers taking over"],"correct":0,"explanation":"When a permit job carries on into the next shift, the people taking over must be told about it: what is still open, how far it has got and what state the plant is in. A failed handover was one of the causes of the Piper Alpha disaster.","why_not":{"1":"The night shift must be told about unfinished permit jobs, not left to find out.","2":"The permit must not be thrown away. The night shift must be told about the open job.","3":"The workers taking over must know about the job, its progress and the state of the plant."},"source_title":"HSE HSG250: Guidance on permit-to-work systems","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg250.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-30","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You think there may be a small gas leak at a joint on your gas cutting equipment. How should you check it?","options":["Use a leak-detecting spray or solution made for oxy/fuel gas equipment","Hold a lit match near the joint and see if it flares","Use the cutting torch flame to find the leak","Sniff along the hose, and carry on if you cannot smell anything"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Using a flame to look for a gas leak can set the gas alight. HSE says use a leak-detecting spray or solution made for oxy/fuel equipment, and never a naked flame. Any leaking part must be repaired or replaced straight away.","why_not":{"1":"Never look for gas leaks with a flame. It could set the gas alight.","2":"Never look for gas leaks with a flame. Use a leak-detecting spray instead.","3":"Sniffing is not a safe check. Use a leak-detecting spray made for oxy/fuel equipment."},"source_title":"HSE INDG297: Safety in gas welding, cutting and similar processes","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg297.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-31","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You are asked to cut an old oil drum in half with a gas cutting torch. It looks empty. What should you know?","options":["Even a tiny amount left inside can explode. Do not cut it unless you know it is safe","If it looks empty, it is safe to cut","It is safe as long as the lid is off","It is only dangerous if the drum is full"],"correct":0,"explanation":"'Empty' drums usually still have some liquid or vapour in the bottom or in the seams. HSE says as little as a teaspoon of flammable liquid can cause an explosion. Never use a cutting torch on a drum that has held, or may have held, something flammable unless you know it is safe.","why_not":{"1":"Empty drums usually still have some liquid or vapour inside. A teaspoon can be enough to explode.","2":"Taking the lid off does not make it safe. Leftover liquid inside can still explode.","3":"A drum that looks empty can still explode. Just a teaspoon of flammable liquid is enough."},"source_title":"HSE INDG297: Safety in gas welding, cutting and similar processes","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg297.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-32","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You need to cut a bracket off an old trailer wheel. The tyre is still on it but is flat. A workmate says it is fine to use the gas torch because the tyre has no air in it. What does HSE say?","options":["Avoid hot cutting on a wheel with a tyre fitted, even a flat one. Use a cold method such as a saw","It is fine, because the tyre is flat","It is fine, as long as you work quickly","It is fine, as long as you pour water on the tyre first"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Heat can make the tyre and any oil on the wheel give off flammable vapour. Trapped inside the tyre, it can explode violently and kill, even if the tyre is flat. If the tyre cannot come off, use a cold cutting method such as a saw.","why_not":{"1":"A flat tyre can still explode when heated. Use a cold method such as a saw.","2":"Speed does not help. Avoid flame cutting on a wheel with a tyre fitted.","3":"Wetting the tyre does not make it safe. Avoid flame cutting with a tyre fitted."},"source_title":"HSE INDG297: Safety in gas welding, cutting and similar processes","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg297.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-33","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"After a flashback on your gas cutting set, you notice that the acetylene cylinder has become hot. What must you do?","options":["Get everyone away from the area straight away and call the fire and rescue service","Carry the cylinder outside quickly so it can cool down","Lay the cylinder on its side to slow the gas","Carry on working, as long as the flame has gone out"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A hot acetylene cylinder can start to break down inside and may explode. If one becomes hot or starts to shake, HSE says you must get away at once and call the fire and rescue service.","why_not":{"1":"Do not go near it. A hot acetylene cylinder could explode, so get away and call the fire service.","2":"Do not handle it. Get everyone away at once and call the fire and rescue service.","3":"The cylinder could still explode. Get everyone away and call the fire and rescue service."},"source_title":"HSE INDG297: Safety in gas welding, cutting and similar processes","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg297.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-34","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"You are repairing pipes on your own in the basement of a large building. Other people are working on the floors above, but nobody is with you or supervising you. Are you a lone worker?","options":["Yes. Working apart from others on the same premises, without close supervision, counts as lone working","No. You are only a lone worker if nobody else is in the building","No. Lone working only means working at night","No. Lone working only applies to delivery drivers"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A lone worker is anyone who works by themselves without close or direct supervision. That includes people working away from others in the same building, such as maintenance and repair staff, not just people who are completely alone.","why_not":{"1":"Working apart from others in the same building, without close supervision, still counts as lone working.","2":"Lone working is not just at night. It includes working apart from others without close supervision.","3":"Lone workers are found in all kinds of jobs, including maintenance and repair."},"source_title":"HSE INDG73: Protecting lone workers","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg73.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-36","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"Your boss says: 'You're working alone, so just judge the risks yourself as you go. We don't need a proper risk assessment.' What does HSE say?","options":["Judging risks as you go does not replace a proper risk assessment done beforehand","That is fine. The lone worker's own judgement is all that is needed","Risk assessments are never needed for lone working","Only self-employed people need a risk assessment"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Some employers ask lone workers to make decisions on the spot about risks nobody could foresee. HSE says this is not a substitute for a full risk assessment. Workers asked to do it must be trained and supported.","why_not":{"1":"HSE says judging risks as you go is not a substitute for a proper risk assessment.","2":"A proper risk assessment is still needed for lone working. Judging as you go does not replace it.","3":"Employers must assess the risks for their lone workers. It is not only for self-employed people."},"source_title":"HSE INDG73: Protecting lone workers","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg73.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-38","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A workmate burns his hand badly on a hot pipe. What does HSE's basic first-aid advice say?","options":["Cool it with cold water until the pain eases. This may take 20 minutes or more, but it must not delay getting him to hospital","Cool it for a few seconds, then keep it dry","Do not cool it at all. Just wrap it up tightly","Finish 20 minutes of cooling before going to hospital, even if that delays the trip"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Cool a burn with cold water until the pain eases, which can take 20 minutes or more. If the casualty needs hospital, the cooling must not hold that up. If in doubt, get medical help, because burns can be serious.","why_not":{"1":"A few seconds is far too short. Cool the burn until the pain eases, maybe 20 minutes or more.","2":"A burn should be cooled with cold water until the pain eases.","3":"Cooling must not delay getting him to hospital."},"source_title":"HSE INDG347: Basic advice on first aid at work","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg347.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-39","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"After grinding, a small metal splinter is stuck in a workmate's eye. What should you do?","options":["Do not try to remove it. Get medical help","Pick it out carefully with tweezers","Rub the eye until it comes out","Lift it out with the corner of a clean cloth"],"correct":0,"explanation":"All eye injuries can be serious. Loose bits can be washed out with clean water or sterile eyewash, but you must never try to remove something that is stuck in the eye. Get medical help.","why_not":{"1":"Never try to remove something stuck in the eye. Get medical help.","2":"Do not try to remove something stuck in the eye, by rubbing or any other way.","3":"Do not try to remove anything stuck in the eye. Get medical help instead."},"source_title":"HSE INDG347: Basic advice on first aid at work","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg347.pdf"},{"id":"specialist-b3-40","topic":"Specialist","sign":null,"question":"A workmate with asthma is having an attack and needs the inhaler his doctor gave him. What is the first-aider's role?","options":["Help him use his own inhaler, and call the emergency services if needed","Take the inhaler away, because only doctors can give medicine","Give him tablets from the first-aid box instead","Do nothing at all until an ambulance arrives"],"correct":0,"explanation":"First aid at work does not include giving tablets or medicines to treat illness. But if someone carries their own medicine from their doctor, such as an asthma inhaler, the first-aider can help them take it and call the emergency services if needed.","why_not":{"1":"If he has his own prescribed inhaler, the first-aider can help him use it.","2":"First aid at work does not include giving tablets or medicines to treat illness.","3":"The first-aider can help him use his own inhaler and call the emergency services."},"source_title":"HSE: Frequently asked questions on first aid","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/firstaid/faqs.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b1-02","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"Your employer brings in a permit you must fill in before any hot work. You think the paperwork is a waste of time. What does the law say?","options":["You can skip it if you have years of experience","Only supervisors have to follow it","You must co-operate with your employer so they can meet their safety duties","You only have to follow it if it is written in your contract"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The law gives every employee a duty to work with their employer on safety. Your employer must meet safety rules, and they need you to follow the systems they set up, such as a hot work permit. Being experienced does not let you skip it.","why_not":{"0":"Experience does not let you skip it; every employee must co-operate with safety systems.","1":"The duty to co-operate is on every employee, not just supervisors.","3":"The duty comes from the law, so it applies whatever your contract says."},"source_title":"Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, section 7(b)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1974/37/section/7"},{"id":"working-environment-b1-04","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"An HSE inspector gives your company a prohibition notice about the way your gang is cutting kerbs. What does this mean?","options":["It is only advice, so the work can carry on as before","The work can carry on if everyone puts on extra PPE","Only the manager must stop; the workers can carry on","That work must not be done until the problems in the notice are put right"],"correct":3,"explanation":"An inspector uses a prohibition notice when work could cause serious injury. The notice says the work must not be done until the problems it lists are fixed. It can take effect straight away. It is not advice, and it covers everyone doing that work, not only the manager.","why_not":{"0":"A prohibition notice is an order, not advice; that work must stop until it is put right.","1":"Extra PPE does not lift the notice; only fixing the problems it lists does.","2":"The notice stops the work itself, so nobody can carry on doing it."},"source_title":"Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, section 22(3)(d)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1974/37/section/22"},{"id":"working-environment-b1-05","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"You were trained to use a cut-off saw in a set way. A workmate shows you a quicker way that is different from your training. What should you do?","options":["Keep using the saw the way you were trained and told","Use the quicker way, because it saves time","Use the quicker way only when the supervisor is not there"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law says you must use machines and equipment the way you were trained and the way your employer told you. A 'quicker way' from a workmate may skip a safety step. If you think there is a better way, ask your supervisor first.","why_not":{"1":"The law says use equipment as you were trained, even if another way is quicker.","2":"You must follow your training all the time, not only when the supervisor is watching."},"source_title":"The Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999, regulation 14(1)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1999/3242/regulation/14"},{"id":"working-environment-b1-06","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"Last month you had an induction on another site for the same company. Today you start on a new site. Do you need an induction here?","options":["No, one induction covers every site for that company","Yes, because the induction must be about this site and its own risks","Only if you are going to work at height"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Every site has its own hazards, layout and arrangements, such as where first aid is and how to report an accident. So every worker needs an induction for the site they are working on, even if they had one somewhere else.","why_not":{"0":"Each site has its own risks, so the induction must be about this site.","2":"Every worker needs a site induction, whatever job they will do there."},"source_title":"HSE: Construction – Site rules and induction","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/site-rules-induction.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b1-09","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A bucket of bolts falls off a scaffold and lands a metre away from you. Nobody is hurt. What is this, and what should you do?","options":["It is nothing, because nobody was hurt","It is a near miss: report it so the cause can be found and fixed","It is a near miss, but you only report it if it happens again","It is an accident, so you must phone HSE yourself"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A near miss is an event where nobody is hurt, but someone could have been. Next time the bucket might hit someone. Reporting it lets the site find out why it fell and stop it happening again. Nobody being hurt was only luck.","why_not":{"0":"Nobody being hurt was luck; it could hurt someone next time, so report it.","2":"Report a near miss the first time, so it is fixed before anyone gets hurt.","3":"Nobody was hurt, so it is a near miss, not an accident; report it on site so it is fixed."},"source_title":"HSE: Investigating accidents and incidents (HSG245)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/hsg245.pdf"},{"id":"working-environment-b1-10","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A worker slips on mud and sprains his ankle. He cannot do his normal work for 5 days in a row, then returns to his normal job. What must his employer do?","options":["Report it to HSE under RIDDOR","Nothing at all, because he was off for less than 7 days","Record it, for example in the accident book, but not report it to HSE","Report it to HSE only if he asks for compensation"],"correct":2,"explanation":"If a worker cannot do their normal work for more than 3 days in a row, the accident must be written down, for example in the accident book. It only has to be reported to HSE under RIDDOR if it lasts more than 7 days in a row. Five days is more than 3 but not more than 7.","why_not":{"0":"Five days is not more than 7, so it does not go to HSE.","1":"Five days is more than 3, so it must still be recorded.","3":"Compensation has nothing to do with it; time off work decides what is done."},"source_title":"HSE: RIDDOR – Types of reportable incidents","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/riddor/types-of-reportable-incidents.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b1-13","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A large section of a scaffold 9 metres high falls down. Luckily nobody is hurt. Must this be reported under RIDDOR?","options":["No, because nobody was hurt","Yes, it is a 'dangerous occurrence' that must be reported","No, it only needs to go in the accident book","Only if a member of the public saw it"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Some events must be reported even when nobody is hurt, because they could easily have killed someone. These are called dangerous occurrences. The collapse of a big part of any scaffold more than 5 metres high is one of them.","why_not":{"0":"This is a dangerous occurrence, which must be reported even when nobody is hurt.","2":"A big scaffold collapse over 5 metres must be reported to the authorities, not just written down.","3":"It must be reported whoever saw it, because it could easily have killed someone."},"source_title":"HSE: RIDDOR – Dangerous occurrences","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/riddor/dangerous-occurences.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b1-15","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"Your gang wants to keep bags of plaster and a small mixer in the canteen cabin overnight. Is that OK?","options":["Yes, if they are stacked neatly in a corner","Yes, if the cabin is locked","No, rest and eating areas must not be used to store plant, equipment or materials"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The canteen is where people rest and eat. It must be kept clean and tidy, and HSE says it must not be used as a store for plant, equipment or materials. Plaster dust near food is also a health risk. Find a proper store instead.","why_not":{"0":"Rest areas must not be used to store plant, equipment or materials, however neat.","1":"Locking the cabin does not help; rest areas must not be used as stores."},"source_title":"HSE: Construction welfare – Changing, eating and rest areas","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/welfare/changing-rooms-and-lockers.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b1-20","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A site uses an air horn to warn people of a fire. What must be true about the warning?","options":["It only needs to be heard in the site office","It can be the same sound as the tea break signal","Everyone must be able to hear it over other noise and know what it means"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A fire warning is only useful if everyone gets it. HSE says it must be a sound that stands out, can be heard over the site noise, and that everyone knows means fire. If it sounds like the tea break signal, people may not leave.","why_not":{"0":"Everyone on site must hear the warning, not just people in the office.","1":"The warning must be distinctive, so people know it means fire and leave."},"source_title":"HSE: Construction – General fire safety","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/generalfire.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b1-22","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"In England, a site fuel tank holds 1,000 litres. Its bund (the outer wall or tray that catches leaks) must be able to hold at least how much?","options":["250 litres","500 litres","1,000 litres","1,100 litres"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The law in England says the bund around an oil tank must hold at least 110% of what the tank holds. For a 1,000 litre tank that is 1,100 litres. The extra 10% is a safety margin, so the bund can catch everything if the tank leaks.","why_not":{"0":"Too small; the bund must hold at least 110% of the tank, which is 1,100 litres.","1":"Too small; the bund must hold at least 110% of the tank, which is 1,100 litres.","2":"Not quite enough; the law asks for 110%, so 10% more than the tank holds."},"source_title":"The Control of Pollution (Oil Storage) (England) Regulations 2001, regulation 3(2)(a)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2001/2954/regulation/3"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-01","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"An HSE inspector gives your company an improvement notice. What does this mean?","options":["The work must stop at once and must not start again","The company has done well and is being praised for its safety","The inspector thinks a safety law is being broken, and the company must put it right within the time given on the notice","It is only advice, so the company can choose to ignore it"],"correct":2,"explanation":"An improvement notice says the inspector thinks a safety law is being broken. It says which law, explains why, and gives the company a time limit to put things right. A notice that stops the work straight away is a different kind. An improvement notice is not praise, and it is not optional.","why_not":{"0":"That is a different notice; an improvement notice gives a time limit to put things right.","1":"It is not praise; it means the inspector thinks a safety law is being broken.","3":"It is not advice; the company must put things right within the time given."},"source_title":"Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, section 21","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1974/37/section/21"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-02","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"An HSE inspector turns up at the site gate in the middle of the working day. Nobody knew they were coming. Can the inspector come onto the site?","options":["Yes. Inspectors can come in at any reasonable time, and at any time if they think there may be danger","No. Inspectors must book a visit at least a week before","Only if the client signs a form to let them in","Only if a police officer comes with them"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law lets an inspector enter a workplace at any reasonable time to do their job. If they think something is or may be dangerous, they can come in at any time. They do not need to book, get the client's permission or bring the police.","why_not":{"1":"The law lets an inspector come in at any reasonable time, without booking.","2":"The inspector's power to enter comes from the law, not from the client's permission.","3":"The law lets an inspector come in at any reasonable time without the police."},"source_title":"Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, section 20(2)(a)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1974/37/section/20"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-03","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"After an accident, an HSE inspector tells you to leave a broken trestle exactly where it fell. Later someone says the area should be tidied up. What should you do?","options":["Tidy it away so the site looks safe","Throw the trestle in the skip so nobody else uses it","Move it to the store, but take a photo first","Leave it where it is. An inspector can order that things are left untouched while they look into what happened"],"correct":3,"explanation":"An inspector can tell people to leave a place, or anything in it, untouched for as long as they need to look into what happened. Moving or throwing away the trestle could destroy evidence. If anyone wants to move it, they should speak to the inspector first.","why_not":{"0":"An inspector can order that things are left untouched, so tidying it could destroy evidence.","1":"Throwing it away destroys evidence the inspector has ordered to be left alone.","2":"A photo is not enough; the inspector ordered that it be left exactly where it is."},"source_title":"Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, section 20(2)(e)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1974/37/section/20"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-04","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"What does the law say your employer must do with the HSE 'Health and safety law' poster?","options":["Keep it locked in the office in case an inspector asks to see it","Put it up where workers can easily see and read it, or give each worker the matching leaflet","Show it only to supervisors and managers","Nothing. It is just a suggestion"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Your employer must either put up the approved poster, in good condition, where you can easily get to it and read it at work, or give you the approved leaflet. Locking it away or showing it only to managers does not meet the law.","why_not":{"0":"The poster must be on show where workers can easily see and read it.","2":"Every worker must be able to see the poster, or be given the leaflet.","3":"It is the law, not a suggestion: show the poster or give each worker the leaflet."},"source_title":"The Health and Safety Information for Employees Regulations 1989, regulation 4(1)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1989/682/regulation/4"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-06","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"You hurt your hand at work and you cannot write. How can the accident still go in the accident book?","options":["It cannot. You must wait until you can write it yourself","Only an HSE inspector is allowed to write in the accident book","Someone else can write the details in for you, as soon as possible","It only goes in the book if you go to hospital"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The details of an accident can be written in the accident book by the injured worker or by someone acting for them. It should be done as soon as it reasonably can be after the accident. You do not have to wait until you can write, and it is not only for hospital cases.","why_not":{"0":"You do not have to wait; someone else can write it in for you.","1":"The injured worker, or someone acting for them, writes the entry, not an HSE inspector.","3":"Accidents go in the book whether or not you go to hospital."},"source_title":"The Social Security (Claims and Payments) Regulations 1979, regulation 24(3)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1979/628/regulation/24"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-07","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"Your supervisor looks into a near miss you were part of. What is the main aim of looking into it?","options":["To find someone to blame and punish","To work out who should pay for any damage","To fill in a form that is then filed away","To learn why it happened and stop it happening again"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE says the aim of looking into an accident or near miss is to learn the lessons and take action so it does not happen again. Looking for someone to blame makes people less willing to say what really happened.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says the aim is to learn lessons, and blame stops people saying what happened.","1":"HSE says the aim is to learn lessons and stop it happening again, not to sort out payment.","2":"Filing a form changes nothing; the aim is to act so it does not happen again."},"source_title":"HSE: Investigating accidents and incidents (HSG245)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/hsg245.pdf"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-08","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A worker hurts his back at work and cannot do his normal job for 10 days in a row. His employer must report it under RIDDOR. By when?","options":["Within 15 days of the accident","Within 24 hours","Within 6 months","Only once he is back at work"],"correct":0,"explanation":"When a worker cannot do their normal work for more than 7 days in a row because of a work accident, the report must reach the enforcing authority within 15 days of the accident. It is done using the online form. Waiting until the worker returns could be too late.","why_not":{"1":"The deadline for an over-7-day injury is 15 days, not 24 hours.","2":"Six months is far too late; the report must arrive within 15 days.","3":"The deadline is 15 days from the accident, whether or not he is back at work."},"source_title":"HSE: RIDDOR - When do I need to report an incident?","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/riddor/when-do-i-report.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-09","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A falling brick hits a worker on the head. He is knocked out for a few seconds, then gets up and says he feels fine. Under RIDDOR, is this a specified injury?","options":["No, because he was only out for a few seconds","Yes. Being knocked out by a head injury must be reported, however long it lasts","No, because he says he feels fine","Only if he is kept in hospital overnight"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Under RIDDOR, being knocked out by a head injury at work is a specified injury. It must be reported however short a time the person was out. Feeling fine afterwards does not change this, and a hospital stay is not needed.","why_not":{"0":"Being knocked out by a head injury must be reported, however short a time.","2":"Feeling fine afterwards does not change it; being knocked out must be reported.","3":"No hospital stay is needed; being knocked out by a head injury is enough."},"source_title":"HSE: RIDDOR - Specified, reportable injuries to workers","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/riddor/specified-injuries.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-11","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A tipper lorry raises its body and touches a bare overhead power line carrying 11,000 volts. Nobody is hurt. Must this be reported under RIDDOR?","options":["No, because nobody was hurt","Only if the power goes off in nearby houses","Only if the lorry is damaged","Yes. Plant touching a bare overhead line of more than 200 volts is a dangerous occurrence and must be reported"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Plant or equipment touching a bare overhead power line of more than 200 volts by accident is a dangerous occurrence under RIDDOR. It must be reported even when nobody is hurt and nothing is damaged.","why_not":{"0":"This is a dangerous occurrence, which must be reported even when nobody is hurt.","1":"Touching a bare line over 200 volts must be reported, whether or not the power goes off.","2":"Touching a bare line over 200 volts must be reported, even if nothing is damaged."},"source_title":"HSE: RIDDOR - Dangerous occurrences (overhead electric lines, paragraph 3)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/riddor/dangerous-occurences.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-12","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"When must toilets, washing and rest facilities be ready on a construction project?","options":["From the start of the project until it finishes","Once the building has a roof on it","After the first month, when more workers arrive","Only when an inspector asks for them"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says contractors must provide welfare for their workers while they are on site, and the principal contractor must make sure this happens from the start of the project until it finishes. Workers need toilets and washing from day one.","why_not":{"1":"Workers need toilets and washing from day one, not once the roof is on.","2":"Welfare must be in place from the very start of the project, not after a month.","3":"Welfare is a duty from start to finish, whether or not an inspector asks."},"source_title":"HSE: Construction welfare - Overview","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/welfare/index.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-13","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A small site has one toilet that men and women both use. When is this allowed?","options":["Never. Men and women must always have separate toilet blocks","Only if fewer than five women work on the site","When the toilet is in its own room with a door that can be locked from the inside","Only if men and women use it at different times of day"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The law says men and women must have separate toilet rooms, unless each toilet is in its own room with a door that locks from the inside. Then one toilet can be shared. The number of women and the time of day do not matter.","why_not":{"0":"One toilet can be shared if it is in its own room that locks from inside.","1":"The number of women does not matter; a lockable separate room is what counts.","3":"The time of day does not matter; a lockable separate room is what counts."},"source_title":"The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, Schedule 2, paragraph 1(4)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/schedule/2"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-14","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"You have been working with wet cement all morning. Where should you wash your hands before lunch?","options":["In the canteen sink, where people make their tea","At the proper washing facilities, away from where people eat","In the bucket of water you used for mixing","Nowhere. Wiping them on a rag is enough"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says hand washing should not usually happen where people eat and drink. This matters most when you work with things like cement or lead, so they do not end up in food. Use the washing facilities, which should be kept apart from eating areas.","why_not":{"0":"Washing cement off where people make tea could get it into their food and drink.","2":"Dirty mixing water does not get cement off your hands; use proper washing facilities.","3":"A rag does not clean cement off; you need proper washing facilities."},"source_title":"HSE: Construction welfare - Toilets and washing","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/welfare/toilets-and-washing.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-15","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A short road repair job has no welfare unit. The firm agrees with a nearby café that the workers can use its toilets. What else must be true?","options":["The café toilets must be open the whole time the workers are on site","HSE must sign the agreement with the café","Nothing else. It is fine even if the café shuts at 2pm and work goes on until 5pm","Each worker must buy something from the café first"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE says a firm can use toilets in a café, public toilets or another building, but the owner must agree and the toilets must be open all the time the workers are on site. The agreement does not have to be written down, and HSE does not sign it.","why_not":{"1":"HSE does not sign it; the agreement is with the owner and need not be written.","2":"The toilets must be open all the time workers are on site, not only until 2pm.","3":"HSE says the owner must agree to their use, not that workers must buy anything."},"source_title":"HSE: Construction welfare - Overview","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/welfare/index.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-18","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"You need somewhere to stack plasterboard. The only empty space is a corridor marked as a fire escape route. What should you do?","options":["Stack it there, but leave a narrow gap to squeeze past","Stack it there, but only for today","Stack it there and take down the escape route sign","Do not stack it there. Escape routes must be kept clear, so ask your supervisor where it should go"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The law says emergency routes and exits, and the ways that lead to them, must be kept clear so they can be used at any time. Even a short time or a narrow gap could trap people in a fire. Find another place to store the boards.","why_not":{"0":"Escape routes must be kept clear, and a narrow gap could trap people in a fire.","1":"Escape routes must be clear so they can be used at any time, even today.","2":"Taking the sign down makes it worse; the route must stay clear and marked."},"source_title":"The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 31(4)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/31"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-19","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"You are asked to use a gas torch to strip old paint. You have never used one, and nobody has told you about the fire risks. What should you do?","options":["Have a go and learn as you work","Ask a workmate to keep an eye on you, then start","Do not start until you have been properly instructed in doing the job safely","Do it quickly at the end of the day, when fewer people are around"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The law says that when a job brings a special risk of fire, you must not do it unless you have been properly instructed. Tell your supervisor you need instruction first. Working late when nobody is around makes a fire more likely to go unnoticed.","why_not":{"0":"The law says you must not do work with a special fire risk until you are instructed.","1":"A workmate watching is not instruction; you must be properly instructed first.","3":"Working when few people are around means a fire may not be noticed."},"source_title":"The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 32(6)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/32"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-20","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"You are fitting foam insulation boards on the third floor. The whole delivery has arrived. What does HSE advise to cut the fire risk?","options":["Carry the whole delivery up now to save trips","Bring up only what you need for now, and keep the rest stored safely until it is needed","Stack all the boards on the stairs so they are handy","Keep the spare boards next to where hot work is being done"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HSE says keeping the amount of burnable material in the work area low, until it is needed, cuts the risk of fire. Foam boards burn. Stacking them on stairs could also block a way out.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says keep burnable material in the work area low until it is needed.","2":"Boards on the stairs add fire risk and could block a way out.","3":"Foam boards burn, so keeping them near hot work makes a fire more likely."},"source_title":"HSE: Process fire risks (construction)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/processfire.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b2-23","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"Your site is next to houses. You are told the breakers may only be used between 8am and 6pm. Who has the power to set rules like this for a building site?","options":["Nobody. There are no rules about building site noise","The company that hired out the breakers","The local council, which can serve a notice setting working hours, noise levels and which machines may be used","Any neighbour who complains"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The law lets the local council serve a notice on a building site about noise. It can say which machines may or may not be used, the hours work may be done, and how much noise is allowed. Breaking the notice without a good reason is an offence.","why_not":{"0":"There are rules: the local council can serve a notice about building site noise.","1":"The hire company has no such power; the local council does.","3":"A neighbour can complain, but only the local council can serve the notice."},"source_title":"Control of Pollution Act 1974, section 60(2) and (3)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1974/40/section/60"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-01","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"Your employer has 12 workers and has done a risk assessment for the job you are on. What does the law say it must do with the main findings?","options":["Write them down only if an inspector asks","Write them down","Throw the assessment away once the job starts","Nothing, because only firms with 50 or more workers have to write them down"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An employer with five or more employees must write down the main (significant) findings of its risk assessment. It must also write down any group of workers it found to be especially at risk. Twelve workers is more than five, so the findings must be written down. This is not only for big firms, and it does not wait for an inspector to ask.","why_not":{"0":"With five or more workers the findings must be written down anyway, not only when asked.","2":"The main findings must be recorded, not thrown away.","3":"The rule starts at five workers, not 50, so 12 workers is enough."},"source_title":"The Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999, regulation 3(6)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1999/3242/regulation/3"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-03","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"You are working in a trench when its side starts to crack and crumble. You climb out at once and will not go back in until it is made safe. Your boss says he will dock your pay for stopping work. What does the law say?","options":["You are protected: your employer must not treat you badly for leaving a danger you reasonably believed was serious and about to happen","You are only protected if someone actually got hurt","You must go back in as soon as your boss tells you to","Your boss can dock your pay because you stopped without asking"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The law protects a worker who leaves, or refuses to go back to, a place of work because of danger they reasonably believe is serious and about to happen, and which they could not reasonably stop. Your employer must not treat you badly for this, for example by cutting your pay. Nobody has to be hurt first.","why_not":{"1":"Nobody has to be hurt; the law protects you for leaving a serious danger.","2":"You can refuse to go back while the serious danger is still there.","3":"Your employer must not treat you badly, such as cutting pay, for leaving a serious danger."},"source_title":"Employment Rights Act 1996, section 44(1A)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1996/18/section/44"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-04","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A 16-year-old is about to start with your firm as a trainee. What must the employer do before taking them on?","options":["Wait until they have had a near miss, then do an assessment","Ask their parents to accept the risks in writing","Nothing extra, because young workers face the same risks as everyone else","Make or review a risk assessment that looks at the risks to young people, such as their lack of experience"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The law says an employer must not employ a young person (someone under 18) until it has made or reviewed a risk assessment covering the risks to young people. It must take special account of their inexperience, their lack of awareness of risks and their immaturity. Parents cannot sign the risks away.","why_not":{"0":"The assessment must be done before the young person starts, not after a near miss.","1":"Parents cannot sign the risks away; the employer must assess the risks to young people.","2":"Young workers face extra risks, like lack of experience, which the employer must assess."},"source_title":"The Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999, regulation 3(4) and (5)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1999/3242/regulation/3"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-05","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"The principal contractor plans to change the walkways across the site. The change will affect everyone working there. What does the law say about the workers?","options":["Only the client needs to be asked","Workers have no say in health and safety on site","The principal contractor must consult the workers, or their representatives, in good time","Workers only need to be told once the change has been made"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Under CDM 2015, the principal contractor must consult workers, or their representatives, in good time about project matters that may affect their health, safety or welfare (unless their own employer has already done so). Telling people after the change is made is not consulting them.","why_not":{"0":"The principal contractor must consult the workers, not just the client.","1":"The law says workers or their representatives must be consulted on site safety.","3":"Telling people after the change is not consulting them in good time."},"source_title":"The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 14(b)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/14"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-06","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"On a project with a principal contractor and several other contractors, who does the law say must make sure a suitable site induction is provided?","options":["The local council","Each worker must arrange their own","The firm that delivers the materials","The principal contractor"],"correct":3,"explanation":"CDM 2015 says the principal contractor must make sure a suitable site induction is provided. The principal contractor runs the construction phase when more than one contractor is working on the project. Workers do not have to arrange their own induction.","why_not":{"0":"The council does not run inductions; the principal contractor must make sure one is given.","1":"Workers do not arrange their own; the principal contractor must make sure one is given.","2":"A delivery firm does not run the site; the principal contractor does."},"source_title":"The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 13(4)(a)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/13"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-08","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"Your firm is refitting part of a supermarket that stays open while you work. The store has its own safety rules as well as your site rules. What does HSE advise?","options":["Follow whichever rules are easier at the time","Make it clear where the site rules apply and where the store's rules apply, so everyone follows the right ones","Let each worker decide which rules to follow","Ignore the store's rules, because only site rules count"],"correct":1,"explanation":"When the client's business carries on during the work, HSE says to make it clear where your site rules apply and where the client's own rules apply. Everyone must know and follow the rules for the area they are in.","why_not":{"0":"HSE says everyone must know and follow the rules for the area they are in.","2":"Workers do not choose; HSE says make it clear which rules apply where.","3":"The store's rules apply in its areas, so both sets of rules count."},"source_title":"HSE: Site rules and induction (construction)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/site-rules-induction.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-09","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"You write up an accident in the site accident book. What does the law say your employer must then do?","options":["Take reasonable steps to look into how the accident happened","Remove the page once you are back at work","Wait to see whether you make a claim before doing anything","Nothing, because the book is only a record"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An employer must take reasonable steps to investigate every accident that is reported to them in this way. If what they find is different from what was written down, they must record what they found. The accident book is not just a place to file things away.","why_not":{"1":"Removing the page does nothing to look into the accident, which the employer must do.","2":"The employer must look into the accident whether or not you make a claim.","3":"The law says the employer must take reasonable steps to look into it."},"source_title":"The Social Security (Claims and Payments) Regulations 1979, regulation 25(1)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1979/628/regulation/25"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-10","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"Your employer has 40 workers who normally work at the same premises, and it keeps an accident book there. How long must each entry be kept?","options":["6 months","Until the end of the job","At least 3 years from the date it was written","Until the injured person is back at work"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Employers with 10 or more people normally working at the same premises (and some others, such as factories) must keep an accident book, or an electronic version, where it is easy to get to. Each entry must be kept for at least 3 years from the date it was made.","why_not":{"0":"Too short; each entry must be kept for at least 3 years.","1":"The end of the job is too soon; entries must be kept at least 3 years.","3":"Returning to work does not end it; entries must be kept at least 3 years."},"source_title":"The Social Security (Claims and Payments) Regulations 1979, regulation 25(3)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1979/628/regulation/25"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-11","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"On your site, labourers are often sent to drive a dumper even though none of them has been trained to use one. Nobody has been hurt and nothing has gone wrong yet. What does HSE's guide on investigating accidents call a situation like this?","options":["An undesired circumstance: a set of conditions that could cause injury or ill health","A near miss","Not a safety matter, because nothing has happened","An accident"],"correct":0,"explanation":"HSE's guide calls this an 'undesired circumstance': a set of conditions that could cause injury or ill health, such as untrained people doing a risky job. An accident is when someone is hurt, and a near miss is an event that could have hurt someone. Here no event has happened yet, but the danger is real and should be dealt with.","why_not":{"1":"A near miss is an event that nearly hurt someone; here no event has happened yet.","2":"Untrained people driving dumpers is a real danger, even before anything goes wrong.","3":"An accident is when someone is hurt; nobody has been hurt here."},"source_title":"HSE: Investigating accidents and incidents (HSG245), key words and phrases","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/hsg245.pdf"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-13","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A worker breaks his leg in a fall at work. This is a specified injury under RIDDOR. How quickly must the responsible person tell the enforcing authority?","options":["Only once he has been off work for more than 7 days","Without delay, and the report must arrive within 10 days","Within 1 month","Within 15 days"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Deaths, specified injuries to workers, hospital treatment of people who are not workers, and dangerous occurrences must be reported without delay, most easily online. The report must arrive within 10 days. For deaths and specified injuries there is also a phone line. The 15-day limit is for injuries that keep a worker off for more than 7 days.","why_not":{"0":"A broken leg is a specified injury, so it must be reported without waiting for time off.","2":"Too slow; a specified injury report must arrive within 10 days.","3":"Fifteen days is for over-7-day injuries; specified injuries must arrive within 10 days."},"source_title":"HSE: RIDDOR - When do I need to report an incident?","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/riddor/when-do-i-report.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-14","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A worker had a specified injury in an accident at work, and it was reported. Eight months later he dies, and the injury was a cause of his death. What must his employer do?","options":["Nothing more, because the injury was already reported","Only tell his family","Nothing, because deaths only count within 30 days of the accident","Tell the enforcing authority about the death without delay"],"correct":3,"explanation":"If a reportable injury to an employee is a cause of their death within one year of the accident, the employer must tell the enforcing authority about the death without delay. This applies even if the injury was reported before.","why_not":{"0":"A death within a year caused by the injury must be reported, even if the injury was.","1":"Telling the family is not enough; the enforcing authority must be told without delay.","2":"The time limit is one year from the accident, not 30 days."},"source_title":"The Reporting of Injuries, Diseases and Dangerous Occurrences Regulations 2013, regulation 6(3)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/1471/regulation/6"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-15","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A driver who is angry about a road closure punches a traffic marshal working on the job and breaks his jaw. Can this count as an accident under RIDDOR?","options":["Only if the marshal hit back","No, because the driver was not a worker","No, because injuries from violence never count","Yes. Violence to people at work that they did not agree to counts as an accident under RIDDOR"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Under RIDDOR an accident is a separate, unplanned event that causes physical injury. HSE says this includes violence to people at work that they did not agree to. So an attack on a worker can be a reportable accident, even when the attacker is a member of the public.","why_not":{"0":"Hitting back is not needed; violence to a worker that he did not agree to counts.","1":"It does not matter who the attacker is; violence to a worker counts.","2":"HSE says violence to people at work that they did not agree to does count."},"source_title":"HSE: RIDDOR explained - what a reportable accident is","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/riddor/key-definitions.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-16","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A plasterer's back has slowly got worse over many months of lifting boards. There was no single event that hurt it. Is this an 'accident' under RIDDOR?","options":["Yes, as soon as he feels a sharp pain","No. Harm that builds up slowly, such as from repeated lifting, is not classed as an accident under RIDDOR","Yes, because work played a part","Yes, any pain felt at work counts as an accident"],"correct":1,"explanation":"RIDDOR needs a separate event you can point to, such as something falling on someone. Harm that builds up slowly from repeated lifting is not classed as an 'accident' under RIDDOR, and just feeling a sharp pain is not an accident either. The plasterer should still tell his supervisor so the work can be made safer.","why_not":{"0":"Feeling a sharp pain is an injury, not an accident; there must be a separate event.","2":"Work playing a part is not enough; harm that builds up slowly is not an accident.","3":"Pain alone is not an accident; there must be a separate event that caused it."},"source_title":"HSE: RIDDOR explained - what a reportable accident is","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/riddor/key-definitions.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-17","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A worker's long hair gets caught in a spinning drill and some skin is torn from his head. He needs hospital treatment. Under RIDDOR, is this a specified injury?","options":["Only if he is off work for more than 7 days","No, because head wounds never count","Yes. Scalping of any degree that needs hospital treatment is a specified injury","Only if he was knocked out"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Scalping means skin being torn or peeled from the head in an accident, for example when hair gets caught in machinery. Any amount of scalping that needs hospital treatment is a specified injury and must be reported. It does not depend on time off work.","why_not":{"0":"Scalping needing hospital treatment must be reported, whatever the time off work.","1":"Scalping is a head wound that is on the specified injury list.","3":"Scalping needing hospital treatment counts on its own, without being knocked out."},"source_title":"HSE: Specified, reportable injuries to workers (RIDDOR)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/riddor/specified-injuries.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-18","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A worker cleaning inside a hot, enclosed tank collapses with heat illness. Under RIDDOR, is this a specified injury?","options":["Yes. An injury from working in an enclosed space that leads to heat illness is a specified injury","No, getting too hot is not an injury","Only if he stays in hospital for a week","Only if he dies"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An injury from working in an enclosed space is a specified injury if it leads to hypothermia or heat illness, needs resuscitation, or means the person is kept in hospital for more than 24 hours. Collapsing with heat illness inside a tank meets the first of these.","why_not":{"1":"Heat illness from working in an enclosed space is on the specified injury list.","2":"Heat illness alone is enough; a long hospital stay is not needed.","3":"Heat illness alone is enough; he does not have to die for it to count."},"source_title":"HSE: Specified, reportable injuries to workers (RIDDOR)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/riddor/specified-injuries.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-19","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"During demolition, a crawler crane being used to pull down a wall tips over. Nobody is hurt. Must this be reported under RIDDOR?","options":["Yes. A crane tipping over must be reported whatever work it was doing, including demolition","No, because it was not lifting a load at the time","No, because nobody was hurt","Only if the crane was hired in"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The collapse or overturning of lifting equipment is a dangerous occurrence under RIDDOR, even when nobody is hurt. HSE says crane incidents must be reported whatever work the crane was doing, not only lifting, and gives demolition as an example.","why_not":{"1":"Crane incidents must be reported whatever work it was doing, not only lifting.","2":"A crane tipping over is a dangerous occurrence, reported even when nobody is hurt.","3":"Who owns the crane does not matter; it must be reported."},"source_title":"HSE: RIDDOR dangerous occurrences - lifting equipment (Schedule 2, paragraph 1)","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/riddor/dangerous-occurences.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-21","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A site uses portable chemical toilets. 14 people work there, 40 hours a week. Using the British Standard that HSE quotes, at least how many chemical toilets should there be?","options":["7","4","2","1"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE points to a British Standard that recommends one chemical toilet for every seven people working a 40-hour week. Fourteen people means at least two. You may need more, or more frequent emptying, depending on how much they are used.","why_not":{"0":"Seven is the number of people per toilet, not the number of toilets.","1":"Four is more than the standard asks for; one per seven people means two.","3":"One toilet is for seven people; 14 people need at least two."},"source_title":"HSE: Construction welfare - toilets and washing","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/welfare/toilets-and-washing.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-25","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"On a site in England, a workmate lights a cigarette inside the canteen cabin at lunchtime. The cabin has a roof and walls, and your whole gang uses it. Is that allowed?","options":["Yes, because it is a break, not work time","Yes, if nobody complains","Yes, if he only has one","No. An enclosed place used for work by more than one person is smoke-free by law, all the time"],"correct":3,"explanation":"In England, enclosed or mostly enclosed places used for work by more than one person are smoke-free all the time, even if people use them at different times. The site cabin is enclosed and the whole gang uses it, so smoking inside is against the law.","why_not":{"0":"The cabin is smoke-free all the time, including breaks.","1":"The law makes it smoke-free whether or not anyone complains.","2":"Even one cigarette is against the law in an enclosed shared workplace."},"source_title":"Health Act 2006, section 2(2) and (4) (England)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/28/section/2"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-26","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"In England, your firm's van is used for work by you and two workmates. One of them wants to smoke in it when he drives it alone. What does the law say?","options":["It is fine on short trips","The van is smoke-free, because it is an enclosed vehicle used for work by more than one person, even at different times","It is fine when he is the only one in the van","Smoke-free rules only cover buses and taxis"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An enclosed vehicle used for paid or voluntary work by more than one person is smoke-free by law. This applies even if those people use the van at different times. So he must not smoke in it, even when he is alone.","why_not":{"0":"The van is smoke-free on every trip, short or long.","2":"It is smoke-free even when he is alone, because more than one person uses it for work.","3":"The rules also cover work vehicles used by more than one person, like this van."},"source_title":"The Smoke-free (Exemptions and Vehicles) Regulations 2007, regulation 11(1) (England)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2007/765/regulation/11"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-27","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A pile of cardboard and timber offcuts catches fire. Which type of extinguisher does HSE match to fires in wood, paper and cloth?","options":["None; leave it to burn out","Carbon dioxide (CO2)","Water","Any type, because they all work the same on every fire"],"correct":2,"explanation":"HSE matches water extinguishers to fires in wood, paper and cloth. It lists carbon dioxide (CO2) for electrical fires, and dry powder or foam for flammable liquids. Extinguishers are not all the same, so check the label before using one.","why_not":{"0":"A fire left to burn can spread; HSE matches water extinguishers to wood, paper and cloth.","1":"HSE lists CO2 for electrical fires, not for wood, paper and cloth.","3":"Extinguishers are not all the same; HSE matches each type to a kind of fire."},"source_title":"HSE: General fire safety (construction) - means of fighting fire","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/safetytopics/generalfire.htm"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-31","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"Where does HSE's fire safety guide say smoking should be allowed on a construction site?","options":["Next to the skip, so ash can go straight in","Inside the building, if the windows are open","Anywhere outdoors","Only in set smoking areas away from buildings and things that can burn"],"correct":3,"explanation":"HSE's guide says there should be no smoking on site except in set smoking areas away from buildings and materials that can burn. A skip full of rubbish is exactly the kind of thing a cigarette end can set alight.","why_not":{"0":"A skip is full of things that burn, so smoking must be away from it.","1":"Smoking must be away from buildings, open windows or not.","2":"Not anywhere; only in set areas away from buildings and things that burn."},"source_title":"HSE: Fire safety in construction (HSG168), fire risk checklist","source_url":"https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/priced/hsg168.pdf"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-35","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A registered carrier takes away a skip of mixed (non-hazardous) building waste. The site and the carrier both sign the written description of the waste, often called a waste transfer note. How long must each of them keep it?","options":["Until the skip has been emptied","At least 2 years","1 week","It does not need to be kept"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Both the person handing over the waste and the person taking it must keep the written description, or a copy, for at least 2 years. They must show it to an officer within 7 days if asked.","why_not":{"0":"Too short; both sides must keep it for at least 2 years.","2":"Too short; both sides must keep it for at least 2 years.","3":"It must be kept for at least 2 years and shown to an officer if asked."},"source_title":"The Waste (England and Wales) Regulations 2011, regulation 35(6)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2011/988/regulation/35"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-36","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"Your boss tells you to drive the firm's van to a quiet lane and tip out a load of old plasterboard there. What does the law say?","options":["It is fine because your boss told you to","It is fine if the lane is out in the countryside","It is fine if it is only one van load","You must not. Dumping waste on land without a permit that allows it is against the law"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The law says nobody may dump waste on land, or knowingly cause or allow it to be dumped, unless an environmental permit allows it. Tipping waste in a lane is fly-tipping. Being told to by your boss does not make it legal, so refuse and tell them why.","why_not":{"0":"Your boss telling you does not make dumping waste legal.","1":"Dumping waste on any land without a permit is against the law, countryside or not.","2":"Even one load dumped without a permit is against the law."},"source_title":"Environmental Protection Act 1990, section 33(1)(a) (England and Wales)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1990/43/section/33"},{"id":"working-environment-b3-37","topic":"Working environment","sign":null,"question":"A workmate suggests burning the plastic coating off some scrap electric cable so the copper can be sold. What does the law say?","options":["It is fine if it is done after work","It is fine on a small fire","Burning the coating off cable to get the metal out is an offence","It is fine if the money goes to charity"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The Clean Air Act makes it an offence to burn the insulation off cable to recover the metal (unless it is part of a specially permitted process). Burning plastic gives off harmful smoke. Put scrap cable in the right waste or recycling route instead.","why_not":{"0":"Burning cable coating to get the metal is an offence at any time.","1":"The size of the fire does not matter; burning cable coating for metal is an offence.","3":"Where the money goes does not matter; it is still an offence."},"source_title":"Clean Air Act 1993, section 33(1)","source_url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1993/11/section/33"}];
